tions . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n8\n1.7 Significance and Contributions of the Research . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n9\n1.7.1 Technical Innovations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n9\n1.7.2 Validated Educational Impact . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 10\n1.7.3 Contribution to AI-Guided Learning Paradigm . . . . . . . . . 10\n1.8 Dissertation Structure . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 10\n1.9 Concluding Remarks . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 11\n\n2\n\nRelated Work\n2.1 Introduction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n2.2 Foundations of Interactive Learning Systems . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n2.2.1 Historical Evolution: From PLATO to AI-Guided Learning . .\n2.2.2 Theoretical Frameworks for Technology-Enhanced Learning . .\n2.2.3 The Evolution from Direct Manipulation to Intelligent Interfaces\n2.3 The CONSUME Component: Eﬀicient Access to Audio-Visual Content\n2.3.1 Video Navigation and Temporal Media Manipulation . . . . . .\n2.3.2 Variable-Speed Playback and Cognitive Processing . . . . . . .\niv\n\n12\n12\n12\n12\n13\n14\n15\n15\n16\n\n\f2.4\n\n2.5\n\n2.6\n\n2.7\n\n2.8\n\n2.9\n\n2.3.3 Self-Supervised Speech Representation Learning . . . . . . . .\n2.3.4 Content-Aware Interfaces and Adaptive Systems . . . . . . . .\n2.3.5 Large-Scale MOOC Analytics and Collective Intelligence . . .\nThe UNDERSTAND Component: Supporting Deep Comprehension . .\n2.4.1 Multimodal Information Processing and Integration . . . . . . .\n2.4.2 Summarization Technologies for Educational Content . . . . . .\n2.4.3 Cognitive Load Management in Digital Learning . . . . . . . .\nThe IMITATE Component: Feedback for Skill Acquisition . . . . . . .\n2.5.1 Computer-Assisted Pronunciation Training: Evolution and Current State . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .\n2.5.2 Visualization Techniques for Performance Feedback . . . . . .\n2.5.3 Multidimensional Assessment Frameworks . .