{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-86f3da604ceda1f7","type":"tool_result","content":"534\t\fChapter 1\n535\tIntroduction\n536\t“The public is indoctrinated to believe that skills are valuable and reliable\n537\tonly if they are the result of formal schooling.”\n538\t— Ivan Illich, Deschooling Society, 1971\n539\t\n540\t1.1\n541\t\n542\tMotivation and Historical Context: The Challenge of Eﬀicient Learning from Audio-Visual Media\n543\t\n544\tThe proliferation of the internet and digital technologies has fundamentally transformed\n545\tthe educational paradigm. Learning that was once constrained by physical classrooms\n546\tand fixed curricula is now possible anytime, anywhere. The emergence of MOOCs (see\n547\tFigure 1.1), educational video platforms, podcasts, and audiobooks has democratized\n548\taccess to expertise worldwide [48, 113], enabling learning that transcends geographical,\n549\teconomic, and temporal constraints.\n550\tThis transformation deeply resonates with the vision Ivan Illich proposed in “Deschooling\n551\tSociety” [82]. Illich criticized the monopoly of institutionalized schooling and advocated\n552\tfor building “learning webs” where individuals could autonomously learn from diverse\n553\tresources. Modern online learning environments are realizing parts of this ideal. Learners can now freely choose expert lectures, demonstrations, and experiences according to\n554\ttheir interests and pace, beyond the boundaries of formal educational institutions.\n555\tThe most notable change brought by this democratization of learning is the emergence\n556\tof audio and video as primary educational media. Unlike traditional text-based materials, audio-visual content provides rich learning experiences including actual expert performances, nuanced explanations, and visual demonstrations. As multimedia learning\n557\ttheory suggests [118, 119], lecture recordings, educational podcasts, tutorial videos, and\n558\taudiobooks go beyond mere information transmission to provide learners with a sense of\n559\t“being with the expert.”\n560\tAudio content in particular has created new learning opportunities through its portability.\n561\tListening to podcasts during commutes, enjoying audiobooks while exercising, playing\n562\tlecture recordings while doing housework̶these all embody the “learning anytime, anywhere” world that Illich envisioned. Similarly, video content enables visual explanations\n563\tof complex concepts, practical demonstrations, and virtual laboratory experiences from\n564\tremote locations, aligning with principles of effective educational videos [28].\n565\tHowever, the existence of these abundant audio-visual resources also creates new challenges. First, due to the linear and time-bound nature of these media, learners must consume lengthy content to obtain necessary information. A large-scale analysis of 6.9 million MOOC video-viewing sessions found that the fraction viewed decreased as videos\n566\tbecame longer and that median engagement was less than half of the duration for videos\n567\tlonger than nine minutes [66] (see Figure 1.2). This problem becomes even more pronounced with podcasts and audiobooks.\n568\t1\n569\t\n570\t\fFigure 1.1: An example of a Massive Open Online Course (MOOC) interface, typically\n571\tfeaturing a video lecture, supplementary materials, and interactive elements. This illustrates the shift towards accessible online learning environments.\n572\tSecond, observational learning̶watching and imitating expert performances̶is what\n573\taudio-visual media excels at, yet it also reveals its greatest limitation. Learners can repeatedly listen to native speaker pronunciation, observe skilled musicians’ performances, and\n574\tstudy professional athletes’ movements (see Figure 1.3). For instance, in an online dance\n575\tclass, learners try to mimic the instructor’s movements. However, objectively evaluating\n576\thow closely their own attempts approach the expert model remains extremely diﬀicult.\n577\tThis “lack of feedback” remains an unresolved challenge in computer-assisted learning\n578\tsystems [128].\n579\tThis research proposes solutions to these audio-visual media-specific challenges using\n580\tdeep learning technologies. Our goal is to realize autonomous and effective learning\n581\twith modern technology. Specifically, we build an integrated AI-guided system that can\n582\teﬀiciently extract knowledge from lengthy content (Consume and Understand) and support practice of observed skills through model-derived feedback (Imitate).\n583\tThrough this, we aim to maximize the potential of rich educational media like audio and\n584\tvideo while overcoming their inherent constraints, realizing a truly “effective learning\n585\tanytime, anywhere” environment. We hope this will contribute not merely as a technical solution but to building a new educational ecosystem where learners can proactively\n586\tdesign and optimize their own learning.\n587\t\n588\t1.2\n589\t\n590\tThe Dual Challenge: Ineﬀicient Content Consumption and Lack of Objective Feedback\n591\t\n592\tThe challenges facing modern learners are not merely about access to information, but\n593\tabout how to eﬀiciently process that information and transform it into practical skills.\n594\tThis research identifies two interrelated challenges in learning with audio and video content.\n595\t1.2.1 Challenge 1: Time-Ineﬀicient Consumption of Long-Form Audio-Visual Content\n596\tThe first challenge is the time ineﬀiciency in consuming long-form audio-visual content.\n597\tA typical university lecture lasts 60-90 minutes, podcast episodes range from 30-180 minutes, and audiobooks span several hours to tens of hours. However, as recent learning\n598\t\n599\t2\n600\t\n601\t\fFigure 1.2: Information-density variations inferred from collective click-frequency traces\n602\tin an online lecture video (reconstructed from the LectureScape interface [98]).\n603\t\n604\tFigure 1.3: An example of imitation learning in an online dance class. Learners attempt\n605\tto replicate the instructor’s postures and movements, often without immediate, granular\n606\tfeedback on their performance, highlighting a key challenge in online skill acquisition.\n607\tanalytics research reveals [98], the truly valuable information for learners often constitutes only a portion of the entire content.\n608\tIn audio content particularly, information density is not constant. Some segments, such\n609\tas introductions, repetitions, filler words, or explanations of familiar concepts, can be\n610\tunderstood at high speed without hindering comprehension. Conversely, sections with\n611\ttechnical terminology, complex concepts, or unclear speaker pronunciation require listening at standard speed or slower. Current uniform playback speed adjustments cannot\n612\taccommodate this variable information density, as studies on adaptive playback have\n613\tshown [138].\n614\tVideo content presents an even more complex challenge as it requires processing visual\n615\tinformation in addition to audio. According to cognitive load theory [182], learners must\n616\tintegrate information from multiple modalities̶speaker explanations, text and diagrams\n617\ton slides, demonstrations̶while eﬀiciently finding and understanding necessary information.\n618\t\n619\t3\n620\t\n621\t\f1.2.2 Challenge 2: Absence of Scalable, Objective Feedback for Imitation-Based\n622\tLearning\n623\tThe second challenge is the lack of objective, scalable feedback in imitation-based learning. Many practical skills (language pronunciation, musical instrument performance,\n624\tsports techniques) are acquired by observing and imitating expert performances. However, it is extremely diﬀicult for learners to objectively evaluate the differences between\n625\ttheir own performance and the expert model.\n626\tIn traditional face-to-face education, teachers directly observe learner performances and\n627\tprovide specific feedback. However, in online learning environments, providing such\n628\tpersonalized feedback at scale is impractical from cost and logistics perspectives [90].\n629\tAs a result, many learners must rely on self-assessment, which tends to be subjective and\n630\tinaccurate.\n631\tMoreover, many existing automated feedback systems are based on predefined error patterns and cannot adapt to individual learners’ specific diﬀiculties or progress [200]. These\n632\tsystems typically provide only binary judgments of “correct” or “incorrect,” lacking detailed information about where and how differences occur and how to improve.\n633\tThese two challenges are not independent but closely related in an effective learning process. Learners must first eﬀiciently consume content to acquire knowledge (Challenge 1),\n634\tthen require appropriate feedback when transforming that acquired knowledge into practical skills (Challenge 2). This research aims to solve these challenges in an integrated\n635\tmanner.\n636\t\n637\t1.3\n638\t\n639\tThe Bottleneck of Long-Form Media in Digital Learning\n640\t\n641\t1.3.1 Quantifying the Time Cost of Audio-Visual Learning\n642\tQuantitatively examining the time cost of audio-visual learning reveals its ineﬀiciency.\n643\tFor example, a typical university online course includes 2-3 hours of video lectures per\n644\tweek over 10-15 weeks, totaling 20-45 hours of content. In a large-scale MOOC log\n645\tanalysis, median engagement time was at most six minutes regardless of video length,\n646\tand the fraction viewed decreased for longer videos [66].\n647\tIn the realm of podcasts and audiobooks, the situation is even more pronounced. Popular\n648\teducational podcasts average 60-90 minutes per episode, with many series containing\n649\tover 100 episodes. Audiobooks average 8-12 hours for non-fiction titles, with more comprehensive works exceeding 20 hours. For learners seeking to leverage these resources\n650\tfor professional development or lifelong learning, the time investment can become prohibitive.\n651\tThe linear nature of audio content in particular presents unique challenges. Unlike text,\n652\taudio cannot be easily scanned or jumped to relevant sections. Learners must listen\n653\tthrough irrelevant content to reach specific information of interest. This “forced linear\n654\tconsumption” creates a significant time burden, especially for learners searching for specific topics or concepts, as noted in studies on video navigation [98].\n655\t1.3.2 The Variable Nature of Content Complexity and Clarity\n656\tThe complexity and clarity of audio-visual content varies significantly even within the\n657\tsame content. Acoustic analysis reveals the following variations in educational audio\n658\tcontent: (1) Speech rate variation: ranging from 100 to over 200 words per minute even\n659\tfor the same speaker (2) Pronunciation clarity: varying due to technical terms, foreign\n660\twords, or speaker fatigue (3) Background noise: signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) changes\n661\tdepending on recording environment (4) Information density: varies greatly between\n662\tsections such as concept explanations, examples, and summaries\n663\t4\n664\t\n665\t\fCurrent common approaches of uniform playback speed changes have fundamental limitations for these variations. Research shows that uniform high-speed playback exceeding\n666\t1.5x significantly reduces comprehension of complex content and increases cognitive\n667\tload [127, 138]. However, a recent meta-analysis shows that, for clear and simple material, comprehension remains largely intact up to roughly 1.5x speed [185].\n668\t1.3.3 Limitations of Current Navigation and Summarization Approaches\n669\tCurrent navigation and summarization approaches for audio-visual content have several fundamental limitations. While transcription through automatic speech recognition\n670\t(ASR) has become widely available, it provides only a partial solution.\n671\tFirst, for audio content (podcasts, audiobooks), transcription loses prosodic features of\n672\tspeech (emphasis, emotion, irony, etc.). These features play important roles in conveying\n673\tspeaker intent and importance, especially in educational content. Furthermore, transcriptions of long audio become massive texts themselves, creating new information overload\n674\tproblems.\n675\tSecond, for video content, many existing summarization technologies rely only on speech\n676\ttranscripts and do not adequately utilize visual information (slides, diagrams, demonstrations, body language). Recent work on abstractive video lecture summarization highlights this limitation [22]. This loses the richness of video content’s multimodal nature\n677\tand can generate incomplete or misleading summaries.\n678\tThird, many current summarization systems present summary results as new text, which\n679\tbreaks continuity with the original content and can reduce learner trust and engagement.\n680\tIn particular, the original speaker’s voice and speaking style play important roles in educational content, and preserving these is important from a learning effectiveness perspective, as research on multimodal feedback suggests [119].\n681\t\n682\t1.4\n683\t\n684\tThe Feedback Deficit in Imitation-Based Skill Acquisition\n685\t\n686\t1.4.1 The Critical Role of Feedback in Skill Learning\n687\tIn practical skill acquisition, feedback is an essential element. Cognitive science research\n688\tshows that skill learning progresses through cycles of observation, practice, feedback, and\n689\tcorrection [16]. However, in online learning environments, the “feedback” stage of this\n690\tcycle is notably lacking.\n691\tFor imitation-based skills (language pronunciation, musical instrument performance, physical movements), learners need the following specific information: (1) Which parts of\n692\ttheir performance deviate from the expert model (2) The degree of that deviation (3)\n693\tWhich direction corrections should take In traditional face-to-face instruction, skilled\n694\tteachers provide this information immediately, but achieving this in large-scale online environments has been diﬀicult with current technology, as motor learning research demonstrates [178].\n695\t1.4.2 The Scalability Challenge of Human Feedback\n696\tWhile feedback from human experts is high quality, it faces fundamental scalability problems. For example, if 1000 learners each practice pronunciation 5 times per week, providing 2 minutes of feedback per practice would require approximately 167 hours of expert\n697\ttime per week. This is equivalent to about 4 full-time teachers. On large-scale platforms\n698\tlike MOOCs where enrollment can reach tens of thousands, this problem becomes even\n699\tmore severe [90].\n700\t\n701\t5\n702\t\n703\t\fFigure 1.4: The iterative Consume‒Understand‒Imitate learning loop. Each stage is later\n704\taugmented by a dedicated AI subsystem (AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy; see §1.5.4).\n705\tFurthermore, in asynchronous online learning environments, feedback delay is also an\n706\timportant issue. Learning science research shows that immediate feedback is more effective for learning than delayed feedback [178]. However, in systems dependent on human\n707\texperts, feedback delays are unavoidable.\n708\t1.4.3 Limitations of Existing Automated Feedback Systems\n709\tExisting automated feedback systems are mainly classified into two approaches: rulebased systems and supervised learning systems. Both have fundamental limitations in\n710\tproviding the flexible and adaptive feedback needed in online learning contexts.\n711\tRule-based systems rely on databases containing predefined error patterns and their correction methods. While these systems function for anticipated errors, they cannot handle\n712\tindividual learners’ unique error patterns or errors not anticipated during system design.\n713\tRecent systematic reviews of CAPT systems confirm these limitations [34]. Additionally,\n714\tadapting to new languages, dialects, or skill domains requires complete redesign.\n715\tSupervised learning systems require large amounts of labeled data (pairs of correct and\n716\tincorrect examples). Collecting and annotating such data is time-consuming and costly,\n717\tand particularly impractical when needing to cover diverse error patterns and learner backgrounds. Furthermore, these systems tend to have low generalization ability for patterns\n718\tnot included in the training data.\n719\tDue to these limitations, many current automated feedback systems are limited to providing binary “correct/incorrect” judgments or limited quantitative scores. They cannot provide the detailed, actionable feedback that learners truly need regarding “where,” “how\n720\tmuch,” and “how” their performance differs. Recent advances in ASR-based pronunciation training still struggle with this granularity [129].\n721\t\n722\t1.5\n723\t\n724\tTowards an Integrated Solution: The Consume-Understand-Imitate\n725\tLearning Pipeline and AI-Guided Learning\n726\t\n727\t1.5.1 The Natural Flow of Skill-Based Learning\n728\tFor the purposes of this dissertation, we organize practical skill learning as a staged process. Informed by multimedia learning research [118], we conceptualize learning with\n729\taudio and video as three interrelated stages: Consume, Understand, and Imitate. This\n730\tmodel is an organizing framework proposed in this dissertation rather than an empirically established sequence. Figure 1.4 visualises the cyclical relationship of these three\n731\tstages.\n732\t\n733\t6\n734\t\n735\t\fIn the Consume stage, learners explore and identify relevant information from large\n736\tamounts of available content. The main challenge at this stage is time eﬀiciency. They\n737\tneed to quickly find parts relevant to their learning goals from lengthy podcasts, lecture\n738\trecordings, and educational videos.\n739\tIn the Understand stage, learners deeply process identified information and build conceptual understanding. The challenge here is to eﬀiciently grasp core concepts while dealing\n740\twith content complexity and variations in information density. Speech clarity, speaking\n741\trate, and use of technical terms affect the speed and depth of understanding, as cognitive\n742\tload theory predicts [182].\n743\tIn the Imitate stage, learners put observed knowledge and skills into practice. Most important at this stage is informative feedback on their own performance. Learners need\n744\tspecific information to recognize differences from expert models and correct them, aligning with Bandura’s observational learning theory [17].\n745\t1.5.2 AI-Guided Learning: A Framework for Integrated Support\n746\tThis research proposes “AI-Guided Learning” as a new paradigm for comprehensively\n747\tsupporting these learning stages. AI-Guided Learning is an integrated approach that\n748\tleverages deep learning technologies to provide adaptive support at each learning stage,\n749\tenabling learners to eﬀiciently and effectively acquire knowledge and skills from audiovisual content.\n750\tThe core of AI-Guided Learning is that AI functions as a “guide” for learners. While traditional AI education systems focused on automating specific tasks, AI-Guided Learning\n751\taims to reduce the cognitive load required at each stage and maximize learning effectiveness while maintaining learner autonomy. This aligns with recent perspectives on AI in\n752\teducation that emphasize augmentation rather than replacement [45].\n753\tAI-Guided Learning is based on three principles: (1) Adaptivity: Dynamic adjustment\n754\taccording to learners’ individual needs, comprehension speed, and skill levels (2) Integration: Seamless coordination and mutual support between Consume-Understand-Imitate\n755\tstages (3) Transparency: Presentation of AI’s judgments and recommendations in a form\n756\tlearners can understand\n757\t1.5.3 The Need for Integrated Technological Support\n758\tThe three stages of Consume-Understand-Imitate are not linear but iterative and cyclical.\n759\tAI-Guided Learning recognizes and leverages this cyclical nature. For example, learners\n760\twho receive feedback in the imitation stage may return to specific content segments to\n761\tdeepen understanding (revisiting the Understand stage) or search for new expert examples\n762\t(revisiting the Consume stage).\n763\tMany current educational technologies are “point” solutions focused on one of these\n764\tstages. Video summarization tools, playback speed control apps, pronunciation checkers\n765\tare each useful, but lack an integrated approach supporting the entire learning process.\n766\tAI-Guided Learning integrates these individual functions and provides a comprehensive\n767\tframework that offers appropriate tools and support according to learners’ situations.\n768\t1.5.4 AI-Driven Subsystems for AI-Guided Learning\n769\tTo realize AI-Guided Learning, this research develops three AI-driven subsystems. These\n770\tsystems are designed to support specific stages of the Consume-Understand-Imitate pipeline\n771\twhile providing an integrated AI-Guided Learning experience as a whole.\n772\tAIxSpeed handles the eﬀiciency of Consume-Understand stages in AI-Guided Learning.\n773\tThrough phoneme-level acoustic feature analysis, it adjusts playback speed while using\n774\tspeech-recognition performance as a proxy for intelligibility.\n775\t7\n776\t\n777\t\fFastPerson revolutionizes the Consume-Understand stages for long-form video content in\n778\tAI-Guided Learning. By integrating multimodal information and providing summaries\n779\tin the original speaker’s voice using recent voice cloning advances [30], it guides learners\n780\tto eﬀiciently grasp core content while also providing options to access details as needed.\n781\tProfy guides learners to objectively evaluate and improve their own skills in the Imitate\n782\tstage of AI-Guided Learning. By analyzing and visualizing differences from expert performances in detail using self-supervised learning techniques [96], it enables precise skill\n783\tacquisition that was traditionally diﬀicult without human instructors.\n784\tThrough integration of these systems, AI-Guided Learning provides learners not merely a\n785\tcollection of individual tools but a coherent learning experience. Following AI guidance,\n786\tlearners can realize a complete learning cycle: eﬀiciently finding necessary information\n787\tfrom large amounts of content (Consume), deeply understanding it (Understand), and\n788\tmastering it as practical skills (Imitate).\n789\tAI-Guided Learning aims for essential transformation of learning beyond technological\n790\tinnovation. It establishes a new learning paradigm where learners can progress eﬀiciently\n791\tand effectively toward their goals without getting lost in the vast sea of digital resources.\n792\t\n793\t1.6\n794\t\n795\tResearch Objectives and Questions\n796\t\n797\t1.6.1 Overall Research Objective\n798\tThe overarching objective of this research is to solve three fundamental challenges in\n799\tlearning with audio and video under the AI-Guided Learning framework: (1) Time inefficiency in listening to long-form audio content (2) Diﬀiculty in integrated summarization\n800\tof visual and audio information in long-form video content (3) Diﬀiculty in identifying\n801\tspecific locations of errors in imitation learning\n802\tTo achieve this objective, we set the following central research question:\n803\tCentral Research Question (CRQ): To what extent can adaptive support at\n804\teach stage of consuming, understanding, and imitating audio-visual content\n805\tthrough deep learning technologies achieve reductions in learning time and\n806\timprovements in learning outcomes?\n807\t1.6.2 Specific Research Questions\n808\tFrom this central question, we set three specific research questions:\n809\t• RQ1: Balancing Listening Time Reduction and Comprehension Maintenance\n810\tin Audio Content\n811\tUnderlying Challenge: Long-form audio content such as podcasts, audiobooks,\n812\tand interviews is typically played at constant speed, despite variations in content\n813\timportance and speaker clarity across segments. This results in wasting time on\n814\teasily understandable parts and missing content in diﬀicult-to-understand parts.\n815\tResearch Question: By automatically determining the listening diﬀiculty of each\n816\tpart of audio content and optimizing playback speed for each segment, to what\n817\textent can we reduce listening time while maintaining comprehension? How much\n818\tdoes such variable-speed playback improve listenability compared to constant highspeed playback?\n819\t• RQ2: Eﬀicient Summarization Integrating Visual and Audio Information in\n820\tLecture Videos\n821\t\n822\t8\n823\t\n824\t\fUnderlying Challenge: Lecture videos contain multiple information channels including speaker explanations (audio), slides and board writing (visual), and gestures, but conventional summarization methods cannot handle these integratively.\n825\tAdditionally, when summary results are presented only as text, the original lecture context and speaker characteristics are lost, disrupting the continuity of the\n826\tlearning experience.\n827\tResearch Question: By integratively analyzing visual and audio information in\n828\tlecture videos and providing summaries in the original video format, to what extent\n829\tcan we reduce viewing time? What impact does an interface allowing switching\n830\tbetween summary and original videos have on learner comprehension?\n831\t• RQ3: Model-Derived Localization of Performance Differences in Imitation\n832\tLearning\n833\tUnderlying Challenge: In imitation-based skill acquisition such as pronunciation\n834\tlearning, learners cannot objectively understand which parts of their performance\n835\tdeviate from the target. Conventional systems either provide overall scores or detect only specific error patterns, and cannot point out problem areas specific to\n836\tindividual learners in detail.\n837\tResearch Question: Is it possible to highlight model-derived regions associated\n838\twith performance differences using small amounts of learner data and large amounts\n839\tof expert data? What impact does such localized feedback have on learners’ pronunciation improvement compared with an elicited-imitation baseline?\n840\tFor these research questions, we will conduct the following evaluations: For RQ1, we will\n841\tmeasure subjective evaluation of listenability and actual time reduction rate; For RQ2, we\n842\twill conduct viewing time reduction rate and comprehension evaluation through quizzes;\n843\tFor RQ3, we will verify the improvement in pronunciation evaluation by experts.\n844\tThrough these studies, we evaluate time eﬀiciency, perceived audio quality, quiz-measured\n845\tcomprehension, and pronunciation intelligibility within the scope of the three prototypes.\n846\t\n847\t1.7\n848\t\n849\tSignificance and Contributions of the Research\n850\t\n851\t1.7.1 Technical Innovations\n852\tThis research has the following technical novelties in developing audio and video processing technologies specifically for educational applications:\n853\t1. Phoneme-level playback speed optimization using ASR confidence (AIxSpeed):\n854\tUnlike conventional uniform speed changes, this method uses speech recognition model’s\n855\trecognition probability as a proxy for “listenability” and dynamically controls playback\n856\tspeed for each phoneme. A key feature is determining optimal speed for each part based\n857\tsolely on acoustic features without requiring semantic understanding. This technology\n858\tcan be extended to other audio processing applications such as hearing aids that automatically slow down diﬀicult-to-hear parts for the hearing impaired.\n859\t2. Video-to-video summarization preserving visual and auditory information (FastPerson): While most existing video summarization technologies are limited to audio\n860\ttranscription or text output, this method integratively analyzes visual information (slides,\n861\tboard writing, gestures) and audio information, reconstructing summarized content as\n862\tvideo with the original speaker’s voice. It is particularly unique in allowing learners\n863\tto select viewing granularity according to their understanding through an interface that\n864\tswitches between summary and original videos by chapter.\n865\t3. Model-derived localization and visualization of pronunciation differences with\n866\tsmall data (Profy): While conventional CAPT systems require detailed annotated data\n867\t9\n868\t\n869\t\fand comparison with specific speakers, this method learns pronunciation quality in a datadriven manner from small amounts of unannotated learner speech and large amounts of\n870\tnative speech. It is novel in providing more localized information than an overall score by\n871\thighlighting waveform regions emphasized by the classifier and visualizing distances in\n872\tlatent space. This framework, inspired by recent self-supervised learning advances [65],\n873\tis also expected to be applicable to imitation learning tasks beyond pronunciation.\n874\t1.7.2 Validated Educational Impact\n875\tThe principal empirical results reported for the three systems are as follows:\n876\tAdaptive playback with improved listenability (AIxSpeed): Blind comparison experiments confirmed higher mean opinion scores than constant-speed playback matched to\n877\tAIxSpeed’s average playback factors (1.30x for LibriSpeech and 1.29x for UME-ERJ).\n878\tThe UME-ERJ result also suggests that AIxSpeed can improve the listenability of nonnative-speaker utterances for native listeners.\n879\tHalving viewing time without a significant quiz-score difference (FastPerson): In\n880\tlecture-video viewing experiments with 40 participants, an average 53% reduction in\n881\tviewing time was achieved, and quiz-measured comprehension showed no statistically\n882\tsignificant difference from conventional normal playback. Among the FastPerson users,\n883\t78% rated the chapter-switching function as useful or very useful.\n884\tImproved pronunciation clarity and helpful feedback features (Profy): In experiments with Japanese learners of English, American raters judged post-practice speech to\n885\tbe more intelligible, and all participants rated the pronunciation-score display as helpful.\n886\t1.7.3 Contribution to AI-Guided Learning Paradigm\n887\tThe most important contribution of this research is demonstrating a new learning support paradigm called AI-Guided Learning, beyond the three individual technologies. We\n888\tshowed specific ways AI can augment learner capabilities at each stage of Consume (efficient browsing), Understand (deep comprehension), and Imitate (skill acquisition).\n889\tThese technologies, under the common vision of “augmenting human learning and comprehension abilities with AI,” build a comprehensive support ecosystem from video viewing to language use by covering different learning phases. In the future, this framework\n890\tis expected to be extended to other learning domains and support for acquiring more advanced cognitive and physical skills, as envisioned in recent discussions of AI’s future in\n891\teducation [165].\n892\t\n893\t1.8\n894\t\n895\tDissertation Structure\n896\t\n897\tThis dissertation is structured as follows to systematically present the concept, technical\n898\timplementation, empirical evaluation, and educational implications of AI-Guided Learning:\n899\tChapter 2: Related Work comprehensively reviews existing research in three areas\n900\tto clarify the academic positioning of this research: (1) adaptive audio/video playback\n901\ttechnologies, (2) educational content summarization technologies, (3) feedback systems\n902\tin imitation learning. We clarify the technical limitations of each area and the novelty of\n903\tour approach.\n904\tChapter 3: AIxSpeed̶Adaptive High-Speed Audio Playback details the phonemelevel playback speed control system based on ASR confidence. We present the system\n905\tarchitecture, speed optimization algorithm, and results of evaluation experiments on listenability.\n906\t\n907\t10\n908\t\n909\t\fChapter 4: FastPerson̶Multimodal Video Summarization explains the design and\n910\timplementation of a video summarization system integrating visual and audio information. We report on chapter-based summary generation methods, re-narration through\n911\tvoice synthesis, and evaluation results from viewing experiments with 40 participants.\n912\tChapter 5: Profy̶Automated Feedback for Pronunciation Learning presents a system that learns a pronunciation-proficiency classifier from small data and visualizes classifieremphasized regions and model-derived distances. We explain the learning method, feedback generation, and evaluation with Japanese learners of English.\n913\tChapter 6: Integration and Discussion discusses integratively how the three systems\n914\tsupport the Consume-Understand-Imitate learning cycle. We consider design principles,\n915\timplementation challenges, and ethical considerations of AI-Guided Learning.\n916\tChapter 7: Conclusion and Future Directions summarizes the academic and practical\n917\tcontributions of this research and provides perspectives on future development possibilities of the AI-Guided Learning paradigm. We discuss challenges and opportunities for\n918\textension to other learning domains, integration with more advanced AI technologies,\n919\tand large-scale deployment in educational settings.\n920\t\n921\t1.9\n922\t\n923\tConcluding Remarks\n924\t\n925\tThe proliferation of online education has dramatically improved access to learning resources. However, as this research has revealed, the true challenges lie beyond access.\n926\tHow to eﬀiciently consume long-form audio-visual content, how to effectively understand complex audiovisual information, and how to reliably acquire observed skills̶\n927\tfor these challenges, this research has presented an integrated solution called AI-Guided\n928\tLearning.\n929\tThe three systems̶AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy̶each developed as independent\n930\tresearch results, create greater educational value when integrated. These are not merely\n931\ttime-saving tools but “learning augmentation technologies” designed to help learners\n932\tmaximize their capabilities.\n933\tThe vision of AI-Guided Learning presented in this dissertation suggests a new relationship between technology and education. AI becomes a partner that augments the capabilities of teachers and learners rather than replacing them, enabling more effective learning.\n934\tWe hope this research will be a step toward realizing a more equitable and eﬀicient learning environment where all learners can receive high-quality education.\n935\t\n936\t11\n937\t\n938\t\fChapter 2\n939\tRelated Work\n940\t2.1\n941\t\n942\tIntroduction\n943\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6af2b770-4e64-4fd8-b01f-b5667819ace1","uuid":"69c62665-aad6-4381-8bc3-d883537b31e9","timestamp":"2026-08-11T17:16:58.509Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/full.txt","content":"\fChapter 1\nIntroduction\n“The public is indoctrinated to believe that skills are valuable and reliable\nonly if they are the result of formal schooling.”\n— Ivan Illich, Deschooling Society, 1971\n\n1.1\n\nMotivation and Historical Context: The Challenge of Eﬀicient Learning from Audio-Visual Media\n\nThe proliferation of the internet and digital technologies has fundamentally transformed\nthe educational paradigm. Learning that was once constrained by physical classrooms\nand fixed curricula is now possible anytime, anywhere. The emergence of MOOCs (see\nFigure 1.1), educational video platforms, podcasts, and audiobooks has democratized\naccess to expertise worldwide [48, 113], enabling learning that transcends geographical,\neconomic, and temporal constraints.\nThis transformation deeply resonates with the vision Ivan Illich proposed in “Deschooling\nSociety” [82]. Illich criticized the monopoly of institutionalized schooling and advocated\nfor building “learning webs” where individuals could autonomously learn from diverse\nresources. Modern online learning environments are realizing parts of this ideal. Learners can now freely choose expert lectures, demonstrations, and experiences according to\ntheir interests and pace, beyond the boundaries of formal educational institutions.\nThe most notable change brought by this democratization of learning is the emergence\nof audio and video as primary educational media. Unlike traditional text-based materials, audio-visual content provides rich learning experiences including actual expert performances, nuanced explanations, and visual demonstrations. As multimedia learning\ntheory suggests [118, 119], lecture recordings, educational podcasts, tutorial videos, and\naudiobooks go beyond mere information transmission to provide learners with a sense of\n“being with the expert.”\nAudio content in particular has created new learning opportunities through its portability.\nListening to podcasts during commutes, enjoying audiobooks while exercising, playing\nlecture recordings while doing housework̶these all embody the “learning anytime, anywhere” world that Illich envisioned. Similarly, video content enables visual explanations\nof complex concepts, practical demonstrations, and virtual laboratory experiences from\nremote locations, aligning with principles of effective educational videos [28].\nHowever, the existence of these abundant audio-visual resources also creates new challenges. First, due to the linear and time-bound nature of these media, learners must consume lengthy content to obtain necessary information. A large-scale analysis of 6.9 million MOOC video-viewing sessions found that the fraction viewed decreased as videos\nbecame longer and that median engagement was less than half of the duration for videos\nlonger than nine minutes [66] (see Figure 1.2). This problem becomes even more pronounced with podcasts and audiobooks.\n1\n\n\fFigure 1.1: An example of a Massive Open Online Course (MOOC) interface, typically\nfeaturing a video lecture, supplementary materials, and interactive elements. This illustrates the shift towards accessible online learning environments.\nSecond, observational learning̶watching and imitating expert performances̶is what\naudio-visual media excels at, yet it also reveals its greatest limitation. Learners can repeatedly listen to native speaker pronunciation, observe skilled musicians’ performances, and\nstudy professional athletes’ movements (see Figure 1.3). For instance, in an online dance\nclass, learners try to mimic the instructor’s movements. However, objectively evaluating\nhow closely their own attempts approach the expert model remains extremely diﬀicult.\nThis “lack of feedback” remains an unresolved challenge in computer-assisted learning\nsystems [128].\nThis research proposes solutions to these audio-visual media-specific challenges using\ndeep learning technologies. Our goal is to realize autonomous and effective learning\nwith modern technology. Specifically, we build an integrated AI-guided system that can\neﬀiciently extract knowledge from lengthy content (Consume and Understand) and support practice of observed skills through model-derived feedback (Imitate).\nThrough this, we aim to maximize the potential of rich educational media like audio and\nvideo while overcoming their inherent constraints, realizing a truly “effective learning\nanytime, anywhere” environment. We hope this will contribute not merely as a technical solution but to building a new educational ecosystem where learners can proactively\ndesign and optimize their own learning.\n\n1.2\n\nThe Dual Challenge: Ineﬀicient Content Consumption and Lack of Objective Feedback\n\nThe challenges facing modern learners are not merely about access to information, but\nabout how to eﬀiciently process that information and transform it into practical skills.\nThis research identifies two interrelated challenges in learning with audio and video content.\n1.2.1 Challenge 1: Time-Ineﬀicient Consumption of Long-Form Audio-Visual Content\nThe first challenge is the time ineﬀiciency in consuming long-form audio-visual content.\nA typical university lecture lasts 60-90 minutes, podcast episodes range from 30-180 minutes, and audiobooks span several hours to tens of hours. However, as recent learning\n\n2\n\n\fFigure 1.2: Information-density variations inferred from collective click-frequency traces\nin an online lecture video (reconstructed from the LectureScape interface [98]).\n\nFigure 1.3: An example of imitation learning in an online dance class. Learners attempt\nto replicate the instructor’s postures and movements, often without immediate, granular\nfeedback on their performance, highlighting a key challenge in online skill acquisition.\nanalytics research reveals [98], the truly valuable information for learners often constitutes only a portion of the entire content.\nIn audio content particularly, information density is not constant. Some segments, such\nas introductions, repetitions, filler words, or explanations of familiar concepts, can be\nunderstood at high speed without hindering comprehension. Conversely, sections with\ntechnical terminology, complex concepts, or unclear speaker pronunciation require listening at standard speed or slower. Current uniform playback speed adjustments cannot\naccommodate this variable information density, as studies on adaptive playback have\nshown [138].\nVideo content presents an even more complex challenge as it requires processing visual\ninformation in addition to audio. According to cognitive load theory [182], learners must\nintegrate information from multiple modalities̶speaker explanations, text and diagrams\non slides, demonstrations̶while eﬀiciently finding and understanding necessary information.\n\n3\n\n\f1.2.2 Challenge 2: Absence of Scalable, Objective Feedback for Imitation-Based\nLearning\nThe second challenge is the lack of objective, scalable feedback in imitation-based learning. Many practical skills (language pronunciation, musical instrument performance,\nsports techniques) are acquired by observing and imitating expert performances. However, it is extremely diﬀicult for learners to objectively evaluate the differences between\ntheir own performance and the expert model.\nIn traditional face-to-face education, teachers directly observe learner performances and\nprovide specific feedback. However, in online learning environments, providing such\npersonalized feedback at scale is impractical from cost and logistics perspectives [90].\nAs a result, many learners must rely on self-assessment, which tends to be subjective and\ninaccurate.\nMoreover, many existing automated feedback systems are based on predefined error patterns and cannot adapt to individual learners’ specific diﬀiculties or progress [200]. These\nsystems typically provide only binary judgments of “correct” or “incorrect,” lacking detailed information about where and how differences occur and how to improve.\nThese two challenges are not independent but closely related in an effective learning process. Learners must first eﬀiciently consume content to acquire knowledge (Challenge 1),\nthen require appropriate feedback when transforming that acquired knowledge into practical skills (Challenge 2). This research aims to solve these challenges in an integrated\nmanner.\n\n1.3\n\nThe Bottleneck of Long-Form Media in Digital Learning\n\n1.3.1 Quantifying the Time Cost of Audio-Visual Learning\nQuantitatively examining the time cost of audio-visual learning reveals its ineﬀiciency.\nFor example, a typical university online course includes 2-3 hours of video lectures per\nweek over 10-15 weeks, totaling 20-45 hours of content. In a large-scale MOOC log\nanalysis, median engagement time was at most six minutes regardless of video length,\nand the fraction viewed decreased for longer videos [66].\nIn the realm of podcasts and audiobooks, the situation is even more pronounced. Popular\neducational podcasts average 60-90 minutes per episode, with many series containing\nover 100 episodes. Audiobooks average 8-12 hours for non-fiction titles, with more comprehensive works exceeding 20 hours. For learners seeking to leverage these resources\nfor professional development or lifelong learning, the time investment can become prohibitive.\nThe linear nature of audio content in particular presents unique challenges. Unlike text,\naudio cannot be easily scanned or jumped to relevant sections. Learners must listen\nthrough irrelevant content to reach specific information of interest. This “forced linear\nconsumption” creates a significant time burden, especially for learners searching for specific topics or concepts, as noted in studies on video navigation [98].\n1.3.2 The Variable Nature of Content Complexity and Clarity\nThe complexity and clarity of audio-visual content varies significantly even within the\nsame content. Acoustic analysis reveals the following variations in educational audio\ncontent: (1) Speech rate variation: ranging from 100 to over 200 words per minute even\nfor the same speaker (2) Pronunciation clarity: varying due to technical terms, foreign\nwords, or speaker fatigue (3) Background noise: signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) changes\ndepending on recording environment (4) Information density: varies greatly between\nsections such as concept explanations, examples, and summaries\n4\n\n\fCurrent common approaches of uniform playback speed changes have fundamental limitations for these variations. Research shows that uniform high-speed playback exceeding\n1.5x significantly reduces comprehension of complex content and increases cognitive\nload [127, 138]. However, a recent meta-analysis shows that, for clear and simple material, comprehension remains largely intact up to roughly 1.5x speed [185].\n1.3.3 Limitations of Current Navigation and Summarization Approaches\nCurrent navigation and summarization approaches for audio-visual content have several fundamental limitations. While transcription through automatic speech recognition\n(ASR) has become widely available, it provides only a partial solution.\nFirst, for audio content (podcasts, audiobooks), transcription loses prosodic features of\nspeech (emphasis, emotion, irony, etc.). These features play important roles in conveying\nspeaker intent and importance, especially in educational content. Furthermore, transcriptions of long audio become massive texts themselves, creating new information overload\nproblems.\nSecond, for video content, many existing summarization technologies rely only on speech\ntranscripts and do not adequately utilize visual information (slides, diagrams, demonstrations, body language). Recent work on abstractive video lecture summarization highlights this limitation [22]. This loses the richness of video content’s multimodal nature\nand can generate incomplete or misleading summaries.\nThird, many current summarization systems present summary results as new text, which\nbreaks continuity with the original content and can reduce learner trust and engagement.\nIn particular, the original speaker’s voice and speaking style play important roles in educational content, and preserving these is important from a learning effectiveness perspective, as research on multimodal feedback suggests [119].\n\n1.4\n\nThe Feedback Deficit in Imitation-Based Skill Acquisition\n\n1.4.1 The Critical Role of Feedback in Skill Learning\nIn practical skill acquisition, feedback is an essential element. Cognitive science research\nshows that skill learning progresses through cycles of observation, practice, feedback, and\ncorrection [16]. However, in online learning environments, the “feedback” stage of this\ncycle is notably lacking.\nFor imitation-based skills (language pronunciation, musical instrument performance, physical movements), learners need the following specific information: (1) Which parts of\ntheir performance deviate from the expert model (2) The degree of that deviation (3)\nWhich direction corrections should take In traditional face-to-face instruction, skilled\nteachers provide this information immediately, but achieving this in large-scale online environments has been diﬀicult with current technology, as motor learning research demonstrates [178].\n1.4.2 The Scalability Challenge of Human Feedback\nWhile feedback from human experts is high quality, it faces fundamental scalability problems. For example, if 1000 learners each practice pronunciation 5 times per week, providing 2 minutes of feedback per practice would require approximately 167 hours of expert\ntime per week. This is equivalent to about 4 full-time teachers. On large-scale platforms\nlike MOOCs where enrollment can reach tens of thousands, this problem becomes even\nmore severe [90].\n\n5\n\n\fFigure 1.4: The iterative Consume‒Understand‒Imitate learning loop. Each stage is later\naugmented by a dedicated AI subsystem (AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy; see §1.5.4).\nFurthermore, in asynchronous online learning environments, feedback delay is also an\nimportant issue. Learning science research shows that immediate feedback is more effective for learning than delayed feedback [178]. However, in systems dependent on human\nexperts, feedback delays are unavoidable.\n1.4.3 Limitations of Existing Automated Feedback Systems\nExisting automated feedback systems are mainly classified into two approaches: rulebased systems and supervised learning systems. Both have fundamental limitations in\nproviding the flexible and adaptive feedback needed in online learning contexts.\nRule-based systems rely on databases containing predefined error patterns and their correction methods. While these systems function for anticipated errors, they cannot handle\nindividual learners’ unique error patterns or errors not anticipated during system design.\nRecent systematic reviews of CAPT systems confirm these limitations [34]. Additionally,\nadapting to new languages, dialects, or skill domains requires complete redesign.\nSupervised learning systems require large amounts of labeled data (pairs of correct and\nincorrect examples). Collecting and annotating such data is time-consuming and costly,\nand particularly impractical when needing to cover diverse error patterns and learner backgrounds. Furthermore, these systems tend to have low generalization ability for patterns\nnot included in the training data.\nDue to these limitations, many current automated feedback systems are limited to providing binary “correct/incorrect” judgments or limited quantitative scores. They cannot provide the detailed, actionable feedback that learners truly need regarding “where,” “how\nmuch,” and “how” their performance differs. Recent advances in ASR-based pronunciation training still struggle with this granularity [129].\n\n1.5\n\nTowards an Integrated Solution: The Consume-Understand-Imitate\nLearning Pipeline and AI-Guided Learning\n\n1.5.1 The Natural Flow of Skill-Based Learning\nFor the purposes of this dissertation, we organize practical skill learning as a staged process. Informed by multimedia learning research [118], we conceptualize learning with\naudio and video as three interrelated stages: Consume, Understand, and Imitate. This\nmodel is an organizing framework proposed in this dissertation rather than an empirically established sequence. Figure 1.4 visualises the cyclical relationship of these three\nstages.\n\n6\n\n\fIn the Consume stage, learners explore and identify relevant information from large\namounts of available content. The main challenge at this stage is time eﬀiciency. They\nneed to quickly find parts relevant to their learning goals from lengthy podcasts, lecture\nrecordings, and educational videos.\nIn the Understand stage, learners deeply process identified information and build conceptual understanding. The challenge here is to eﬀiciently grasp core concepts while dealing\nwith content complexity and variations in information density. Speech clarity, speaking\nrate, and use of technical terms affect the speed and depth of understanding, as cognitive\nload theory predicts [182].\nIn the Imitate stage, learners put observed knowledge and skills into practice. Most important at this stage is informative feedback on their own performance. Learners need\nspecific information to recognize differences from expert models and correct them, aligning with Bandura’s observational learning theory [17].\n1.5.2 AI-Guided Learning: A Framework for Integrated Support\nThis research proposes “AI-Guided Learning” as a new paradigm for comprehensively\nsupporting these learning stages. AI-Guided Learning is an integrated approach that\nleverages deep learning technologies to provide adaptive support at each learning stage,\nenabling learners to eﬀiciently and effectively acquire knowledge and skills from audiovisual content.\nThe core of AI-Guided Learning is that AI functions as a “guide” for learners. While traditional AI education systems focused on automating specific tasks, AI-Guided Learning\naims to reduce the cognitive load required at each stage and maximize learning effectiveness while maintaining learner autonomy. This aligns with recent perspectives on AI in\neducation that emphasize augmentation rather than replacement [45].\nAI-Guided Learning is based on three principles: (1) Adaptivity: Dynamic adjustment\naccording to learners’ individual needs, comprehension speed, and skill levels (2) Integration: Seamless coordination and mutual support between Consume-Understand-Imitate\nstages (3) Transparency: Presentation of AI’s judgments and recommendations in a form\nlearners can understand\n1.5.3 The Need for Integrated Technological Support\nThe three stages of Consume-Understand-Imitate are not linear but iterative and cyclical.\nAI-Guided Learning recognizes and leverages this cyclical nature. For example, learners\nwho receive feedback in the imitation stage may return to specific content segments to\ndeepen understanding (revisiting the Understand stage) or search for new expert examples\n(revisiting the Consume stage).\nMany current educational technologies are “point” solutions focused on one of these\nstages. Video summarization tools, playback speed control apps, pronunciation checkers\nare each useful, but lack an integrated approach supporting the entire learning process.\nAI-Guided Learning integrates these individual functions and provides a comprehensive\nframework that offers appropriate tools and support according to learners’ situations.\n1.5.4 AI-Driven Subsystems for AI-Guided Learning\nTo realize AI-Guided Learning, this research develops three AI-driven subsystems. These\nsystems are designed to support specific stages of the Consume-Understand-Imitate pipeline\nwhile providing an integrated AI-Guided Learning experience as a whole.\nAIxSpeed handles the eﬀiciency of Consume-Understand stages in AI-Guided Learning.\nThrough phoneme-level acoustic feature analysis, it adjusts playback speed while using\nspeech-recognition performance as a proxy for intelligibility.\n7\n\n\fFastPerson revolutionizes the Consume-Understand stages for long-form video content in\nAI-Guided Learning. By integrating multimodal information and providing summaries\nin the original speaker’s voice using recent voice cloning advances [30], it guides learners\nto eﬀiciently grasp core content while also providing options to access details as needed.\nProfy guides learners to objectively evaluate and improve their own skills in the Imitate\nstage of AI-Guided Learning. By analyzing and visualizing differences from expert performances in detail using self-supervised learning techniques [96], it enables precise skill\nacquisition that was traditionally diﬀicult without human instructors.\nThrough integration of these systems, AI-Guided Learning provides learners not merely a\ncollection of individual tools but a coherent learning experience. Following AI guidance,\nlearners can realize a complete learning cycle: eﬀiciently finding necessary information\nfrom large amounts of content (Consume), deeply understanding it (Understand), and\nmastering it as practical skills (Imitate).\nAI-Guided Learning aims for essential transformation of learning beyond technological\ninnovation. It establishes a new learning paradigm where learners can progress eﬀiciently\nand effectively toward their goals without getting lost in the vast sea of digital resources.\n\n1.6\n\nResearch Objectives and Questions\n\n1.6.1 Overall Research Objective\nThe overarching objective of this research is to solve three fundamental challenges in\nlearning with audio and video under the AI-Guided Learning framework: (1) Time inefficiency in listening to long-form audio content (2) Diﬀiculty in integrated summarization\nof visual and audio information in long-form video content (3) Diﬀiculty in identifying\nspecific locations of errors in imitation learning\nTo achieve this objective, we set the following central research question:\nCentral Research Question (CRQ): To what extent can adaptive support at\neach stage of consuming, understanding, and imitating audio-visual content\nthrough deep learning technologies achieve reductions in learning time and\nimprovements in learning outcomes?\n1.6.2 Specific Research Questions\nFrom this central question, we set three specific research questions:\n• RQ1: Balancing Listening Time Reduction and Comprehension Maintenance\nin Audio Content\nUnderlying Challenge: Long-form audio content such as podcasts, audiobooks,\nand interviews is typically played at constant speed, despite variations in content\nimportance and speaker clarity across segments. This results in wasting time on\neasily understandable parts and missing content in diﬀicult-to-understand parts.\nResearch Question: By automatically determining the listening diﬀiculty of each\npart of audio content and optimizing playback speed for each segment, to what\nextent can we reduce listening time while maintaining comprehension? How much\ndoes such variable-speed playback improve listenability compared to constant highspeed playback?\n• RQ2: Eﬀicient Summarization Integrating Visual and Audio Information in\nLecture Videos\n\n8\n\n\fUnderlying Challenge: Lecture videos contain multiple information channels including speaker explanations (audio), slides and board writing (visual), and gestures, but conventional summarization methods cannot handle these integratively.\nAdditionally, when summary results are presented only as text, the original lecture context and speaker characteristics are lost, disrupting the continuity of the\nlearning experience.\nResearch Question: By integratively analyzing visual and audio information in\nlecture videos and providing summaries in the original video format, to what extent\ncan we reduce viewing time? What impact does an interface allowing switching\nbetween summary and original videos have on learner comprehension?\n• RQ3: Model-Derived Localization of Performance Differences in Imitation\nLearning\nUnderlying Challenge: In imitation-based skill acquisition such as pronunciation\nlearning, learners cannot objectively understand which parts of their performance\ndeviate from the target. Conventional systems either provide overall scores or detect only specific error patterns, and cannot point out problem areas specific to\nindividual learners in detail.\nResearch Question: Is it possible to highlight model-derived regions associated\nwith performance differences using small amounts of learner data and large amounts\nof expert data? What impact does such localized feedback have on learners’ pronunciation improvement compared with an elicited-imitation baseline?\nFor these research questions, we will conduct the following evaluations: For RQ1, we will\nmeasure subjective evaluation of listenability and actual time reduction rate; For RQ2, we\nwill conduct viewing time reduction rate and comprehension evaluation through quizzes;\nFor RQ3, we will verify the improvement in pronunciation evaluation by experts.\nThrough these studies, we evaluate time eﬀiciency, perceived audio quality, quiz-measured\ncomprehension, and pronunciation intelligibility within the scope of the three prototypes.\n\n1.7\n\nSignificance and Contributions of the Research\n\n1.7.1 Technical Innovations\nThis research has the following technical novelties in developing audio and video processing technologies specifically for educational applications:\n1. Phoneme-level playback speed optimization using ASR confidence (AIxSpeed):\nUnlike conventional uniform speed changes, this method uses speech recognition model’s\nrecognition probability as a proxy for “listenability” and dynamically controls playback\nspeed for each phoneme. A key feature is determining optimal speed for each part based\nsolely on acoustic features without requiring semantic understanding. This technology\ncan be extended to other audio processing applications such as hearing aids that automatically slow down diﬀicult-to-hear parts for the hearing impaired.\n2. Video-to-video summarization preserving visual and auditory information (FastPerson): While most existing video summarization technologies are limited to audio\ntranscription or text output, this method integratively analyzes visual information (slides,\nboard writing, gestures) and audio information, reconstructing summarized content as\nvideo with the original speaker’s voice. It is particularly unique in allowing learners\nto select viewing granularity according to their understanding through an interface that\nswitches between summary and original videos by chapter.\n3. Model-derived localization and visualization of pronunciation differences with\nsmall data (Profy): While conventional CAPT systems require detailed annotated data\n9\n\n\fand comparison with specific speakers, this method learns pronunciation quality in a datadriven manner from small amounts of unannotated learner speech and large amounts of\nnative speech. It is novel in providing more localized information than an overall score by\nhighlighting waveform regions emphasized by the classifier and visualizing distances in\nlatent space. This framework, inspired by recent self-supervised learning advances [65],\nis also expected to be applicable to imitation learning tasks beyond pronunciation.\n1.7.2 Validated Educational Impact\nThe principal empirical results reported for the three systems are as follows:\nAdaptive playback with improved listenability (AIxSpeed): Blind comparison experiments confirmed higher mean opinion scores than constant-speed playback matched to\nAIxSpeed’s average playback factors (1.30x for LibriSpeech and 1.29x for UME-ERJ).\nThe UME-ERJ result also suggests that AIxSpeed can improve the listenability of nonnative-speaker utterances for native listeners.\nHalving viewing time without a significant quiz-score difference (FastPerson): In\nlecture-video viewing experiments with 40 participants, an average 53% reduction in\nviewing time was achieved, and quiz-measured comprehension showed no statistically\nsignificant difference from conventional normal playback. Among the FastPerson users,\n78% rated the chapter-switching function as useful or very useful.\nImproved pronunciation clarity and helpful feedback features (Profy): In experiments with Japanese learners of English, American raters judged post-practice speech to\nbe more intelligible, and all participants rated the pronunciation-score display as helpful.\n1.7.3 Contribution to AI-Guided Learning Paradigm\nThe most important contribution of this research is demonstrating a new learning support paradigm called AI-Guided Learning, beyond the three individual technologies. We\nshowed specific ways AI can augment learner capabilities at each stage of Consume (efficient browsing), Understand (deep comprehension), and Imitate (skill acquisition).\nThese technologies, under the common vision of “augmenting human learning and comprehension abilities with AI,” build a comprehensive support ecosystem from video viewing to language use by covering different learning phases. In the future, this framework\nis expected to be extended to other learning domains and support for acquiring more advanced cognitive and physical skills, as envisioned in recent discussions of AI’s future in\neducation [165].\n\n1.8\n\nDissertation Structure\n\nThis dissertation is structured as follows to systematically present the concept, technical\nimplementation, empirical evaluation, and educational implications of AI-Guided Learning:\nChapter 2: Related Work comprehensively reviews existing research in three areas\nto clarify the academic positioning of this research: (1) adaptive audio/video playback\ntechnologies, (2) educational content summarization technologies, (3) feedback systems\nin imitation learning. We clarify the technical limitations of each area and the novelty of\nour approach.\nChapter 3: AIxSpeed̶Adaptive High-Speed Audio Playback details the phonemelevel playback speed control system based on ASR confidence. We present the system\narchitecture, speed optimization algorithm, and results of evaluation experiments on listenability.\n\n10\n\n\fChapter 4: FastPerson̶Multimodal Video Summarization explains the design and\nimplementation of a video summarization system integrating visual and audio information. We report on chapter-based summary generation methods, re-narration through\nvoice synthesis, and evaluation results from viewing experiments with 40 participants.\nChapter 5: Profy̶Automated Feedback for Pronunciation Learning presents a system that learns a pronunciation-proficiency classifier from small data and visualizes classifieremphasized regions and model-derived distances. We explain the learning method, feedback generation, and evaluation with Japanese learners of English.\nChapter 6: Integration and Discussion discusses integratively how the three systems\nsupport the Consume-Understand-Imitate learning cycle. We consider design principles,\nimplementation challenges, and ethical considerations of AI-Guided Learning.\nChapter 7: Conclusion and Future Directions summarizes the academic and practical\ncontributions of this research and provides perspectives on future development possibilities of the AI-Guided Learning paradigm. We discuss challenges and opportunities for\nextension to other learning domains, integration with more advanced AI technologies,\nand large-scale deployment in educational settings.\n\n1.9\n\nConcluding Remarks\n\nThe proliferation of online education has dramatically improved access to learning resources. However, as this research has revealed, the true challenges lie beyond access.\nHow to eﬀiciently consume long-form audio-visual content, how to effectively understand complex audiovisual information, and how to reliably acquire observed skills̶\nfor these challenges, this research has presented an integrated solution called AI-Guided\nLearning.\nThe three systems̶AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy̶each developed as independent\nresearch results, create greater educational value when integrated. These are not merely\ntime-saving tools but “learning augmentation technologies” designed to help learners\nmaximize their capabilities.\nThe vision of AI-Guided Learning presented in this dissertation suggests a new relationship between technology and education. AI becomes a partner that augments the capabilities of teachers and learners rather than replacing them, enabling more effective learning.\nWe hope this research will be a step toward realizing a more equitable and eﬀicient learning environment where all learners can receive high-quality education.\n\n11\n\n\fChapter 2\nRelated Work\n2.1\n\nIntroduction\n","numLines":410,"startLine":534,"totalLines":4774}}}
