{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2b86534778c2712","type":"tool_result","content":"1763\t\fChapter 3\n1764\tAIxSpeed: Playback Speed Optimization Using\n1765\tListening Comprehension of Speech Recognition\n1766\tModels\n1767\t3.1\n1768\t\n1769\tIntroduction\n1770\t\n1771\tThis chapter presents the AIxSpeed system, specifically designed to address a key challenge within the broader AI-Guided Learning framework: the time-ineﬀicient consumption of audio content during the Consume-Understand learning stages. As discussed\n1772\tin Chapter 1, modern learners often face the necessity of eﬀiciently acquiring knowledge from extensive long-form audio materials such as podcasts, audiobooks, and lecture recordings. This research focuses on optimizing the auditory learning experience\n1773\tby proposing an innovative method for adaptive playback speed control at the phoneme\n1774\tlevel, guided by a speech recognition model’s listening comprehension. However, due\n1775\tto the linear nature and variable information density of these contents, uniform playback speed cannot achieve optimal learning eﬀiciency, potentially leading to decreased\n1776\tattention, increased cognitive load from processing overly dense or unnecessarily slow\n1777\tinformation, and ultimately, suboptimal learning outcomes. AIxSpeed presents an innovative approach of adaptive playback speed control at the phoneme level to address this\n1778\tchallenge.\n1779\tThe core innovation of AIxSpeed lies in its ability to determine optimal playback speed\n1780\tfor each segment based purely on acoustic features without requiring semantic understanding of the educational content. This is achieved by leveraging speech recognition\n1781\tmodels’ confidence scores as a proxy for human listening ease. This content-agnostic approach enables the construction of a versatile system applicable to a wide range of audio\n1782\tcontent regardless of language or topic.\n1783\tWith the proliferation of video distribution services, opportunities for watching videos\n1784\tfor various purposes, including information gathering, learning, and entertainment, have\n1785\tsignificantly increased. Humans possess the ability to comprehend naturally observed\n1786\tphenomena at rates faster than their original occurrence. Consequently, it has become\n1787\tcommon practice to increase the playback speed of videos and audio to understand more\n1788\tcontent in a shorter period. Research has demonstrated that, under specific conditions,\n1789\tdifferences in video playback speed do not affect learning effectiveness in educational\n1790\tvideo viewing and may even enhance performance [32, 103]. Furthermore, it has been\n1791\treported that the proportion of viewers who prefer high-speed viewing of dramas ranges\n1792\tfrom 30% to 80%, varying by country [51].\n1793\tHowever, current high-speed playback technologies have fundamental limitations. As\n1794\tdetailed in Chapter 1, the information density of audio content is not constant. While\n1795\tintroductions, repetitions, filler words, and explanations of familiar concepts can be understood at high speed without hindering comprehension, sections with technical termi30\n1796\t\n1797\t\fFigure 3.1: AIxSpeed optimizes the playback speed of a video in units as small as\n1798\tphonemes, maximizing the speed of audio to the extent that humans can hear it while\n1799\tallowing users to watch videos at a comfortable speed without having to adjust the playback speed.\n1800\tnology, complex concepts, or unclear speaker pronunciation require listening at standard\n1801\tspeed or slower. Existing uniform playback speed adjustments cannot accommodate this\n1802\tvariable information density and listening diﬀiculty, resulting in wasting time on easily\n1803\tunderstandable parts and missing content in diﬀicult-to-understand parts.\n1804\tGiven the numerous advantages of high-speed listening to video and audio content, a multitude of methods have been proposed to automatically adjust playback speed according\n1805\tto content structure and user state, aiming to further enhance human high-speed listening capabilities. These methods are extensively studied as video summarization [7] and\n1806\taudio summarization [191]. Existing approaches can be broadly classified into two strategies. The first strategy involves scanning user intentions and behavior to retain only the\n1807\tnecessary parts for viewing or adjusting playback speed proportional to the user’s concentration level [102, 92]. The second strategy accelerates unnecessary parts of the content\n1808\t(i.e., parts without speech) or decelerates parts containing speech [74, 216].\n1809\tHowever, these existing methods face two significant challenges. Firstly, they do not\n1810\texplicitly model whether the resulting speech at different playback speeds remains intelligible to humans, making it diﬀicult to estimate user comprehension across a wide\n1811\trange of speech types. Secondly, these systems vary playback speed for large chunks of\n1812\tspeech, leaving room for adjustment at smaller time units. Fine-grained adjustment at\n1813\tthe phoneme level enables more localized speed control.\n1814\tTo address these challenges, this research proposes the AIxSpeed system. This system\n1815\tadjusts audiovisual output speed while maintaining intelligibility by measuring speech\n1816\tintelligibility after increasing the playback speed. As illustrated in Figure 3.1, AIxSpeed\n1817\tflexibly optimizes video playback speed at the phoneme level. By leveraging the listening\n1818\tability of neural network-based speech recognition models, which are reported to rival\n1819\thuman performance [204], the system simultaneously maximizes video playback speed\n1820\tand speech recognition rate after speed alteration.\n1821\tThis chapter first examines the validity of using speech recognizers as a proxy for evaluating human listening performance through the correlation of changes in human and\n1822\tspeech recognition performance when speed is increased. Subsequently, it compares\n1823\twhether speech with playback speed controlled at the phoneme level, as generated by\n1824\tthe proposed method, or speech played at a constant high speed is easier for humans to\n1825\tcomprehend. Furthermore, the chapter experimentally confirms that AIxSpeed can transform non-native speakers’ utterances into speech that is more comprehensible to native\n1826\tspeakers by optimizing speech rate at the phoneme level.\n1827\tThe primary contributions of this research are threefold:\n1828\t1. Showing that human transcription accuracy and ASR accuracy changed similarly\n1829\t31\n1830\t\n1831\t\facross the tested playback speeds above 1.0x (r = 0.9977), supporting ASR as a\n1832\tproxy under those tested conditions.\n1833\t2. Proposing a method to increase playback speed while maintaining speech intelligibility at the phoneme level. This is achieved through a novel learning framework that leverages a pre-trained speech representation model (Wav2Vec2) but\n1834\tuniquely fine-tunes it with a dual-objective loss function specifically designed\n1835\tfor AIxSpeed. This loss function aims to simultaneously maximize playback\n1836\tspeed (by minimizing Lossspeed ) and preserve the intelligibility of the content (by\n1837\tminimizing Lossctc via the ASR proxy), a distinct approach from standard ASR\n1838\tfine-tuning or simple rule-based speed control. The fine-grained, phoneme-level\n1839\tadjustment guided by this framework is a core innovation.\n1840\t3. Improving the intelligibility of non-native speakers’ speech through phoneme-level\n1841\toptimization of speech rate, potentially opening new applications in language learning and cross-cultural communication.\n1842\tThese contributions enable AIxSpeed not only to support the enhancement of human\n1843\thigh-speed listening ability but also to improve speech comprehension by generating\n1844\tvariable-speed speech that considers the balance between playback speed and speech intelligibility. In particular, by performing optimization based purely on acoustic features\n1845\twithout depending on content, the system achieves high versatility applicable to a wide\n1846\trange of audio content. The subsequent sections of this chapter present a detailed exposition of the AIxSpeed system’s design, implementation, evaluation experiments, and\n1847\tanalysis of results.\n1848\t\n1849\t3.2\n1850\t\n1851\tPilot Survey: Validating ASR as a Proxy for Human Listening\n1852\t\n1853\tThe purpose of this study is to automate the maximization of speed to the extent that\n1854\tspeech is understandable. To achieve this goal, we must validate the hypothesis that as\n1855\tplayback speed increases, the recognition performance of both humans and speech recognizers will decrease in a similar manner. If this hypothesis is correct, we can evaluate\n1856\thow well a human can hear when playback speed is increased using a speech recognizer\n1857\tinstead of a human.\n1858\tSeveral attempts have been made to evaluate human hearing with speech recognizers in\n1859\tthis way. For example, it has been shown that the results of human mean opinion score\n1860\t(MOS) listening tests correlate with the results of the speech recognition-based MOS estimation method introduced in [86], and in [56], the understanding and comprehension\n1861\tscores of a listener with simulated age-related hearing loss were highly correlated with a\n1862\tspeech recognition-based system. A similar hypothesis has been used in speech learning\n1863\tsupport research to evaluate a learner’s speech ability based on speech recognition performance [184]. In other words, if a speech recognizer can recognize speech, it judges\n1864\tthat the person speaks well.\n1865\tHowever, the relationship between the machine learning model and the ability to understand human speech when the playback speed is varied, which is the focus of this study,\n1866\thas not been evaluated. Therefore, we first investigated whether this hypothesis is true.\n1867\tThis study compared human listening performance and speech recognition performance\n1868\tfor speech at 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.25, 1.5, 1.75, and 2.0x playback speeds.\n1869\tTo measure human listening performance, speech data of English sentences were prepared at each playback speed, and the subjects were asked to transcribe the data. The\n1870\ttarget English sentences were selected from LibriSpeech [136], a large English speech\n1871\tcorpus created for the development and evaluation of automatic speech recognition systems. This corpus contains over 1,000 hours of audiobook readings and transcriptions.\n1872\t32\n1873\t\n1874\t\f(a) CER of ML models\n1875\t\n1876\t(b) CER of humans\n1877\t\n1878\t(c) WER of ML models\n1879\t\n1880\t(d) WER of humans\n1881\t\n1882\tFigure 3.2: Comparison of playback speed and listening comprehension in machine\n1883\tlearning (ML)-based speech recognizers and humans, showing transitions in human and\n1884\tspeech recognition model transcription performance at speeds greater than 1x correlated\n1885\tby a coeﬀicient of 0.99.\n1886\tThe participants were 140 English speakers who lived in the United States and had graduated from a US high school. All participants were recruited from the Amazon Mechanical\n1887\tTurk and were compensated for their time. Each subject was given 15 English sentences\n1888\tthat had been randomly sped up by 0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.25, 1.5, 1.75, or 2.0x and asked to\n1889\ttranscribe them.\n1890\tSpeech-recognizer transcriptions were collected by inputting 15 English sentences at\n1891\teach playback speed into a Wav2Vec2-based speech recognition model, similar to the human performance evaluation [13]. Wav2Vec2 is a transformer-based model pre-trained\n1892\tthrough large-scale self-supervised learning, demonstrating state-of-the-art performance\n1893\tin speech recognition tasks.\n1894\tFigure 3.2 shows a graph of the change in listening performance of the human and machine learning models when the playback speed was changed. In these figures, the horizontal axis is the playback speed, and the vertical axis is the recognition performance.\n1895\tRecognition performance was evaluated using character error rate (CER) and word error\n1896\trate (WER), which are commonly used in speech recognition (the lower these values are,\n1897\tthe better).\n1898\tThe WER was calculated as follows:\n1899\t(# of inserted words + # of replaced words + # of deleted words)\n1900\t,\n1901\t(# of correct words)\n1902\t\n1903\tand CER was calculated as:\n1904\t(# of inserted characters + # of replaced characters + # of deleted characters)\n1905\t.\n1906\t(# of correct characters)\n1907\t\n1908\tFor both the human and machine learning models, listening performance decreased as\n1909\tplayback speed increased from 1.0x. For playback speeds greater than 1.0x, the corre33\n1910\t\n1911\t\flation coeﬀicient between the change in listening performance for the human and machine learning models was 0.9977. This extremely high correlation strongly suggests\n1912\tthat speech recognition models can function as a proxy for human listening ability.\n1913\tOn the other hand, when the playback speed was slowed down from 1.0x, the machine\n1914\tlearning model showed a decrease in recognition performance, but the decline was barely\n1915\tobservable for humans. In particular, while the performance of WER decreased slowly,\n1916\tthe performance of CER showed almost no decrease. This indicates that human recognition performance on a character-by-character basis does not drop nearly as much when\n1917\tlistening to slowed speech.\n1918\tThus, when the playback speed increased, the recognition performance of the human and\n1919\tmachine learning models decreased similarly, but they exhibited different behavior when\n1920\tthe playback speed was decreased. However, since this study focuses on increasing the\n1921\tplayback speed of speech, the difference in behavior when the playback speed is slower\n1922\tthan 1.0x has no effect. Therefore, by taking advantage of the fact that listening performance decreases as playback speed is increased for humans and speech recognition\n1923\tmodels alike, we replaced human listening performance with speech recognition performance to develop the desired system.\n1924\t\n1925\t3.3\n1926\t\n1927\tSystem Design\n1928\t\n1929\tThe AIxSpeed system (Figure 3.4) ingeniously combines the power of pre-trained speech\n1930\tmodels with a task-specific optimization strategy. While building upon the robust speech\n1931\trepresentations learned by Wav2Vec2 [13], our primary innovation lies in the architectural design involving a playback speed adjuster and a speech recognizer that share features, and crucially, in the formulation of a composite loss function (Loss = Lossspeed +\n1932\tλLossctc ). This function guides the model to learn optimal phoneme-level speed modulations by explicitly balancing the drive for increased speed with the constraint of maintaining intelligibility as judged by the ASR proxy. This allows AIxSpeed to dynamically\n1933\tadjust speed based on localized acoustic intelligibility, moving beyond the capabilities\n1934\tof conventional playback controls or ASR systems not designed for this specific audio\n1935\ttransformation task. AIxSpeed increases the speed as much as possible, as long as the\n1936\tuser can understand it. This system allows users to watch videos in a time-eﬀicient manner without having to adjust the playback speed for each video. In addition, the system\n1937\tcan automatically improve intelligibility by adjusting the speech speed to accommodate\n1938\tnon-native speakers who are not proficient in the target language.\n1939\tThe working process of AIxSpeed is illustrated in Figure 3.3. The system first extracts\n1940\tthe human voice from the target video. Next, it splits this voice into specified equal\n1941\tintervals. Next, using each segmented voice as input, the system calculates the optimal\n1942\tplayback speed for each segment voice, taking into account the characteristics of the voice\n1943\tas a whole. Finally, the system changes each voice to the specified playback speed and\n1944\tcombines them into a single voice. At the same time, the combined voice is recognized\n1945\tby speech recognition to confirm that the resulting single voice is understandable.\n1946\tThis system consists of two mechanisms, as shown in Figure 3.4. One is a playback\n1947\tspeed adjuster (left), and the other is a speech recognizer (right). The former is used to\n1948\tmaximize the playback speed of the input speech, while the latter is used to evaluate how\n1949\tunderstandable the input speech is. By training these two models simultaneously, it is\n1950\tpossible to generate speech that plays back as fast as possible within the comprehension\n1951\trange.\n1952\t\n1953\t34\n1954\t\n1955\t\fFigure 3.3: The work process of AIxSpeed: extracting human voices from the target\n1956\tvideo, dividing them into specified equal intervals, calculating the optimal playback\n1957\tspeed for each divided voice, changing each voice to the fixed playback speed and synthesizing it into one voice, and performing speech recognition on the synthesized voice\n1958\tto confirm understandability.\n1959\t3.3.1 Playback Speed Adjuster\n1960\tIn this study, we use Wav2Vec2, a self-supervised neural network designed for speech\n1961\tsignal processing systems, to optimize the playback speed. Wav2Vec2 is a model developed by Facebook’s research team that can learn speech representations from large-scale\n1962\tunlabeled speech data. This model can be thought of as a speech version of masked language modeling like BERT, learning by masking parts of the input speech and predicting\n1963\tthe original speech from the remaining parts.\n1964\tThe playback speed controller is divided into a feature extractor layer and a linear layer,\n1965\tas shown in Figure 3.4 (left). They are trained by pre-training through self-supervised\n1966\trepresentation learning on unlabeled speech data and regression learning, which outputs\n1967\t\n1968\t35\n1969\t\n1970\t\fFigure 3.4: AIxSpeed architecture: Our system simultaneously optimizes the playback\n1971\tspeed regulator (left) and the speech recognizer (right) to maximize the playback speed\n1972\tto the extent that the model can recognize.\n1973\tthe playback speed based on the features after the representation learning.\n1974\tThe pre-training method for the feature extractor layer is similar to masked language\n1975\tmodeling, as exemplified by bidirectional encoder representations from transformers\n1976\t(BERT) [47] in natural language processing, where a portion of the input is masked and\n1977\tthe corresponding utterance features are estimated from the remaining input. In this way,\n1978\tthe model can learn good-quality features of the target language to which the rate of\n1979\tutterance should be adapted.\n1980\tSpecifically, Wav2Vec2 has the following architecture:\n1981\t• Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) encoder: Converts raw speech waveforms\n1982\tinto 512-dimensional feature vectors.\n1983\t– Number of layers: 7\n1984\t– Channels per layer: 512\n1985\t– Kernel sizes: {10, 3, 3, 3, 3, 2, 2}\n1986\t– Strides: {5, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2}\n1987\t• Transformer encoder: Processes CNN features through stacked self-attention and\n1988\tfeedforward layers.\n1989\t– Transformer blocks: 12\n1990\t– Hidden dimension: 768\n1991\t– Feed-forward dimension: 3072\n1992\t– Attention heads: 8\n1993\t• Quantization module: Converts continuous features into discrete tokens, enabling\n1994\tmask prediction tasks.\n1995\tTypically, these self-supervised learners are used to tackle tasks such as speech recognition and speaker identification by pre-training and then fine-tuning with a small amount\n1996\t36\n1997\t\n1998\t\fFigure 3.5: Application example showing how the playback speed changed when\n1999\tAIxSpeed was applied to two YouTube videos, demonstrating flexible changes according to the speaker’s speech.\n2000\tof label data. For example, in speech recognition, we have added a projection layer and\n2001\ta connectionist temporal classification (CTC) layer [63] to the output of self-supervised\n2002\tlearners, such as Wav2Vec2 and HuBERT [80], to enable transcription from speech waveforms.\n2003\tSimilar to these methods, we pre-train on unlabeled speech data and then connect and\n2004\ttrain a linear layer that outputs rates. In general speech processing tasks, such as speech\n2005\trecognition [112] and speaker identification [15, 88], the difference from the class label\n2006\tis minimized as an error function. On the other hand, the goal of the playback speed\n2007\tadjuster is to maximize the speed. Therefore, we designed the following function whose\n2008\tvalue decreases as the speed increases.\n2009\t(\n2010\tLossspeed =\n2011\t\n2012\t1\n2013\t10\n2014\t\n2015\t) 1 ∑Ss=1 rs\n2016\tS\n2017\t\n2018\t,\n2019\t\n2020\twhere rs is the playback speed for each segment obtained using Wav2Vec2 and a linear\n2021\tlayer with audio X as input as follows:\n2022\tC = c[1:T ] = Wav2Vec2 (X) ,\n2023\tR = r[1:S] = Linear (C) .\n2024\tThis loss function design trains the model to output as high a playback speed as possible. By using an exponential function, the loss decreases rapidly as speed increases,\n2025\treinforcing the incentive for acceleration.\n2026\t3.3.2 Speech Recognizer\n2027\tThe speech recognizer transcribes the speech converted to the playback speed obtained\n2028\tby the playback speed adjuster (Figure 3.4 (right)). When speech parts with different\n2029\tplayback speeds are combined, noise is generated in the speech data and the sound quality\n2030\tis degraded. We use voice separation technology [121] to extract only the speaker’s voice\n2031\tand reduce the effect of noise.\n2032\tThe resulting speech is then fed into a speech recognizer for speech recognition. The\n2033\tspeech recognition process consists of the extraction of acoustic features from the speech\n2034\t37\n2035\t\n2036\t\fwaveform, estimation of the classes of acoustic features for each frame, and generation\n2037\tof hypotheses from the sequence of class probabilities. Since the speech recognizer can\n2038\tpartially share the neural network with the playback speed adjuster, the overall network\n2039\tsize can be reduced.\n2040\tAs shown in Figure 3.4, the dotted speech feature extractor is shared. The speech features\n2041\tobtained by this mechanism are used as input to generate text in the projection layer. In\n2042\tthis process, the speech recognizer is trained to minimize CTC loss, as in normal speech\n2043\trecognition.\n2044\tThe CTC loss is defined as follows:\n2045\tLossctc = − log P (Y |X)\n2046\twhere Y is the correct character string and X is the input speech. CTC can estimate the\n2047\toptimal character string considering all possible alignments even when the alignment\n2048\tbetween input and output is unknown.\n2049\tIn summary, the entire model is trained to minimize the following error function, which is\n2050\ta combination of this error function and the error function of the playback speed adjuster:\n2051\tLoss = Lossspeed + λLossctc .\n2052\tHere, λ is a hyperparameter that adjusts the importance of the playback speed calculation and speech recognition. By properly setting this hyperparameter, we can balance\n2053\tmaximizing speed and maintaining recognition accuracy.\n2054\t\n2055\t3.4\n2056\t\n2057\tPrototype Implementation\n2058\t\n2059\tAs a prototype of AIxSpeed, an application that optimizes audio playback speed using\n2060\tEnglish as the target language has been implemented. This section describes the implementation and usage of the application.\n2061\t3.4.1 Implementation Details\n2062\tWav2Vec2 was used for the prototype’s shared utterance learning model (the utterance\n2063\tlearning part shared by the speech recognizer and the playback speed adjuster). For pretraining, LibriSpeech [136] was used as the dataset, train-clean-360 as the training data,\n2064\tand dev-clean as the validation data.\n2065\tThe dataset consists of clean speech data in LibriSpeech and was partitioned using the\n2066\tdata partitioning method proposed in LibriSpeech for training/validation. The pre-training\n2067\tdid not require the corresponding transcribed text, only the speech data. This is a major\n2068\tadvantage of self-supervised learning, meaning that large amounts of unlabeled speech\n2069\tdata can be utilized.\n2070\tThe hyperparameters for pre-training are as follows:\n2071\t• Batch size: 32\n2072\t• Learning rate: 5e-5 (warmup steps: 32,000)\n2073\t• Number of epochs: 400,000 steps\n2074\t• Optimizer: Adam (β1 = 0.9, β2 = 0.98, ϵ = 1 × 10−6 )\n2075\t• Mask probability: 0.065\n2076\t• Mask length: 10\n2077\t38\n2078\t\n2079\t\fTable 3.1: Performance comparison of models\n2080\t(a) LibriSpeech\n2081\t\n2082\tModel\n2083\tWav2Vec2 (1.00x)\n2084\tWav2Vec2 (1.30x)\n2085\tWav2Vec2 (1.50x)\n2086\tAIxSpeed\n2087\t\n2088\tAvg. Speed\n2089\t1.00\n2090\t1.30\n2091\t1.50\n2092\t1.30\n2093\t\n2094\tCER\n2095\t4.38\n2096\t5.61\n2097\t6.83\n2098\t5.21\n2099\t\n2100\tWER\n2101\t12.57\n2102\t14.58\n2103\t17.19\n2104\t12.96\n2105\t\n2106\tCER\n2107\t26.74\n2108\t33.71\n2109\t36.93\n2110\t26.45\n2111\t\n2112\tWER\n2113\t55.02\n2114\t63.90\n2115\t66.51\n2116\t53.13\n2117\t\n2118\t(b) UME-ERJ\n2119\t\n2120\tModel\n2121\tWav2Vec2 (1.00x)\n2122\tWav2Vec2 (1.29x)\n2123\tWav2Vec2 (1.50x)\n2124\tAIxSpeed\n2125\t\n2126\tAvg. Speed\n2127\t1.00\n2128\t1.29\n2129\t1.50\n2130\t1.29\n2131\t\n2132\t• Temperature parameter (Gumbel softmax): Linear decay from 2.0 to 0.5\n2133\tWe then trained a speech recognizer and a playback speed adjuster using two sets of\n2134\tspeech data, including the transcribed text. One was LibriSpeech’s train-clean-100, and\n2135\tthe other was the English speech database read by Japanese students (UME-ERJ) [123].\n2136\tThe latter is a dataset of English spoken by non-native Japanese speakers, with 202 speakers (100 males and 102 females) reading simple English sentences.\n2137\tThe playback speed adjuster and the speech recognizer were trained separately. First, the\n2138\tspeech recognizer was trained using LibriSpeech and UME-ERJ, and then the playback\n2139\tspeed adjuster was trained using the same data with fixed weights for the speech recognizer. This staged training allows the playback speed adjuster to optimize speed while\n2140\tmaintaining performance after the speech recognizer achieves stable performance.\n2141\tAll speech files used for training were normalized to a sampling frequency of 16 kHz.\n2142\tThe Wav2Vec2 used the initial parameters implemented in PyTorch [139], and the final\n2143\tlayers of both the playback rate adjuster and the speech recognizer were 768-dimensional\n2144\tlinear layers. The hyperparameter λ for the loss function was set to 10−7 , and the model\n2145\twas trained for 5 epochs with a batch size of 32 using the AdamW [110] optimization\n2146\talgorithm.\n2147\tThe detailed hyperparameters for fine-tuning are as follows:\n2148\t• Learning rate: 1e-4 (speech recognizer), 1e-5 (playback speed adjuster)\n2149\t• Weight decay: 0.01\n2150\t• Dropout rate: 0.1\n2151\t• Gradient clipping: 1.0\n2152\t• Warmup steps: 10% of total steps\n2153\t3.4.2 Usage of the Application\n2154\tExamples of using AIxSpeed are shown in Figure 3.5. This is an example of the prototype\n2155\tapplied to a video uploaded to YouTube. The horizontal axis is the playback time, and the\n2156\tvertical axis represents the playback speed output by the model for each playback time.\n2157\tThe first is an example of speeding up a dialogue, movie, or lecture.\n2158\tTwo speakers appear in the video, and the optimal playback speed can be set for each\n2159\tspeaker. Of particular interest is that the two speakers in the video speak at different\n2160\t39\n2161\t\n2162\t\fspeeds, so the average playback speed for the two speakers is different. It can also be seen\n2163\tthat the playback speed increases drastically from the moment when the dialog between\n2164\tthe two speakers ends and there is no more speech from the person. Although we did\n2165\tnot intend to design this feature, we can see that our system, like conventional playback\n2166\tspeed controllers, can speed up the playback speed in the parts where there is no speech.\n2167\tThe second example applies speed adjustment to non-native speech. The model selected\n2168\ta higher average playback factor than for the native-speaker speech in the first video. This\n2169\texample shows different model-selected speeds, but does not by itself establish improved\n2170\thuman comprehension.\n2171\tAn important feature of this system is its fine-grained playback-speed control at the\n2172\tphoneme level. The experiments evaluated ASR performance and perceived speech quality, but did not separately evaluate the preservation of prosody or intonation.\n2173\t\n2174\t3.5\n2175\t\n2176\tEvaluation\n2177\t\n2178\t3.5.1 Technical Evaluation\n2179\tTo evaluate whether the proposed method can optimize playback speed while maintaining ASR performance, we compared the CER and WER values for AIxSpeed-modified\n2180\tspeech with those for constant-speed playback. The comparison conditions were 1.0x,\n2181\t1.5x, and constant playback matched to AIxSpeed’s average playback factor.\n2182\tA standard Wav2Vec2-based speech recognition model, which was the speech recognizer\n2183\tused in our method, was used to compute the CER and WER for comparison. The model\n2184\tperformance is shown in Table 3.1 (a) and (b) for LibriSpeech and UME-ERJ, respectively.\n2185\tAIxSpeed produces speech 1.30 times faster on average for LibriSpeech and 1.29 times\n2186\tfaster on average for UME-ERJ. Both results show that the playback speed optimized\n2187\tby AIxSpeed has lower values for both CER and WER than constant-speed playback\n2188\tmatched to the same average factor. Thus, the proposed method retained better ASR\n2189\tperformance than the matched constant-speed baseline.\n2190\tIn addition, for UME-ERJ, speech generated by AIxSpeed had a lower WER than speech\n2191\tat 1.0x playback. This indicates that it was more recognizable to the ASR model used\n2192\tfor evaluation; it does not directly establish improved intelligibility or comprehension for\n2193\thuman listeners, which requires a dedicated human study.\n2194\t3.5.2 User Evaluation\n2195\tUser experiments were conducted to evaluate the perceived quality of the generated\n2196\tspeech. Speech generated by the proposed method was compared with speech played\n2197\tat a constant speed matched to the same average playback factor. Quality was evaluated\n2198\tusing the mean opinion score (MOS), which is commonly used in speech synthesis research [190, 198]. This measure rates speech quality on a five-point scale from 1 (poor)\n2199\tto 5 (excellent).\n2200\tParticipants were 50 US residents who used English on a daily basis. 20 sentences were\n2201\textracted from each of the LibriSpeech and UME-ERJ datasets, and half were converted\n2202\tto speech with the proposed speed-up, while the other half were converted to speech with\n2203\ta constant speed-up. Participants were given a total of 40 sentences of audio and asked\n2204\tto rate the quality of the audio.\n2205\t• The source of the audio (AIxSpeed or baseline) was not disclosed to participants.\n2206\tFigure 3.6 shows the perceived quality of speech at baseline and with AIxSpeed. The\n2207\tmean opinion scores for LibriSpeech and UME-ERJ were 0.5 and 0.8 points higher with\n2208\t40\n2209\t\n2210\t\fFigure 3.6: Voice quality comparison between baseline (constant playback speed increase\n2211\tthroughout) and AIxSpeed (flexible playback speed increase).\n2212\tthe proposed method, respectively. The source paper did not report a significance test for\n2213\tthese differences.\n2214\tA larger MOS difference was observed for UME-ERJ than for LibriSpeech, but further\n2215\tevaluation is needed to determine whether AIxSpeed is especially effective for non-nativespeaker speech.\n2216\t\n2217\t3.6\n2218\t\n2219\tDiscussion\n2220\t\n2221\t3.6.1 Evaluation Results and Implications\n2222\tThe technical evaluation shows that the proposed method produces lower CER and WER\n2223\tthan constant-speed speech at the same average playback factor. The user evaluation\n2224\tlikewise shows higher mean opinion scores for AIxSpeed speech than for the matched\n2225\tconstant-speed baseline. This average speed-up of approximately 1.3x (e.g., reducing a\n2226\t60-minute lecture to about 46 minutes) represents a tangible time saving for learners.\n2227\tThis allows users to watch videos at a reasonable speed without having to adjust the\n2228\tplayback speed for each video. However, the improvement in MOS values by using the\n2229\tproposed method is by no means suﬀicient. In the current model, the average conversion\n2230\tto a faster playback speed is about 1.3 times, but it is a future task to investigate whether\n2231\tit is possible to make this even faster.\n2232\tIn fact, many video playback services implement 1.5x and 2.0x playback speeds, and\n2233\tsome people watch dramas and lectures at such speeds. Therefore, we expect that it will\n2234\tbe possible to convert up to this speed and make the audio easy to understand. In addition,\n2235\tsince each user has a different preferred playback speed, personalizing the model so that\n2236\tit plays at the optimal playback speed for users is also a future issue.\n2237\t\n2238\t41\n2239\t\n2240\t\f3.6.2 Limitations and Future Work\n2241\tOne key aspect of AIxSpeed is its content-agnostic approach, relying purely on acoustic\n2242\tfeatures to determine playback speed. While this allows for broad applicability across\n2243\tlanguages and topics without needing semantic understanding, it also presents certain\n2244\tlimitations, particularly in educational contexts. For instance, the system does not currently consider the semantic importance of content segments or a speaker’s pedagogical\n2245\tintent. A lecturer might intentionally slow down their speech to emphasize a critical point\n2246\tor explain a complex idea, even if their pronunciation is perfectly clear. In such cases,\n2247\tAIxSpeed, prioritizing acoustic intelligibility as determined by the ASR proxy, might\n2248\tinappropriately speed up these segments, potentially hindering deep comprehension or\n2249\tmissing pedagogical cues. Future research could explore integrating natural language\n2250\tprocessing techniques to identify semantically important segments or to understand discourse structures (e.g., cues for emphasis or topic shifts). This would allow for a more\n2251\tnuanced speed adjustment that balances acoustic intelligibility with the preservation of\n2252\tpedagogically salient prosodic variations. Furthermore, adapting the system to accommodate intentional pauses or variations in speech rhythm that contribute to the speaker’s\n2253\tstyle or the listener’s engagement, rather than solely optimizing for raw speed based on\n2254\tASR confidence, remains an avenue for future enhancement.\n2255\tThe personalization mentioned in Section 3.6.1 could involve allowing users, or an overarching AI-Guided Learning system, to set a target intelligibility level (e.g., by adjusting the ASR confidence threshold that AIxSpeed uses as a proxy for human listening\n2256\tease). For example, advanced learners or those familiar with the content might opt for\n2257\ta slightly lower intelligibility threshold to maximize speed further, while beginners or\n2258\tthose encountering complex material might prefer a higher threshold to ensure thorough\n2259\tunderstanding. This would cater to the diverse needs, prior knowledge, and capabilities\n2260\tof learners, addressing the potential gap between a generic optimization and individual\n2261\tlearner preferences.\n2262\tWhile the current model achieves a commendable average speed-up of approximately\n2263\t1.3x while improving or maintaining intelligibility compared to constant speed-up, future iterations could explore architectural or loss function modifications to safely push\n2264\ttowards higher average speeds (e.g., 1.5x to 2.0x), which are common user-selectable options in many commercial players. Investigating the system’s behavior and the perceived\n2265\tintelligibility limits at even more extreme speeds (e.g., up to 4x for highly redundant or\n2266\texceptionally clear speech segments) could reveal further optimization potential or define\n2267\tan ”intelligibility cliff” for adaptive speed methods. However, achieving high intelligibility at such significantly accelerated rates for general audio content would likely require\n2268\tsubstantial advancements in both the speed modification algorithm and the underlying\n2269\tintelligibility prediction model.\n2270\tWhile AIxSpeed demonstrated effectiveness on English speech (both native and nonnative), its performance across a wider range of languages, accents, and acoustic environments (e.g., noisy conditions, overlapping speech) requires further investigation to\n2271\tfully substantiate its claimed versatility. The current ASR proxy model is trained primarily on English, and its reliability as an intelligibility measure for other languages would\n2272\tneed careful validation or language-specific adaptation.\n2273\t3.6.3 Listening Comprehension and Speech Recognition\n2274\tAs discussed in the preliminary research chapter (Section 3.2), it can be seen that there is\n2275\ta relationship between speech recognition performance and human transcription ability.\n2276\tThus, we expect to build automated systems for various tasks and evaluations by replacing\n2277\thuman speech comprehension ability with machine learning models, as in this system.\n2278\t\n2279\t42\n2280\t\n2281\t\fOn the other hand, speech recognition performance based on playback speed does not\n2282\tperfectly match human speech comprehension. In other words, as the playback speed\n2283\tincreases, the dictation performance decreases in both cases, but the performance values\n2284\tare not exactly the same. Thus, we anticipate that by training speech recognition models\n2285\tto match these relationships as closely as possible, it will be possible to use them more\n2286\tgenerally as alternatives to humans.\n2287\tDistillation, a technique that learns to approximate an output that matches existing results,\n2288\twill be the technical key. Specifically, by using human listening data as a “teacher” and\n2289\ttraining speech recognition models to show similar performance degradation patterns, it\n2290\tmay be possible to build more accurate proxy models of human listening ability.\n2291\t3.6.4 Adjustment of Non-Native Speakers’ Speech\n2292\tThe UME-ERJ ASR result suggests that moderate acceleration of non-native speech may\n2293\tbe useful under some conditions. Because this study did not measure human intelligibility or comprehension for that comparison, this remains a hypothesis for future evaluation.\n2294\tOne motivation for this study was an informal observation that accelerated playback of\n2295\tsome non-native speech could sound easier to follow. This observation is not treated as\n2296\tempirical evidence in the present study.\n2297\t3.6.5 Potential Applications Beyond Speed Optimization and Societal Implementation\n2298\tThe technology of AIxSpeed has various application possibilities beyond simple highspeed playback. Particularly noteworthy is its application to hearing aids for the hearing\n2299\timpaired. By utilizing the technology for evaluating listening diﬀiculty at the phoneme\n2300\tlevel, it is thought that it will be possible to develop adaptive hearing aids that automatically play diﬀicult-to-hear parts slowly.\n2301\tSuch hearing aids could dynamically adjust playback speed according to environmental\n2302\tsounds and speaker characteristics. This is a possible research direction, not an evaluated\n2303\tapplication of the present study: studies with hearing-impaired users and noisy speech\n2304\twould be needed to determine whether it improves listening or comprehension.\n2305\tFurthermore, applications to language learning can be expected. By automatically playing parts containing diﬀicult phonemes or words slowly according to the learner’s level,\n2306\tmore effective listening practice becomes possible. It may also be used in presentation\n2307\tpractice or lecture preparation, where speakers can objectively evaluate and improve the\n2308\tlistening diﬀiculty of their own utterances.\n2309\tFurthermore, when compared to standard playback speed controls found in many video/audio\n2310\tplatforms (e.g., YouTube, podcast players), AIxSpeed offers a significant advantage through\n2311\tits adaptive and fine-grained (phoneme-level) speed adjustments. Instead of a uniform speed-up that can make diﬀicult passages unintelligible or slow passages tedious,\n2312\tAIxSpeed optimizes locally, aiming to maximize speed only where content remains clear,\n2313\tthus providing a more consistently comfortable and eﬀicient listening experience. This\n2314\tcontrasts with comprehensive language learning platforms like Duolingo, which focus\n2315\ton interactive exercises and curriculum, whereas AIxSpeed enhances the consumption\n2316\tof existing, often passive, audio learning materials.\n2317\tRegarding broader societal implementation, AIxSpeed could be integrated as an advanced\n2318\tfeature within existing Learning Management Systems (LMS), as a plug-in for popular\n2319\tvideo and audio streaming platforms, or even as a browser extension enhancing webbased audio content. Key technical challenges for widespread adoption include ensuring\n2320\treal-time or near real-time processing capabilities for on-the-fly adjustments, optimizing\n2321\tcomputational eﬀiciency for deployment on diverse end-user devices (including mobile),\n2322\t43\n2323\t\n2324\t\fand developing intuitive user interfaces that provide appropriate control and transparency\n2325\tover the automated speed adjustments. Furthermore, practical deployment would necessitate considerations regarding the processing of copyrighted material and ensuring user\n2326\tdata privacy, especially if personalization features are implemented. Pilot studies in authentic educational settings would be crucial to gather user feedback on usability and\n2327\tperceived learning benefits, and to iteratively refine the system for robust, real-world application.\n2328\t\n2329\t3.7\n2330\t\n2331\tConclusion\n2332\t\n2333\tThis chapter has presented AIxSpeed, a system that addresses rapid listening to audio\n2334\tcontent in the Consume stage of the AI-Guided Learning framework. AIxSpeed automatically adjusts playback speed at the phoneme level using ASR performance as a proxy\n2335\tderived from the pilot comparison with human transcription performance.\n2336\tThe key contributions of AIxSpeed are threefold. Firstly, the pilot study found a correlation of 0.9977 between changes in human transcription accuracy and ASR accuracy at\n2337\ttested playback speeds above 1.0x. Secondly, AIxSpeed introduces phoneme-level speed\n2338\tadjustment and achieved lower CER and WER than constant-speed playback matched to\n2339\tthe same average factor. Thirdly, the UME-ERJ evaluation found lower WER than at 1.0x\n2340\tplayback; this is an ASR result and does not establish improved human intelligibility for\n2341\tnon-native speech.\n2342\tThe experimental results presented in this chapter provide evidence for the eﬀicacy of\n2343\tAIxSpeed. At its average playback factors (1.30x for LibriSpeech and 1.29x for UMEERJ), the system produced lower Character Error Rate (CER) and Word Error Rate\n2344\t(WER) than constant-speed playback at the same factors. In the user study, AIxSpeed\n2345\tspeech also received higher mean opinion scores than the matched constant-speed baseline. These findings indicate the system’s potential to enhance the eﬀiciency of audio\n2346\tcontent consumption while maintaining intelligibility.\n2347\tDespite these promising results, it is important to acknowledge the limitations of the\n2348\tcurrent implementation of AIxSpeed. The system’s performance may vary depending on\n2349\tfactors such as the acoustic characteristics of the input speech, the diversity of speakers,\n2350\tand the complexity of the content. Further research is needed to assess and optimize\n2351\tthe system’s performance across a wider range of speech types and listening conditions.\n2352\tAdditionally, the long-term effects of using AIxSpeed on listeners’ cognitive load and\n2353\tinformation retention need to be thoroughly investigated.\n2354\tLooking ahead, AIxSpeed holds considerable promise for a variety of applications. In the\n2355\tcontext of video distribution services, it could enhance user experience by providing personalized playback speeds that maximize comprehension and engagement. In language\n2356\tlearning scenarios, AIxSpeed could be integrated into tools that help learners adapt to different speech rates and accents, potentially accelerating the acquisition of listening skills.\n2357\tFurthermore, the system’s ability to improve the intelligibility of non-native speech could\n2358\thave significant implications for international communication and collaboration in educational and professional settings.\n2359\tOne possible future direction is adaptive playback for hearing-impaired users. A system\n2360\tcould adjust playback speed according to the environment and speaker, but AIxSpeed was\n2361\tnot evaluated with hearing-impaired users or as a hearing aid. Whether such adaptation\n2362\timproves accessibility requires dedicated clinical and usability evaluation.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6af2b770-4e64-4fd8-b01f-b5667819ace1","uuid":"8367e75f-cc7b-44bd-ae8b-f00966155aaa","timestamp":"2026-08-11T17:23:12.596Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/full.txt","content":"\fChapter 3\nAIxSpeed: Playback Speed Optimization Using\nListening Comprehension of Speech Recognition\nModels\n3.1\n\nIntroduction\n\nThis chapter presents the AIxSpeed system, specifically designed to address a key challenge within the broader AI-Guided Learning framework: the time-ineﬀicient consumption of audio content during the Consume-Understand learning stages. As discussed\nin Chapter 1, modern learners often face the necessity of eﬀiciently acquiring knowledge from extensive long-form audio materials such as podcasts, audiobooks, and lecture recordings. This research focuses on optimizing the auditory learning experience\nby proposing an innovative method for adaptive playback speed control at the phoneme\nlevel, guided by a speech recognition model’s listening comprehension. However, due\nto the linear nature and variable information density of these contents, uniform playback speed cannot achieve optimal learning eﬀiciency, potentially leading to decreased\nattention, increased cognitive load from processing overly dense or unnecessarily slow\ninformation, and ultimately, suboptimal learning outcomes. AIxSpeed presents an innovative approach of adaptive playback speed control at the phoneme level to address this\nchallenge.\nThe core innovation of AIxSpeed lies in its ability to determine optimal playback speed\nfor each segment based purely on acoustic features without requiring semantic understanding of the educational content. This is achieved by leveraging speech recognition\nmodels’ confidence scores as a proxy for human listening ease. This content-agnostic approach enables the construction of a versatile system applicable to a wide range of audio\ncontent regardless of language or topic.\nWith the proliferation of video distribution services, opportunities for watching videos\nfor various purposes, including information gathering, learning, and entertainment, have\nsignificantly increased. Humans possess the ability to comprehend naturally observed\nphenomena at rates faster than their original occurrence. Consequently, it has become\ncommon practice to increase the playback speed of videos and audio to understand more\ncontent in a shorter period. Research has demonstrated that, under specific conditions,\ndifferences in video playback speed do not affect learning effectiveness in educational\nvideo viewing and may even enhance performance [32, 103]. Furthermore, it has been\nreported that the proportion of viewers who prefer high-speed viewing of dramas ranges\nfrom 30% to 80%, varying by country [51].\nHowever, current high-speed playback technologies have fundamental limitations. As\ndetailed in Chapter 1, the information density of audio content is not constant. While\nintroductions, repetitions, filler words, and explanations of familiar concepts can be understood at high speed without hindering comprehension, sections with technical termi30\n\n\fFigure 3.1: AIxSpeed optimizes the playback speed of a video in units as small as\nphonemes, maximizing the speed of audio to the extent that humans can hear it while\nallowing users to watch videos at a comfortable speed without having to adjust the playback speed.\nnology, complex concepts, or unclear speaker pronunciation require listening at standard\nspeed or slower. Existing uniform playback speed adjustments cannot accommodate this\nvariable information density and listening diﬀiculty, resulting in wasting time on easily\nunderstandable parts and missing content in diﬀicult-to-understand parts.\nGiven the numerous advantages of high-speed listening to video and audio content, a multitude of methods have been proposed to automatically adjust playback speed according\nto content structure and user state, aiming to further enhance human high-speed listening capabilities. These methods are extensively studied as video summarization [7] and\naudio summarization [191]. Existing approaches can be broadly classified into two strategies. The first strategy involves scanning user intentions and behavior to retain only the\nnecessary parts for viewing or adjusting playback speed proportional to the user’s concentration level [102, 92]. The second strategy accelerates unnecessary parts of the content\n(i.e., parts without speech) or decelerates parts containing speech [74, 216].\nHowever, these existing methods face two significant challenges. Firstly, they do not\nexplicitly model whether the resulting speech at different playback speeds remains intelligible to humans, making it diﬀicult to estimate user comprehension across a wide\nrange of speech types. Secondly, these systems vary playback speed for large chunks of\nspeech, leaving room for adjustment at smaller time units. Fine-grained adjustment at\nthe phoneme level enables more localized speed control.\nTo address these challenges, this research proposes the AIxSpeed system. This system\nadjusts audiovisual output speed while maintaining intelligibility by measuring speech\nintelligibility after increasing the playback speed. As illustrated in Figure 3.1, AIxSpeed\nflexibly optimizes video playback speed at the phoneme level. By leveraging the listening\nability of neural network-based speech recognition models, which are reported to rival\nhuman performance [204], the system simultaneously maximizes video playback speed\nand speech recognition rate after speed alteration.\nThis chapter first examines the validity of using speech recognizers as a proxy for evaluating human listening performance through the correlation of changes in human and\nspeech recognition performance when speed is increased. Subsequently, it compares\nwhether speech with playback speed controlled at the phoneme level, as generated by\nthe proposed method, or speech played at a constant high speed is easier for humans to\ncomprehend. Furthermore, the chapter experimentally confirms that AIxSpeed can transform non-native speakers’ utterances into speech that is more comprehensible to native\nspeakers by optimizing speech rate at the phoneme level.\nThe primary contributions of this research are threefold:\n1. Showing that human transcription accuracy and ASR accuracy changed similarly\n31\n\n\facross the tested playback speeds above 1.0x (r = 0.9977), supporting ASR as a\nproxy under those tested conditions.\n2. Proposing a method to increase playback speed while maintaining speech intelligibility at the phoneme level. This is achieved through a novel learning framework that leverages a pre-trained speech representation model (Wav2Vec2) but\nuniquely fine-tunes it with a dual-objective loss function specifically designed\nfor AIxSpeed. This loss function aims to simultaneously maximize playback\nspeed (by minimizing Lossspeed ) and preserve the intelligibility of the content (by\nminimizing Lossctc via the ASR proxy), a distinct approach from standard ASR\nfine-tuning or simple rule-based speed control. The fine-grained, phoneme-level\nadjustment guided by this framework is a core innovation.\n3. Improving the intelligibility of non-native speakers’ speech through phoneme-level\noptimization of speech rate, potentially opening new applications in language learning and cross-cultural communication.\nThese contributions enable AIxSpeed not only to support the enhancement of human\nhigh-speed listening ability but also to improve speech comprehension by generating\nvariable-speed speech that considers the balance between playback speed and speech intelligibility. In particular, by performing optimization based purely on acoustic features\nwithout depending on content, the system achieves high versatility applicable to a wide\nrange of audio content. The subsequent sections of this chapter present a detailed exposition of the AIxSpeed system’s design, implementation, evaluation experiments, and\nanalysis of results.\n\n3.2\n\nPilot Survey: Validating ASR as a Proxy for Human Listening\n\nThe purpose of this study is to automate the maximization of speed to the extent that\nspeech is understandable. To achieve this goal, we must validate the hypothesis that as\nplayback speed increases, the recognition performance of both humans and speech recognizers will decrease in a similar manner. If this hypothesis is correct, we can evaluate\nhow well a human can hear when playback speed is increased using a speech recognizer\ninstead of a human.\nSeveral attempts have been made to evaluate human hearing with speech recognizers in\nthis way. For example, it has been shown that the results of human mean opinion score\n(MOS) listening tests correlate with the results of the speech recognition-based MOS estimation method introduced in [86], and in [56], the understanding and comprehension\nscores of a listener with simulated age-related hearing loss were highly correlated with a\nspeech recognition-based system. A similar hypothesis has been used in speech learning\nsupport research to evaluate a learner’s speech ability based on speech recognition performance [184]. In other words, if a speech recognizer can recognize speech, it judges\nthat the person speaks well.\nHowever, the relationship between the machine learning model and the ability to understand human speech when the playback speed is varied, which is the focus of this study,\nhas not been evaluated. Therefore, we first investigated whether this hypothesis is true.\nThis study compared human listening performance and speech recognition performance\nfor speech at 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.25, 1.5, 1.75, and 2.0x playback speeds.\nTo measure human listening performance, speech data of English sentences were prepared at each playback speed, and the subjects were asked to transcribe the data. The\ntarget English sentences were selected from LibriSpeech [136], a large English speech\ncorpus created for the development and evaluation of automatic speech recognition systems. This corpus contains over 1,000 hours of audiobook readings and transcriptions.\n32\n\n\f(a) CER of ML models\n\n(b) CER of humans\n\n(c) WER of ML models\n\n(d) WER of humans\n\nFigure 3.2: Comparison of playback speed and listening comprehension in machine\nlearning (ML)-based speech recognizers and humans, showing transitions in human and\nspeech recognition model transcription performance at speeds greater than 1x correlated\nby a coeﬀicient of 0.99.\nThe participants were 140 English speakers who lived in the United States and had graduated from a US high school. All participants were recruited from the Amazon Mechanical\nTurk and were compensated for their time. Each subject was given 15 English sentences\nthat had been randomly sped up by 0.5, 0.75, 1.0, 1.25, 1.5, 1.75, or 2.0x and asked to\ntranscribe them.\nSpeech-recognizer transcriptions were collected by inputting 15 English sentences at\neach playback speed into a Wav2Vec2-based speech recognition model, similar to the human performance evaluation [13]. Wav2Vec2 is a transformer-based model pre-trained\nthrough large-scale self-supervised learning, demonstrating state-of-the-art performance\nin speech recognition tasks.\nFigure 3.2 shows a graph of the change in listening performance of the human and machine learning models when the playback speed was changed. In these figures, the horizontal axis is the playback speed, and the vertical axis is the recognition performance.\nRecognition performance was evaluated using character error rate (CER) and word error\nrate (WER), which are commonly used in speech recognition (the lower these values are,\nthe better).\nThe WER was calculated as follows:\n(# of inserted words + # of replaced words + # of deleted words)\n,\n(# of correct words)\n\nand CER was calculated as:\n(# of inserted characters + # of replaced characters + # of deleted characters)\n.\n(# of correct characters)\n\nFor both the human and machine learning models, listening performance decreased as\nplayback speed increased from 1.0x. For playback speeds greater than 1.0x, the corre33\n\n\flation coeﬀicient between the change in listening performance for the human and machine learning models was 0.9977. This extremely high correlation strongly suggests\nthat speech recognition models can function as a proxy for human listening ability.\nOn the other hand, when the playback speed was slowed down from 1.0x, the machine\nlearning model showed a decrease in recognition performance, but the decline was barely\nobservable for humans. In particular, while the performance of WER decreased slowly,\nthe performance of CER showed almost no decrease. This indicates that human recognition performance on a character-by-character basis does not drop nearly as much when\nlistening to slowed speech.\nThus, when the playback speed increased, the recognition performance of the human and\nmachine learning models decreased similarly, but they exhibited different behavior when\nthe playback speed was decreased. However, since this study focuses on increasing the\nplayback speed of speech, the difference in behavior when the playback speed is slower\nthan 1.0x has no effect. Therefore, by taking advantage of the fact that listening performance decreases as playback speed is increased for humans and speech recognition\nmodels alike, we replaced human listening performance with speech recognition performance to develop the desired system.\n\n3.3\n\nSystem Design\n\nThe AIxSpeed system (Figure 3.4) ingeniously combines the power of pre-trained speech\nmodels with a task-specific optimization strategy. While building upon the robust speech\nrepresentations learned by Wav2Vec2 [13], our primary innovation lies in the architectural design involving a playback speed adjuster and a speech recognizer that share features, and crucially, in the formulation of a composite loss function (Loss = Lossspeed +\nλLossctc ). This function guides the model to learn optimal phoneme-level speed modulations by explicitly balancing the drive for increased speed with the constraint of maintaining intelligibility as judged by the ASR proxy. This allows AIxSpeed to dynamically\nadjust speed based on localized acoustic intelligibility, moving beyond the capabilities\nof conventional playback controls or ASR systems not designed for this specific audio\ntransformation task. AIxSpeed increases the speed as much as possible, as long as the\nuser can understand it. This system allows users to watch videos in a time-eﬀicient manner without having to adjust the playback speed for each video. In addition, the system\ncan automatically improve intelligibility by adjusting the speech speed to accommodate\nnon-native speakers who are not proficient in the target language.\nThe working process of AIxSpeed is illustrated in Figure 3.3. The system first extracts\nthe human voice from the target video. Next, it splits this voice into specified equal\nintervals. Next, using each segmented voice as input, the system calculates the optimal\nplayback speed for each segment voice, taking into account the characteristics of the voice\nas a whole. Finally, the system changes each voice to the specified playback speed and\ncombines them into a single voice. At the same time, the combined voice is recognized\nby speech recognition to confirm that the resulting single voice is understandable.\nThis system consists of two mechanisms, as shown in Figure 3.4. One is a playback\nspeed adjuster (left), and the other is a speech recognizer (right). The former is used to\nmaximize the playback speed of the input speech, while the latter is used to evaluate how\nunderstandable the input speech is. By training these two models simultaneously, it is\npossible to generate speech that plays back as fast as possible within the comprehension\nrange.\n\n34\n\n\fFigure 3.3: The work process of AIxSpeed: extracting human voices from the target\nvideo, dividing them into specified equal intervals, calculating the optimal playback\nspeed for each divided voice, changing each voice to the fixed playback speed and synthesizing it into one voice, and performing speech recognition on the synthesized voice\nto confirm understandability.\n3.3.1 Playback Speed Adjuster\nIn this study, we use Wav2Vec2, a self-supervised neural network designed for speech\nsignal processing systems, to optimize the playback speed. Wav2Vec2 is a model developed by Facebook’s research team that can learn speech representations from large-scale\nunlabeled speech data. This model can be thought of as a speech version of masked language modeling like BERT, learning by masking parts of the input speech and predicting\nthe original speech from the remaining parts.\nThe playback speed controller is divided into a feature extractor layer and a linear layer,\nas shown in Figure 3.4 (left). They are trained by pre-training through self-supervised\nrepresentation learning on unlabeled speech data and regression learning, which outputs\n\n35\n\n\fFigure 3.4: AIxSpeed architecture: Our system simultaneously optimizes the playback\nspeed regulator (left) and the speech recognizer (right) to maximize the playback speed\nto the extent that the model can recognize.\nthe playback speed based on the features after the representation learning.\nThe pre-training method for the feature extractor layer is similar to masked language\nmodeling, as exemplified by bidirectional encoder representations from transformers\n(BERT) [47] in natural language processing, where a portion of the input is masked and\nthe corresponding utterance features are estimated from the remaining input. In this way,\nthe model can learn good-quality features of the target language to which the rate of\nutterance should be adapted.\nSpecifically, Wav2Vec2 has the following architecture:\n• Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) encoder: Converts raw speech waveforms\ninto 512-dimensional feature vectors.\n– Number of layers: 7\n– Channels per layer: 512\n– Kernel sizes: {10, 3, 3, 3, 3, 2, 2}\n– Strides: {5, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2}\n• Transformer encoder: Processes CNN features through stacked self-attention and\nfeedforward layers.\n– Transformer blocks: 12\n– Hidden dimension: 768\n– Feed-forward dimension: 3072\n– Attention heads: 8\n• Quantization module: Converts continuous features into discrete tokens, enabling\nmask prediction tasks.\nTypically, these self-supervised learners are used to tackle tasks such as speech recognition and speaker identification by pre-training and then fine-tuning with a small amount\n36\n\n\fFigure 3.5: Application example showing how the playback speed changed when\nAIxSpeed was applied to two YouTube videos, demonstrating flexible changes according to the speaker’s speech.\nof label data. For example, in speech recognition, we have added a projection layer and\na connectionist temporal classification (CTC) layer [63] to the output of self-supervised\nlearners, such as Wav2Vec2 and HuBERT [80], to enable transcription from speech waveforms.\nSimilar to these methods, we pre-train on unlabeled speech data and then connect and\ntrain a linear layer that outputs rates. In general speech processing tasks, such as speech\nrecognition [112] and speaker identification [15, 88], the difference from the class label\nis minimized as an error function. On the other hand, the goal of the playback speed\nadjuster is to maximize the speed. Therefore, we designed the following function whose\nvalue decreases as the speed increases.\n(\nLossspeed =\n\n1\n10\n\n) 1 ∑Ss=1 rs\nS\n\n,\n\nwhere rs is the playback speed for each segment obtained using Wav2Vec2 and a linear\nlayer with audio X as input as follows:\nC = c[1:T ] = Wav2Vec2 (X) ,\nR = r[1:S] = Linear (C) .\nThis loss function design trains the model to output as high a playback speed as possible. By using an exponential function, the loss decreases rapidly as speed increases,\nreinforcing the incentive for acceleration.\n3.3.2 Speech Recognizer\nThe speech recognizer transcribes the speech converted to the playback speed obtained\nby the playback speed adjuster (Figure 3.4 (right)). When speech parts with different\nplayback speeds are combined, noise is generated in the speech data and the sound quality\nis degraded. We use voice separation technology [121] to extract only the speaker’s voice\nand reduce the effect of noise.\nThe resulting speech is then fed into a speech recognizer for speech recognition. The\nspeech recognition process consists of the extraction of acoustic features from the speech\n37\n\n\fwaveform, estimation of the classes of acoustic features for each frame, and generation\nof hypotheses from the sequence of class probabilities. Since the speech recognizer can\npartially share the neural network with the playback speed adjuster, the overall network\nsize can be reduced.\nAs shown in Figure 3.4, the dotted speech feature extractor is shared. The speech features\nobtained by this mechanism are used as input to generate text in the projection layer. In\nthis process, the speech recognizer is trained to minimize CTC loss, as in normal speech\nrecognition.\nThe CTC loss is defined as follows:\nLossctc = − log P (Y |X)\nwhere Y is the correct character string and X is the input speech. CTC can estimate the\noptimal character string considering all possible alignments even when the alignment\nbetween input and output is unknown.\nIn summary, the entire model is trained to minimize the following error function, which is\na combination of this error function and the error function of the playback speed adjuster:\nLoss = Lossspeed + λLossctc .\nHere, λ is a hyperparameter that adjusts the importance of the playback speed calculation and speech recognition. By properly setting this hyperparameter, we can balance\nmaximizing speed and maintaining recognition accuracy.\n\n3.4\n\nPrototype Implementation\n\nAs a prototype of AIxSpeed, an application that optimizes audio playback speed using\nEnglish as the target language has been implemented. This section describes the implementation and usage of the application.\n3.4.1 Implementation Details\nWav2Vec2 was used for the prototype’s shared utterance learning model (the utterance\nlearning part shared by the speech recognizer and the playback speed adjuster). For pretraining, LibriSpeech [136] was used as the dataset, train-clean-360 as the training data,\nand dev-clean as the validation data.\nThe dataset consists of clean speech data in LibriSpeech and was partitioned using the\ndata partitioning method proposed in LibriSpeech for training/validation. The pre-training\ndid not require the corresponding transcribed text, only the speech data. This is a major\nadvantage of self-supervised learning, meaning that large amounts of unlabeled speech\ndata can be utilized.\nThe hyperparameters for pre-training are as follows:\n• Batch size: 32\n• Learning rate: 5e-5 (warmup steps: 32,000)\n• Number of epochs: 400,000 steps\n• Optimizer: Adam (β1 = 0.9, β2 = 0.98, ϵ = 1 × 10−6 )\n• Mask probability: 0.065\n• Mask length: 10\n38\n\n\fTable 3.1: Performance comparison of models\n(a) LibriSpeech\n\nModel\nWav2Vec2 (1.00x)\nWav2Vec2 (1.30x)\nWav2Vec2 (1.50x)\nAIxSpeed\n\nAvg. Speed\n1.00\n1.30\n1.50\n1.30\n\nCER\n4.38\n5.61\n6.83\n5.21\n\nWER\n12.57\n14.58\n17.19\n12.96\n\nCER\n26.74\n33.71\n36.93\n26.45\n\nWER\n55.02\n63.90\n66.51\n53.13\n\n(b) UME-ERJ\n\nModel\nWav2Vec2 (1.00x)\nWav2Vec2 (1.29x)\nWav2Vec2 (1.50x)\nAIxSpeed\n\nAvg. Speed\n1.00\n1.29\n1.50\n1.29\n\n• Temperature parameter (Gumbel softmax): Linear decay from 2.0 to 0.5\nWe then trained a speech recognizer and a playback speed adjuster using two sets of\nspeech data, including the transcribed text. One was LibriSpeech’s train-clean-100, and\nthe other was the English speech database read by Japanese students (UME-ERJ) [123].\nThe latter is a dataset of English spoken by non-native Japanese speakers, with 202 speakers (100 males and 102 females) reading simple English sentences.\nThe playback speed adjuster and the speech recognizer were trained separately. First, the\nspeech recognizer was trained using LibriSpeech and UME-ERJ, and then the playback\nspeed adjuster was trained using the same data with fixed weights for the speech recognizer. This staged training allows the playback speed adjuster to optimize speed while\nmaintaining performance after the speech recognizer achieves stable performance.\nAll speech files used for training were normalized to a sampling frequency of 16 kHz.\nThe Wav2Vec2 used the initial parameters implemented in PyTorch [139], and the final\nlayers of both the playback rate adjuster and the speech recognizer were 768-dimensional\nlinear layers. The hyperparameter λ for the loss function was set to 10−7 , and the model\nwas trained for 5 epochs with a batch size of 32 using the AdamW [110] optimization\nalgorithm.\nThe detailed hyperparameters for fine-tuning are as follows:\n• Learning rate: 1e-4 (speech recognizer), 1e-5 (playback speed adjuster)\n• Weight decay: 0.01\n• Dropout rate: 0.1\n• Gradient clipping: 1.0\n• Warmup steps: 10% of total steps\n3.4.2 Usage of the Application\nExamples of using AIxSpeed are shown in Figure 3.5. This is an example of the prototype\napplied to a video uploaded to YouTube. The horizontal axis is the playback time, and the\nvertical axis represents the playback speed output by the model for each playback time.\nThe first is an example of speeding up a dialogue, movie, or lecture.\nTwo speakers appear in the video, and the optimal playback speed can be set for each\nspeaker. Of particular interest is that the two speakers in the video speak at different\n39\n\n\fspeeds, so the average playback speed for the two speakers is different. It can also be seen\nthat the playback speed increases drastically from the moment when the dialog between\nthe two speakers ends and there is no more speech from the person. Although we did\nnot intend to design this feature, we can see that our system, like conventional playback\nspeed controllers, can speed up the playback speed in the parts where there is no speech.\nThe second example applies speed adjustment to non-native speech. The model selected\na higher average playback factor than for the native-speaker speech in the first video. This\nexample shows different model-selected speeds, but does not by itself establish improved\nhuman comprehension.\nAn important feature of this system is its fine-grained playback-speed control at the\nphoneme level. The experiments evaluated ASR performance and perceived speech quality, but did not separately evaluate the preservation of prosody or intonation.\n\n3.5\n\nEvaluation\n\n3.5.1 Technical Evaluation\nTo evaluate whether the proposed method can optimize playback speed while maintaining ASR performance, we compared the CER and WER values for AIxSpeed-modified\nspeech with those for constant-speed playback. The comparison conditions were 1.0x,\n1.5x, and constant playback matched to AIxSpeed’s average playback factor.\nA standard Wav2Vec2-based speech recognition model, which was the speech recognizer\nused in our method, was used to compute the CER and WER for comparison. The model\nperformance is shown in Table 3.1 (a) and (b) for LibriSpeech and UME-ERJ, respectively.\nAIxSpeed produces speech 1.30 times faster on average for LibriSpeech and 1.29 times\nfaster on average for UME-ERJ. Both results show that the playback speed optimized\nby AIxSpeed has lower values for both CER and WER than constant-speed playback\nmatched to the same average factor. Thus, the proposed method retained better ASR\nperformance than the matched constant-speed baseline.\nIn addition, for UME-ERJ, speech generated by AIxSpeed had a lower WER than speech\nat 1.0x playback. This indicates that it was more recognizable to the ASR model used\nfor evaluation; it does not directly establish improved intelligibility or comprehension for\nhuman listeners, which requires a dedicated human study.\n3.5.2 User Evaluation\nUser experiments were conducted to evaluate the perceived quality of the generated\nspeech. Speech generated by the proposed method was compared with speech played\nat a constant speed matched to the same average playback factor. Quality was evaluated\nusing the mean opinion score (MOS), which is commonly used in speech synthesis research [190, 198]. This measure rates speech quality on a five-point scale from 1 (poor)\nto 5 (excellent).\nParticipants were 50 US residents who used English on a daily basis. 20 sentences were\nextracted from each of the LibriSpeech and UME-ERJ datasets, and half were converted\nto speech with the proposed speed-up, while the other half were converted to speech with\na constant speed-up. Participants were given a total of 40 sentences of audio and asked\nto rate the quality of the audio.\n• The source of the audio (AIxSpeed or baseline) was not disclosed to participants.\nFigure 3.6 shows the perceived quality of speech at baseline and with AIxSpeed. The\nmean opinion scores for LibriSpeech and UME-ERJ were 0.5 and 0.8 points higher with\n40\n\n\fFigure 3.6: Voice quality comparison between baseline (constant playback speed increase\nthroughout) and AIxSpeed (flexible playback speed increase).\nthe proposed method, respectively. The source paper did not report a significance test for\nthese differences.\nA larger MOS difference was observed for UME-ERJ than for LibriSpeech, but further\nevaluation is needed to determine whether AIxSpeed is especially effective for non-nativespeaker speech.\n\n3.6\n\nDiscussion\n\n3.6.1 Evaluation Results and Implications\nThe technical evaluation shows that the proposed method produces lower CER and WER\nthan constant-speed speech at the same average playback factor. The user evaluation\nlikewise shows higher mean opinion scores for AIxSpeed speech than for the matched\nconstant-speed baseline. This average speed-up of approximately 1.3x (e.g., reducing a\n60-minute lecture to about 46 minutes) represents a tangible time saving for learners.\nThis allows users to watch videos at a reasonable speed without having to adjust the\nplayback speed for each video. However, the improvement in MOS values by using the\nproposed method is by no means suﬀicient. In the current model, the average conversion\nto a faster playback speed is about 1.3 times, but it is a future task to investigate whether\nit is possible to make this even faster.\nIn fact, many video playback services implement 1.5x and 2.0x playback speeds, and\nsome people watch dramas and lectures at such speeds. Therefore, we expect that it will\nbe possible to convert up to this speed and make the audio easy to understand. In addition,\nsince each user has a different preferred playback speed, personalizing the model so that\nit plays at the optimal playback speed for users is also a future issue.\n\n41\n\n\f3.6.2 Limitations and Future Work\nOne key aspect of AIxSpeed is its content-agnostic approach, relying purely on acoustic\nfeatures to determine playback speed. While this allows for broad applicability across\nlanguages and topics without needing semantic understanding, it also presents certain\nlimitations, particularly in educational contexts. For instance, the system does not currently consider the semantic importance of content segments or a speaker’s pedagogical\nintent. A lecturer might intentionally slow down their speech to emphasize a critical point\nor explain a complex idea, even if their pronunciation is perfectly clear. In such cases,\nAIxSpeed, prioritizing acoustic intelligibility as determined by the ASR proxy, might\ninappropriately speed up these segments, potentially hindering deep comprehension or\nmissing pedagogical cues. Future research could explore integrating natural language\nprocessing techniques to identify semantically important segments or to understand discourse structures (e.g., cues for emphasis or topic shifts). This would allow for a more\nnuanced speed adjustment that balances acoustic intelligibility with the preservation of\npedagogically salient prosodic variations. Furthermore, adapting the system to accommodate intentional pauses or variations in speech rhythm that contribute to the speaker’s\nstyle or the listener’s engagement, rather than solely optimizing for raw speed based on\nASR confidence, remains an avenue for future enhancement.\nThe personalization mentioned in Section 3.6.1 could involve allowing users, or an overarching AI-Guided Learning system, to set a target intelligibility level (e.g., by adjusting the ASR confidence threshold that AIxSpeed uses as a proxy for human listening\nease). For example, advanced learners or those familiar with the content might opt for\na slightly lower intelligibility threshold to maximize speed further, while beginners or\nthose encountering complex material might prefer a higher threshold to ensure thorough\nunderstanding. This would cater to the diverse needs, prior knowledge, and capabilities\nof learners, addressing the potential gap between a generic optimization and individual\nlearner preferences.\nWhile the current model achieves a commendable average speed-up of approximately\n1.3x while improving or maintaining intelligibility compared to constant speed-up, future iterations could explore architectural or loss function modifications to safely push\ntowards higher average speeds (e.g., 1.5x to 2.0x), which are common user-selectable options in many commercial players. Investigating the system’s behavior and the perceived\nintelligibility limits at even more extreme speeds (e.g., up to 4x for highly redundant or\nexceptionally clear speech segments) could reveal further optimization potential or define\nan ”intelligibility cliff” for adaptive speed methods. However, achieving high intelligibility at such significantly accelerated rates for general audio content would likely require\nsubstantial advancements in both the speed modification algorithm and the underlying\nintelligibility prediction model.\nWhile AIxSpeed demonstrated effectiveness on English speech (both native and nonnative), its performance across a wider range of languages, accents, and acoustic environments (e.g., noisy conditions, overlapping speech) requires further investigation to\nfully substantiate its claimed versatility. The current ASR proxy model is trained primarily on English, and its reliability as an intelligibility measure for other languages would\nneed careful validation or language-specific adaptation.\n3.6.3 Listening Comprehension and Speech Recognition\nAs discussed in the preliminary research chapter (Section 3.2), it can be seen that there is\na relationship between speech recognition performance and human transcription ability.\nThus, we expect to build automated systems for various tasks and evaluations by replacing\nhuman speech comprehension ability with machine learning models, as in this system.\n\n42\n\n\fOn the other hand, speech recognition performance based on playback speed does not\nperfectly match human speech comprehension. In other words, as the playback speed\nincreases, the dictation performance decreases in both cases, but the performance values\nare not exactly the same. Thus, we anticipate that by training speech recognition models\nto match these relationships as closely as possible, it will be possible to use them more\ngenerally as alternatives to humans.\nDistillation, a technique that learns to approximate an output that matches existing results,\nwill be the technical key. Specifically, by using human listening data as a “teacher” and\ntraining speech recognition models to show similar performance degradation patterns, it\nmay be possible to build more accurate proxy models of human listening ability.\n3.6.4 Adjustment of Non-Native Speakers’ Speech\nThe UME-ERJ ASR result suggests that moderate acceleration of non-native speech may\nbe useful under some conditions. Because this study did not measure human intelligibility or comprehension for that comparison, this remains a hypothesis for future evaluation.\nOne motivation for this study was an informal observation that accelerated playback of\nsome non-native speech could sound easier to follow. This observation is not treated as\nempirical evidence in the present study.\n3.6.5 Potential Applications Beyond Speed Optimization and Societal Implementation\nThe technology of AIxSpeed has various application possibilities beyond simple highspeed playback. Particularly noteworthy is its application to hearing aids for the hearing\nimpaired. By utilizing the technology for evaluating listening diﬀiculty at the phoneme\nlevel, it is thought that it will be possible to develop adaptive hearing aids that automatically play diﬀicult-to-hear parts slowly.\nSuch hearing aids could dynamically adjust playback speed according to environmental\nsounds and speaker characteristics. This is a possible research direction, not an evaluated\napplication of the present study: studies with hearing-impaired users and noisy speech\nwould be needed to determine whether it improves listening or comprehension.\nFurthermore, applications to language learning can be expected. By automatically playing parts containing diﬀicult phonemes or words slowly according to the learner’s level,\nmore effective listening practice becomes possible. It may also be used in presentation\npractice or lecture preparation, where speakers can objectively evaluate and improve the\nlistening diﬀiculty of their own utterances.\nFurthermore, when compared to standard playback speed controls found in many video/audio\nplatforms (e.g., YouTube, podcast players), AIxSpeed offers a significant advantage through\nits adaptive and fine-grained (phoneme-level) speed adjustments. Instead of a uniform speed-up that can make diﬀicult passages unintelligible or slow passages tedious,\nAIxSpeed optimizes locally, aiming to maximize speed only where content remains clear,\nthus providing a more consistently comfortable and eﬀicient listening experience. This\ncontrasts with comprehensive language learning platforms like Duolingo, which focus\non interactive exercises and curriculum, whereas AIxSpeed enhances the consumption\nof existing, often passive, audio learning materials.\nRegarding broader societal implementation, AIxSpeed could be integrated as an advanced\nfeature within existing Learning Management Systems (LMS), as a plug-in for popular\nvideo and audio streaming platforms, or even as a browser extension enhancing webbased audio content. Key technical challenges for widespread adoption include ensuring\nreal-time or near real-time processing capabilities for on-the-fly adjustments, optimizing\ncomputational eﬀiciency for deployment on diverse end-user devices (including mobile),\n43\n\n\fand developing intuitive user interfaces that provide appropriate control and transparency\nover the automated speed adjustments. Furthermore, practical deployment would necessitate considerations regarding the processing of copyrighted material and ensuring user\ndata privacy, especially if personalization features are implemented. Pilot studies in authentic educational settings would be crucial to gather user feedback on usability and\nperceived learning benefits, and to iteratively refine the system for robust, real-world application.\n\n3.7\n\nConclusion\n\nThis chapter has presented AIxSpeed, a system that addresses rapid listening to audio\ncontent in the Consume stage of the AI-Guided Learning framework. AIxSpeed automatically adjusts playback speed at the phoneme level using ASR performance as a proxy\nderived from the pilot comparison with human transcription performance.\nThe key contributions of AIxSpeed are threefold. Firstly, the pilot study found a correlation of 0.9977 between changes in human transcription accuracy and ASR accuracy at\ntested playback speeds above 1.0x. Secondly, AIxSpeed introduces phoneme-level speed\nadjustment and achieved lower CER and WER than constant-speed playback matched to\nthe same average factor. Thirdly, the UME-ERJ evaluation found lower WER than at 1.0x\nplayback; this is an ASR result and does not establish improved human intelligibility for\nnon-native speech.\nThe experimental results presented in this chapter provide evidence for the eﬀicacy of\nAIxSpeed. At its average playback factors (1.30x for LibriSpeech and 1.29x for UMEERJ), the system produced lower Character Error Rate (CER) and Word Error Rate\n(WER) than constant-speed playback at the same factors. In the user study, AIxSpeed\nspeech also received higher mean opinion scores than the matched constant-speed baseline. These findings indicate the system’s potential to enhance the eﬀiciency of audio\ncontent consumption while maintaining intelligibility.\nDespite these promising results, it is important to acknowledge the limitations of the\ncurrent implementation of AIxSpeed. The system’s performance may vary depending on\nfactors such as the acoustic characteristics of the input speech, the diversity of speakers,\nand the complexity of the content. Further research is needed to assess and optimize\nthe system’s performance across a wider range of speech types and listening conditions.\nAdditionally, the long-term effects of using AIxSpeed on listeners’ cognitive load and\ninformation retention need to be thoroughly investigated.\nLooking ahead, AIxSpeed holds considerable promise for a variety of applications. In the\ncontext of video distribution services, it could enhance user experience by providing personalized playback speeds that maximize comprehension and engagement. In language\nlearning scenarios, AIxSpeed could be integrated into tools that help learners adapt to different speech rates and accents, potentially accelerating the acquisition of listening skills.\nFurthermore, the system’s ability to improve the intelligibility of non-native speech could\nhave significant implications for international communication and collaboration in educational and professional settings.\nOne possible future direction is adaptive playback for hearing-impaired users. A system\ncould adjust playback speed according to the environment and speaker, but AIxSpeed was\nnot evaluated with hearing-impaired users or as a hearing aid. Whether such adaptation\nimproves accessibility requires dedicated clinical and usability evaluation.","numLines":600,"startLine":1763,"totalLines":4774}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-abb1ca69ce261bd1","type":"tool_result","content":"2381\t\fChapter 4\n2382\tFastPerson: Enhancing Video-Based Learning\n2383\tthrough Video Summarization that Preserves\n2384\tLinguistic and Visual Contexts\n2385\t4.1\n2386\t\n2387\tIntroduction\n2388\t\n2389\tThis chapter presents FastPerson, a novel video summarization system that enables efficient comprehension of video content in the Consume-Understand stages toward realizing AI-Guided Learning, the overall goal of this thesis. As discussed in Chapter 1,\n2390\tlecture videos contain multiple information channels including speaker explanations (audio), slides and board writing (visual), and gestures, but conventional summarization\n2391\tmethods cannot handle these integratively. FastPerson addresses this challenge through\n2392\tan innovative approach that integrates visual and audio information and generates summary videos in the original speaker’s voice.\n2393\tThe core innovation of FastPerson lies in its novel “video-to-video summarization” paradigm.\n2394\tUnlike conventional approaches that predominantly generate text-based transcripts or\n2395\tsummaries from audio [97], thereby sacrificing the rich visual context, or those that offer simple chaptering without true content distillation [206], FastPerson processes both\n2396\tvisual and audio streams to produce a condensed video summary. This approach maintains the multimodal integrity of the original lecture. Furthermore, by employing voice\n2397\tcloning technology to retain the original speaker’s vocal characteristics, FastPerson ensures a seamless and cognitively coherent learning experience, which is often disrupted\n2398\twhen navigating between original content and disparate summary formats.\n2399\tThe proliferation of video-sharing platforms has significantly enhanced the use of videos\n2400\tacross various fields, with education emerging as a particularly impactful domain. The\n2401\tadoption of video-based learning methods, such as flipped learning and Massive Open\n2402\tOnline Courses (MOOCs), has revolutionized educational paradigms. Flipped learning\n2403\tinverts the conventional classroom model by encouraging students to engage with video\n2404\tlectures before class, allowing in-class sessions to focus on interactive discussions and\n2405\thands-on learning experiences [23]. Similarly, MOOCs have democratized access to\n2406\teducation, offering an extensive array of video lectures and educational resources to a\n2407\tglobal audience [73, 66].\n2408\tNumerous studies have confirmed the effectiveness of video-based learning methods.\n2409\tZhang et al. demonstrated that interactive videos improve learners’ comprehension [214],\n2410\twhile Kay underscored the positive impact of video-based learning on comprehension,\n2411\tadaptation, and achievement [93]. Despite these benefits, significant challenges persist\n2412\tin the realm of video-based learning.\n2413\tUsers often struggle with the sheer volume and length of educational videos, leading to\n2414\ttime-consuming searches and diﬀiculties in aligning learning pace with individual comprehension levels. As Chen et al. [33] found, users may spend up to 80% of their time\n2415\t46\n2416\t\n2417\t\fon video platforms browsing and viewing only parts of content, indicating a significant\n2418\tineﬀiciency in locating relevant information. Moreover, the linear nature of long-form\n2419\tvideo can impose substantial cognitive load and challenge sustained attention, particularly when learners must sift through extensive footage to find specific segments of interest or to grasp core concepts distributed across the timeline [180, 158].\n2420\tTo address these challenges, various video summarization methods have been proposed\n2421\tto help users grasp video content quickly. Existing approaches typically focus on either visual or audio information to determine important parts of the video and generate\n2422\tsummarized versions [189]. However, these methods are limited in their effectiveness\n2423\tfor lecture videos, where crucial information is often distributed across both visual and\n2424\taudio channels, and may not always overlap.\n2425\tFor example, while an instructor verbally explains complex concepts, slides may display\n2426\tcomplementary diagrams or equations. Audio-only summarization loses these visual elements, while visual-only summarization misses the instructor’s detailed explanations\n2427\tand nuances. Furthermore, many existing summarization systems present results in text\n2428\tformat, breaking continuity with the original video and fragmenting the learning experience.\n2429\tIn response to these limitations, this chapter introduces FastPerson, a novel video summarization technique that comprehensively considers both visual and audio information.\n2430\tUnlike conventional methods, FastPerson creates a summary text that integrates information from both modalities before generating a summary video with narration. This\n2431\tapproach ensures a more holistic representation of the video content, reducing the risk\n2432\tof learners missing critical information from either the visual or audio components.\n2433\tThe key contributions of FastPerson are threefold:\n2434\t1. A comprehensive video summarization method that generates summary videos\n2435\treflecting important information from both visual and audio elements of lecture\n2436\tvideos. Particularly, it establishes a new paradigm of “video-to-video summarization,” providing a learning experience beyond conventional text-based summarization.\n2437\t2. A user-centered learning experience design that enables seamless switching between original and summary videos. By preserving the original speaker’s voice\n2438\tquality through voice cloning technology, it minimizes cognitive dissonance and\n2439\tallows learners to freely choose between concise and detailed information acquisition for each video chapter.\n2440\t3. Empirical evidence that the proposed method significantly reduces viewing time,\n2441\twith no statistically significant difference in quiz-measured comprehension from\n2442\tconventional playback.\n2443\tThese contributions position FastPerson as a crucial component within the Understand\n2444\tphase of our proposed AI-Guided Learning framework (see Chapter 1). While AIxSpeed\n2445\t(Chapter 3) focuses on optimizing the Consume phase for audio content through adaptive\n2446\tplayback, FastPerson extends this pursuit of eﬀiciency to the complex, multimodal nature\n2447\tof video lectures. By enabling learners to eﬀiciently grasp core concepts from visual\n2448\tand auditory information, FastPerson not only addresses the specific challenges of videobased learning but also prepares learners for the subsequent Imitate phase, supported by\n2449\tProfy (Chapter 5), which facilitates skill acquisition through targeted feedback. Together,\n2450\tthese systems aim to create a synergistic learning environment that enhances time eﬀiciency and learning outcomes across the entire spectrum of multimedia engagement.\n2451\tThe remainder of this chapter is organized as follows: Section 4.2 provides an overview\n2452\tof related work in video summarization and learning. Section 4.3 details the design and\n2453\t47\n2454\t\n2455\t\fFigure 4.1: FastPerson: Video summarization method that generates a summary sentence\n2456\tusing visual (objects and sentences on the frame) and audio information (speaker’s voice)\n2457\tbefore synthesizing the sentence into speech using the speech characteristics of the original speaker.\n2458\timplementation of the FastPerson system. Section 4.4 presents the evaluation methodology and experimental setup. Section 4.5 discusses the results of the user study and their\n2459\timplications. Finally, Section 4.6 concludes the chapter with a summary of findings and\n2460\tdirections for future research.\n2461\tBy addressing the challenges of eﬀicient video content comprehension, FastPerson complements the audio-focused approach of AIxSpeed presented in the previous chapter. Together, these systems contribute to a more comprehensive solution for time-eﬀicient learning in multimedia environments, paving the way for the skill acquisition support system,\n2462\tProfy, which will be introduced in the subsequent chapter.\n2463\t\n2464\t4.2\n2465\t\n2466\tSystem Design\n2467\t\n2468\tFastPerson can create a summarized video that considers both visual (text and objects\n2469\ton the screen) and audio information (speaker’s voice). FastPerson automatically divides\n2470\tthe original video into multiple coherent video segments, providing the user with the\n2471\tsummarized video and original video for each segment.\n2472\tThe summary and original videos are aligned by combining the summary text with speech\n2473\tsynthesized to resemble the original speaker and a corresponding clip from the original\n2474\tvideo, thereby supporting a seamless transition between the two versions. In addition,\n2475\twhen generating the summary video, the summary ratio is adjusted by reflecting the\n2476\tamount of visual and audio information to avoid over- or under-information while preserving important content for the learner.\n2477\tThis method is expected to be applied to a wide range of lecture videos, and therefore,\n2478\tthe use of LLM-based summarization is designed to eliminate the need for training with\n2479\tsupervised data. We describe the summarization method by focusing on the FastPerson\n2480\tarchitecture, as shown in Figure 4.1.\n2481\t4.2.1 Video Chapter Segmentation\n2482\tFastPerson first detects scene transitions and silence in a video and divides them into\n2483\tsegments to summarize the video into coherent chapters and generate a video summary.\n2484\tFor scene transition detection, we use a technique proposed by Nisreen et al. [150] to\n2485\t48\n2486\t\n2487\t\fanalyze the variation of the color distribution (histogram) between consecutive frames\n2488\tand determine the boundaries of the segments based on the magnitude of the variation.\n2489\tAccording to Truong et al. [189], distinct variations between consecutive frames indicate\n2490\ta change in scene, and histogram variations indicate changes in the mood or background\n2491\tof the video. Specifically, we calculate histograms in RGB color space with 64 bins and\n2492\tmeasure similarity between consecutive frames using Bhattacharyya distance. Changes\n2493\tin the Bhattacharyya distance exceeding a threshold of 0.6 between consecutive frames\n2494\tare detected as scene transitions. This threshold was empirically determined based on a\n2495\tpilot study involving diverse lecture videos, where it demonstrated a favorable balance\n2496\tbetween identifying meaningful segment breaks and avoiding over-segmentation due to\n2497\tminor visual shifts.\n2498\tWe use an algorithm that detects periods when the sound waveform has an amplitude below a certain threshold as silence because visual and audio breaks do not always coincide.\n2499\tThis method is based on the work of Scheirer et al., which enables detecting periods of\n2500\tlow amplitude or the absence of certain frequencies while identifying segments of silence\n2501\tor stillness [163]. Specifically, we calculate the RMS (Root Mean Square) of audio in\n2502\t500ms windows and detect periods where the amplitude is below 5% of the maximum\n2503\tamplitude for more than 1 second as silence periods. This 5% threshold was selected\n2504\tbased on preliminary experiments to effectively identify pauses in speech without misclassifying quiet speech segments as silence.\n2505\tThese visual scene transitions and periods of silence in audio are considered to separate\n2506\tand segment the video at the cut point. This functionality enables automatic chaptering\n2507\t(segmentation) without the need for the video creator to tag each contiguous segment.\n2508\t4.2.2 Visual and Audio Information Extraction\n2509\tOur method converts visual and auditory information into textual information for generating a summary video that considers both. We use optical character recognition (OCR)\n2510\tand object detection techniques to obtain visual information.\n2511\tOCR is used for digitizing imaged document information, and it can detect characters\n2512\tin images and videos and convert them into documents [176]. This technology can effectively capture document information in video images, such as the content of slides or\n2513\thandwritten notes on a whiteboard in a lecture video. This information can then be used\n2514\tto reflect the topic and content of the documents in the video in the summary.\n2515\tOur system uses the Tesseract OCR engine [176] for OCR. Tesseract 4.0 employs LSTMbased neural networks and supports over 100 languages. As preprocessing, we apply\n2516\tgrayscale conversion, noise removal (Gaussian blur), and binarization (Otsu’s method)\n2517\tto improve OCR accuracy. These preprocessing steps are standard techniques known to\n2518\tenhance Tesseract’s performance, particularly for text in video frames which can vary in\n2519\tquality.\n2520\tObject detection is a technique for detecting and identifying specific objects or entities\n2521\tin each frame of a video [156]. The objects in the video are often closely related to the\n2522\tstory focus or theme of the segment. If a car is shown on a slide in a scene from an online\n2523\tlecture on machine learning, the speaker is expected to talk about automated driving or\n2524\timage recognition, and the object “car” is considered a key point in that video segment.\n2525\tTherefore, key scenes and important entities in the video can be identified using object\n2526\tdetection, and effective summaries can be generated based on this information. Our system uses the faster R-CNN [156] with ResNet-50-FPN as the object model backbone.\n2527\tThis model is pre-trained on the MS COCO dataset and can detect 80 classes of common objects. The detection threshold is set to 0.7 to extract only high-confidence objects.\n2528\tThis value was chosen after empirical testing to balance precision and recall for object\n2529\tdetection in lecture video contexts.\n2530\t49\n2531\t\n2532\t\fThe accurate extraction of speech information is necessary for academic content in videos,\n2533\tespecially lectures and seminars. Audio information is extracted from a video as follows:\n2534\t(1) Audio stream is extracted from the video file.\n2535\t(2) This audio stream is fed as an input to a speech recognition model to obtain a transcription of the document.\n2536\tThe speech signal needs to be converted into document data with high accuracy because\n2537\tthe accuracy at this stage can affect the quality of the summary. Recent advances in speech\n2538\trecognition have been attributed to models based on RNN [64, 68] and Transformer [192,\n2539\t196].\n2540\tIn our method, we employ Whisper [147] as the speech recognition model. Chosen for its\n2541\tstate-of-the-art accuracy across diverse audio conditions, a result of its large-scale training (approx. 680,000 hours of multilingual speech), Whisper’s deep learning architecture enables robust transcription essential for downstream summarization. Specifically,\n2542\twe use the Whisper Large model (1550M parameters) with a beam search width of 5\n2543\tand a temperature parameter of 0 for inference. These parameters were chosen based\n2544\ton the recommendations in the original Whisper paper for achieving high accuracy in\n2545\ttranscription tasks.\n2546\tDespite the high performance of this method, its recognition accuracy is not perfect.\n2547\tTherefore, we use OCR to recognize text in the video, complementing technical terms\n2548\tand proper nouns missed by speech recognition.\n2549\t4.2.3 Summary Audio Generation\n2550\tVideo summarization uses data labeled with important points in a video, along with supervised learning methods to detect important locations in the video. However, our target\n2551\teducational videos cover a wide variety of domains, and it is diﬀicult to use training data\n2552\tto extract such important points.\n2553\tRecently, a number of LLMs have been proposed in the natural language processing domain based on Transformer architectures, including BERT [47], T5 [151], and GPT3 [148,\n2554\t149]. These models are trained on documents and dialogues from different domains and\n2555\thave a wide range of domain knowledge, hence enabling them to perform various natural\n2556\tlanguage processing, including summary generation [19].\n2557\tOur system can summarize lecture videos without the need for teacher data using this\n2558\tLLM. Usually, when a summary is generated from speech, a transcript of the speech is\n2559\tused as input to generate the summary. However, this method generates a summary based\n2560\ton both the transcript of the speech and visual metadata obtained through OCR and object\n2561\trecognition, thereby reflecting the visual information of the video in the summary.\n2562\tThe following instructions are provided to an LLM [154] to generate a document summary for each video segment using both visual and audio information. In this study, we\n2563\tuse GPT-3.5-turbo with a temperature parameter of 0.7 and top-p of 0.9 for inference.\n2564\t\u0013\n2565\tInstructions\n2566\t\n2567\t\u0010\n2568\t\n2569\tUsing the provided transcription of spoken content, OCR-derived textual data, and\n2570\tobject detection information, synthesize a comprehensive summary. This summary\n2571\tshould highlight the key themes and actions depicted in the video. Focus on distilling the essence of the video by combining insights from the transcription (which\n2572\tcaptures the spoken words), the OCR data (which provides text found within the\n2573\tvideo), and object detection (which identifies significant objects and actions).\n2574\t\u0012\n2575\t\n2576\t\u0011\n2577\t\n2578\t50\n2579\t\n2580\t\fAlong with these instructions, the transcribed text of each video segment, OCR, and\n2581\tresulting visual information from object recognition are entered into the LLM as documents. This method enables creating a summary that emphasizes nuances not included\n2582\tin the speech content, speaker’s movements, visual objects, and documents within slides\n2583\tand visual presentations.\n2584\t4.2.4 Summary Length Calculation\n2585\tThe length of the document summary must appropriately reflect the amount of information in the original video when summarizing a video. In other words, if the video is long\n2586\tor rich in content, it is necessary to provide suﬀicient information in the summary, and\n2587\tif the original video is short, the summary should be shortened by the same amount to\n2588\teliminate redundancy.\n2589\tTherefore, we adjust the number of characters in the output summary based on the length\n2590\tof the transcribed document. In addition, the number of output characters after summarization is adjusted based on the amount of visual information because our method\n2591\tgenerates video summaries based on visual information as well as audio. For example,\n2592\tif the amount of text on a slide is large even if the speaker’s speech is short, it must be\n2593\tsummarized to accurately reflect the information content of the slide.\n2594\tFrom this point of view, the proposed system calculates the number of output characters\n2595\tN of the summary using Equation 4.1:\n2596\tN = max(Nmin , ws · Lt + wv · (Lo + Lc ))\n2597\t\n2598\t(4.1)\n2599\t\n2600\twhere\n2601\t• N represents the final output word count of the summary.\n2602\t• Nmin is the minimum summary length (e.g., 50 words).\n2603\t• Lt denotes the length of the transcribed text.\n2604\t• Lo represents the number of objects identified on the screen.\n2605\t• Lc represents the word count of OCR-processed visual elements.\n2606\t• ws and wv represent the weight coeﬀicients to adjust the importance between audio\n2607\tand visual information, respectively.\n2608\tThe weight coeﬀicients ws and wv are selected based on experimental data and user preferences. These coeﬀicients can be varied to adjust the emphasis on visual or auditory\n2609\tinformation. Although the balance can be altered according to user interest and comprehension levels to provide personalized summary videos, in the present study, fixed values\n2610\tof 0.3 and 2.5 are set for ws and wv , respectively. We set Nmin = 50 words to ensure\n2611\tsummaries are not overly terse.\n2612\tThese values were determined through preliminary experiments, confirming that they\n2613\tgenerate appropriate summary lengths for both lecture videos rich in visual information\n2614\t(slide-centered) and those centered on audio information (speaker-centered).\n2615\t4.2.5 Summary Video Generation\n2616\tA conventional audio summarization method used for audiobooks evaluates the importance of each audio transcription on a sentence-by-sentence basis and directly extracts\n2617\tand combines important audio segments. However, this method poses a challenge to the\n2618\tcontinuity of audio and video when merging the extracted segments. This method cannot\n2619\tproduce a video summary that reflects the visual representation of the video.\n2620\t51\n2621\t\n2622\t\fFigure 4.2: Overview of the FastPerson user interface: A Video Window showcases\n2623\tthe current video playback and offers user-controlled speed adjustments B Video Title\n2624\tprovides segment-specific titles C Summary and Original buttons allow seamless toggling between video versions and facilitate segment switches D Search Container offers\n2625\ta text-based search mechanism E Segments Container displays the thumbnails of video\n2626\tsegments, ensuring quick navigation.\n2627\tTherefore, this method uses a speech synthesis [166, 190] method, which uses a document\n2628\tsummary with visual and audio information as the input and speech synthesis technology\n2629\tto generate its reading voice. After the synthesized speech is generated, the optimal video\n2630\tsegment is selected from the original video based on the length of the speech.\n2631\tThe video segment is cut from the beginning, middle, or end of the original video, depending on the length of the summarized video. Based on the results of a simple user\n2632\texperiment, the video segment in the middle of the original video is used as the default\n2633\tjoining method. A continuous and concise video summary is achieved by combining this\n2634\tselected video segment with the synthesized audio.\n2635\tThis method avoids unnatural connections caused by combining different parts of the\n2636\tvideo. In addition, users are expected to frequently switch between the original and synthesized videos when using this method, and therefore, it is necessary to provide a seamless experience.\n2637\tThus, we adopt a method that minimizes the change in the audio between the original\n2638\tand summarized videos using the speaker adaptation technique when synthesizing the\n2639\taudio [9]. The features of the speaker’s voice in the original video are extracted, and\n2640\ta synthesized voice is generated that reflects the speaker’s voice quality. This method\n2641\treduces the mismatch of voice features when switching between the original and synthesized videos, thereby enabling seamless switching.\n2642\tSpecifically, this research uses the Coqui TTS (Text-to-Speech) system and adopts the\n2643\tVITS (Variational Inference with adversarial learning for end-to-end Text-to-Speech)\n2644\tmodel. For speaker adaptation, we extract clean speech samples of at least 30 seconds\n2645\tfrom the original video and learn speaker embeddings. This duration was found to be\n2646\tsuﬀicient for VITS to capture the primary characteristics of the speaker’s voice in our\n2647\tpreliminary tests. Using these embeddings, we generate synthesized speech close to the\n2648\toriginal speaker’s voice quality from the summary text.\n2649\t\n2650\t52\n2651\t\n2652\t\f4.3\n2653\t\n2654\tApplication and Interaction\n2655\t\n2656\t4.3.1 Application Design\n2657\tFastPerson enables users to switch between the original longer video and summarized\n2658\tvideo for each segment within each video, depending on their level of interest and understanding. In addition, a static component placed on the side of the video displays a\n2659\tthumbnail image and summary text for each video segment, enabling the user to quickly\n2660\taccess the corresponding video chapter.\n2661\ta Video Window allows users to view\n2662\tThe entire user interface is shown in Figure 4.2; ⃝\n2663\tvideos in the window in either a summarized or original format. The playback speed can\n2664\tbe adjusted based on preference, enabling flexible viewing of content.\n2665\tb Video Title is placed at the top of the video window and displays the title of the\n2666\t⃝\n2667\tc Summary and Original buttons allow the user to\n2668\tsegment that is currently playing. ⃝\n2669\tquickly switch between the summary and original full version of the video. In this case,\n2670\tmaking the voice quality of the summary video similar to that of the original video allows\n2671\tthe user to perceive continuity between the two versions.\n2672\tNext to the video is a window displaying a pair of text summaries and thumbnail images\n2673\trepresenting the chapters of the video. These windows are effective in helping users\n2674\tquickly access the segment of interest in the video [37, 140].\n2675\td Search Window provides a document entry box and a search button to search for\n2676\t⃝\n2677\tspecific keywords in a video segment. This feature allows the user to see where their\n2678\ttopics of interest are discussed in the video.\n2679\te Segment Window displays thumbnails, titles, and summary text for each video seg⃝\n2680\tment. Users can quickly access the appropriate video segment by clicking on the appropriate thumbnail.\n2681\tThe application is built using Flask, a lightweight Python web framework. The FastPerson backend manages the files for each video segment and delivers them eﬀiciently. The\n2682\tbackend includes functions to load the associated thumbnail and text files based on the\n2683\tname of a specific segment in the video, enabling a quick delivery of the video segment\n2684\trequested by the user.\n2685\tOn the front end, the user interface is built using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. The\n2686\tinterface includes a video player, video title display, a button to toggle between the summarized and original videos, a search function, and thumbnails and summaries for each\n2687\tsegment. JavaScript is used to control video playback, implement the search function,\n2688\tand respond to user interactions.\n2689\t4.3.2 User Interaction\n2690\tFastPerson provides a personalized learning experience by allowing users to adjust the\n2691\tamount of time they spend watching a video and where they spend time. For example,\n2692\tusers can quickly grasp the gist of an entire video by watching only the summary video.\n2693\tAlternatively, users can view the summary video for the parts they understand and the\n2694\toriginal detailed video for the parts they do not.\n2695\tThe design of this learning experience is inspired by the flexibility of the information\n2696\tacquisition experience when reading a book. An example of how the system can be used\n2697\tto view a video is presented below.\n2698\t1. Skimming through the entire summary: By clicking the Summary button (C)\n2699\tand playing the video, users can watch the summarized version of the video. This\n2700\tis similar to the action of quickly skimming through the entire text content.\n2701\t\n2702\t53\n2703\t\n2704\t\fTable 4.1: Details about the videos used in the evaluation experiment.\n2705\t\n2706\tTitle\n2707\t\n2708\tVideo 1\n2709\t\n2710\tVideo 2\n2711\t\n2712\tOrigin of Life\n2713\t\n2714\tMarket Revolution\n2715\t\n2716\t12:58\n2717\tWhat molecule, also called the\n2718\tbiological blueprint for life, do all\n2719\tliving organisms possess?\n2720\tAll living things on Earth share\n2721\timportant processes. What are\n2722\tthose four processes?\n2723\t\n2724\t21:55\n2725\tThe activity shown in the image\n2726\tcontributed most directly to which\n2727\tof the following?\n2728\tWhich of the following\n2729\tdevelopments most directly relates\n2730\tto the overall trend from 1800 to\n2731\t1840 depicted on the graph?\n2732\tAccording to the passage, which of\n2733\tthe following best explains the\n2734\tmost important effect that\n2735\ttechnological developments had on\n2736\tthe American society?\n2737\tWhich of the following had a\n2738\tsignificant long-term result of the\n2739\tmajor pattern depicted on the map?\n2740\tNone\n2741\t\n2742\tThumbnail\n2743\t\n2744\tLength\n2745\tQuestion 1\n2746\t\n2747\tQuestion 2\n2748\t\n2749\tQuestion 3\n2750\t\n2751\tQuestion 4\n2752\t\n2753\tQuestion 5\n2754\t\n2755\tIn 1828, a German chemist,\n2756\tFriedrich Wohler, proved that\n2757\torganic life is composed of the\n2758\tsame components as inorganic\n2759\tmatter. How did he do this?\n2760\tWhat ingredient(s) are considered\n2761\tnecessary for the creation of life?\n2762\tWhat quality of life allows for its\n2763\tslow diversification?\n2764\t\n2765\t2. Skimming over parts that are interesting: Using the Segments Container (E),\n2766\tusers can obtain a brief overview of each segment based on the associated thumbnail and text. Clicking on any segment will play its respective video after clicking\n2767\tthe Summary button (C), enabling users to sample content before deciding to\n2768\twatch the detailed video.\n2769\t3. Watching only the parts of interest repeatedly: Users can replay any segment\n2770\tmultiple times for a thorough understanding, which mirrors the experience of rereading one’s favorite section of a book.\n2771\t4. Searching for specific content: The Search Container (D) allows users to search\n2772\tfor specific terms or keywords across segments, providing direct access to desired\n2773\tcontent, which is similar to using an index in a book.\n2774\t5. Switching between the summary and the detailed view: At any given point,\n2775\tusers can toggle between the summarized and original videos using the Summary\n2776\tand Original buttons (C), catering to their comprehension and interest levels.\n2777\t\n2778\t4.4\n2779\t\n2780\tEvaluation\n2781\t\n2782\tWe conducted a series of quantitative and qualitative evaluation experiments to assess\n2783\tthe learning effectiveness and user experience of the FastPerson system. The specific\n2784\tobjectives were as follows:\n2785\t54\n2786\t\n2787\t\fFigure 4.3: Correct answer rates for each question of the video.\n2788\t• To compare the learning effects when using the FastPerson system versus a standard\n2789\tvideo playback player.\n2790\t• To evaluate the effectiveness of summarization using the FastPerson system and its\n2791\tfeature of switching between the summarized and original videos in aiding understanding.\n2792\t• To assess the usability and user satisfaction of the FastPerson interface.\n2793\t\n2794\t4.4.1 Participants\n2795\tParticipants from diverse backgrounds were recruited through the crowdsourcing platform Prolific. A total of 40 people participated in the study and were randomly assigned\n2796\tto an intervention group that used FastPerson (19 participants) or a control group that\n2797\tused a generic video player (21 participants).\n2798\tThe participants were selected based on their fluency in English and completion of at least\n2799\tsecondary education, because the videos were in English and the participants needed to\n2800\tbe able to understand the content of the videos. The participants’ demographics (gender,\n2801\tage, educational background, etc.) were collected as additional information to assess the\n2802\teffect of the experiment.\n2803\tThe participants comprised 23 males and 17 females, with an average age of 26.05 years\n2804\t(σ = 7.05). Among the participants, 35, 35, 15, and 15% had a high school diploma\n2805\tor GED, university bachelor’s degree (BA/BSc/other), technical school or community\n2806\tcollege, and graduate degree (MA/MSc/MPhil/etc.), respectively.\n2807\tA post-experiment questionnaire measured the participants’ prior knowledge of the videos\n2808\tthey watched on a 5-point scale (1 being “no prior knowledge” and 5 being “extensive\n2809\tknowledge”), and the results showed that, for the first video, the intervention and control\n2810\tgroups had means of 2.63 (σ = 1.16) and 2.10 (σ = 0.94), respectively.\n2811\tFor the second video, the intervention and control groups had means of 1.32 (σ = 0.58)\n2812\tand 1.38 (σ = 0.59), respectively. A t-test between the two groups revealed t-statistic\n2813\tvalues of 1.607 with a p-value of 0.116 and -0.351 with a p-value of 0.727 for the first\n2814\tand second videos, with no statistically significant difference between the two groups at\n2815\tthe 0.05 level of significance.\n2816\tThus, there was no clear difference in prior knowledge between the two groups. Further,\n2817\tthe participants were motivated to achieve high scores because it was clearly stated that\n2818\tthose with the highest scores would receive a bonus reward. All participants understood\n2819\tand agreed that the process was voluntary and that they had the right to withdraw at any\n2820\ttime.\n2821\t55\n2822\t\n2823\t\fFigure 4.4: Average video viewing time by viewing method.\n2824\t4.4.2 Procedure\n2825\tIn this experiment, participants gathered through crowdsourcing were randomly assigned\n2826\tto an intervention group using FastPerson and a control group that used a general video\n2827\tplayer. Two videos were selected from Khan Academy, an online learning service, for\n2828\tviewing, and the accompanying questions were used to measure the learning effect of the\n2829\tvideos.\n2830\tTo prevent the participants’ understanding of the videos from changing drastically based\n2831\ton their prerequisite knowledge, we selected lecture videos with a level of diﬀiculty that\n2832\tcould be understood from the video alone. The selected videos were from the fields of\n2833\tbiology and history, as shown in Table 4.1. The videos were accompanied by questions\n2834\tused to assess the participants’ understanding of their content.\n2835\tSeveral questions for the first video could not be answered correctly without looking at\n2836\tmaterials besides the video, and therefore, five questions excluding these ones were used\n2837\tto check the participants’ understanding of the video. For the second video, the four\n2838\tquestions included in the video were used to assess comprehension.\n2839\tThe first video is a lecture video that focuses on the speaker and visual elements, while the\n2840\tsecond focuses on the board. The participants viewed these videos under their assigned\n2841\tcondition and subsequently answered the accompanying questions.\n2842\tThe intervention group also completed an additional questionnaire about FastPerson. The\n2843\texperiment was conducted through Prolific, with a median working time of 46 min and\n2844\t26 s, and each participant was paid £7.\n2845\t4.4.3 Evaluation of Learning Effectiveness\n2846\tWe analyzed the percentage of correct answers to questions about the videos and the\n2847\ttime taken to view the videos after watching the same two videos for the control and the\n2848\t56\n2849\t\n2850\t\fintervention groups to evaluate the learning effectiveness. The right side of Figure 4.3\n2851\tcompares the average correct response rates for questions related to Video 1 (“Q1”) and\n2852\tVideo 2 (“Q2”).\n2853\tFor Video 1, the correct answer rate for the control group (red bar) was 0.70 (σ = 0.33)\n2854\tand that of intervention group (blue bar) was 0.71 (σ = 0.42) while those for Video 2 are\n2855\t0.63 (σ = 0.39) and 0.67 (σ = 0.46), respectively. Therefore, there is no clear difference\n2856\tin the percentage of correct answers between both groups.\n2857\tIn fact, a t-test between the two groups yields t-statistic values of 0.170 with a p-value\n2858\tof 0.864 and 0.528 with a p-value of 0.598 for the first and second videos, respectively,\n2859\tneither of which is statistically significant when the significance level is set at 0.05.\n2860\tFurther, the percentage of correct answers for each question (left side of Figure 4.3) shows\n2861\tthat the score for viewing on a general video player was higher for question 1 of video 1\n2862\t(Q1-1), while the score for viewing on FastPerson was higher for question 3 of video 1\n2863\t(Q1-3) and question 1 of video 2 (Q2-1).\n2864\tQ1-1 is “What molecule, also called the biological blueprint for life, is found in all living\n2865\torganisms?” and the correct answer is “DNA,” which is picked up as a keyword in the\n2866\tFastPerson summary, indicating that it is a keyword that “all living organisms possess.”\n2867\tHowever, the percentage of correct answers is likely to have decreased because of a lack\n2868\tof explanation of the term “biological blueprint for life.”\n2869\tMeanwhile, although Q1-3 and Q2-1 are adequately covered in both the simple video\n2870\tplayback and FastPerson, the topics of the other incorrect choices in the question are\n2871\trelatively fewer in FastPerson. Therefore, users who watched FastPerson are not expected\n2872\tto select these incorrect choices, resulting in a higher percentage of correct answers.\n2873\tThe time taken to watch the videos is summarized in Figure 4.4, where the average time\n2874\ttaken by users to watch Videos 1 and 2 was 1003 (σ = 312) and 1352 (σ = 270) s,\n2875\trespectively, using the regular video player. FastPerson, which provides summary videos,\n2876\ttook users an average of 469 (σ = 424) and 634 (σ = 496) s to watch Videos 1 and 2,\n2877\trespectively.\n2878\tUsing FastPerson reduced the viewing times for Videos 1 and 2 by 53.24 and 53.11%,\n2879\trespectively. A t-test between the two groups showed that the t-statistic values for the first\n2880\tand second videos were 4.409, with a p-value of 1.063 · 10−3 , and 5.281, with a p-value\n2881\tof 9.955 · 10−6 , respectively. Both differences were statistically significant at p < 0.01.\n2882\tTherefore, FastPerson reduced viewing time without a statistically significant difference\n2883\tin quiz-measured comprehension compared with the original-video condition. This 53%\n2884\ttime reduction represents a significant improvement in time eﬀiciency, which has important implications especially for time-constrained learners.\n2885\t4.4.4 Evaluation of User Experience\n2886\tWe conducted a questionnaire survey on the proposed method after 19 participants in the\n2887\tFastPerson intervention group finished learning from the videos to evaluate the usability,\n2888\tlearning effectiveness, and user experience of the system.\n2889\tThe survey included the following aspects:\n2890\t1. Interface Usability\n2891\tQ1: Was the system interface intuitive to use? (1: Very Diﬀicult - 5: Very Intuitive)\n2892\tQ2: Was it easy to find the required information? (1: Very Diﬀicult - 5: Very\n2893\tEasy)\n2894\tQ3: How was your experience of using the Summary and Original buttons? (Openended Response)\n2895\t57\n2896\t\n2897\t\fFigure 4.5: Survey results on user experience.\n2898\tQ4: What improvements would you suggest for the user interface design? (Openended Response)\n2899\t2. Learning Experience Satisfaction\n2900\tQ5: Was learning with our system enjoyable? (1: Not Enjoyable At All - 5: Extremely Enjoyable)\n2901\tQ6: Compared with regular video players, how satisfied are you with learning\n2902\tusing our system? (1: Not Satisfied At All - 5: Extremely Satisfied)\n2903\tQ7: How would you rate your learning experience with our system compared to\n2904\tthat with other methods? (Open-ended Response)\n2905\t3. Usefulness of Summary and Original Videos\n2906\tQ8: Was the summarized video helpful in understanding the information? (1: Not\n2907\tHelpful At All - 5: Extremely Helpful)\n2908\tQ9: Was the feature to switch between the Original and Summary videos useful?\n2909\t(1: Not Useful At All - 5: Extremely Useful)\n2910\tQ10: How did you perceive the quality of the summarized videos? (Open-ended\n2911\tResponse)\n2912\t4. Evaluation of Specific Features\n2913\tQ11: Was it convenient to access specific video segments using thumbnails and\n2914\tsummary texts? (1: Not Convenient At All - 5: Extremely Convenient)\n2915\tQ12: Was it easy to find information in the videos using the search window? (1:\n2916\tVery Diﬀicult - 5: Very Easy)\n2917\tThe questions included both numerical and open-ended questions, and the results for the\n2918\tnumerical questions are shown in Figure 4.5. For each question, the percentage of users\n2919\twho responded to each of the five levels is presented as a bar graph.\n2920\t\n2921\t58\n2922\t\n2923\t\fEvaluation of Interface Usability\n2924\tThe average rating was 3.89 (σ = 1.20) for the question about the system’s ease of use,\n2925\tespecially intuitiveness (Q1). This indicates that, on average, the users found the interface\n2926\tintuitive.\n2927\tFor the question about the ease of finding information in the system (Q2), the average\n2928\trating was 3.11 (σ = 1.33). This rating reflects that users found it moderately easy to\n2929\tfind the required information. However, the relatively high standard deviation highlights\n2930\tvariation in the user experience.\n2931\tThe open-ended questions provided additional insights. For example, in terms of the\n2932\tusability of the Summary and Original buttons, many participants stated that they were\n2933\t“very easy to use and understand” and appreciated the usefulness and convenience of\n2934\tswitching between the different video modes (Q3). However, one user commented, “The\n2935\tinterface is all over the place, and you have to spend a lot of time to understand it.”\n2936\tThis suggests that there is room for improvement in the placement of buttons and components. Suggestions for interface design improvements received a wide range of responses\n2937\t(Q4). Although some participants felt that the humanization of the summary descriptions\n2938\tand voice reading needed further improvements, others called for improvements to the\n2939\toverall visual aspect of the site.\n2940\tEvaluation of Learning Experience Satisfaction\n2941\tA quantitative measure of the enjoyment of learning with FastPerson was obtained with\n2942\ta mean rating of 3.00 (Q5). Although this rating indicates a moderate level of enjoyment\n2943\tamong users, the standard deviation of 1.29 suggests that there is a wide range of variation\n2944\tin the participants’ experiences.\n2945\tNext, we measured satisfaction with learning using FastPerson compared with a regular\n2946\tvideo player and found a mean rating of 2.95 (σ = 1.43) (Q6). This could be attributed\n2947\tto some users preferring or being more familiar with traditional video players.\n2948\tIn the qualitative feedback received through the open-ended question Q7, users who rated\n2949\ttheir experience positively indicated that the features of FastPerson that allowed them to\n2950\tquickly obtain the information they needed and the interactive nature of the learning\n2951\texperience contributed to their enjoyment and satisfaction.\n2952\tEvaluation of the Usefulness of Summary Videos\n2953\tThe mean rating for the usefulness of the summary videos in understanding the information was 3.42 (σ = 1.61) (Q8). This rating indicates a moderately positive acceptance\n2954\tof the effectiveness of the summary videos in conveying information. Similarly, the average rating for the ability to switch between summary and original video formats was\n2955\t3.84 (σ = 1.26) (Q9).\n2956\tUsers positively rated the ability to switch between in-depth and summary video formats.\n2957\tIn particular, 78% of FastPerson users rated the chapter-switching function as “useful”\n2958\tor “very useful.” This reports perceived usefulness; the study did not separately test the\n2959\tfeature’s causal effect on learning outcomes.\n2960\tIn the qualitative feedback (Q10), many users praised the concept of the summary videos,\n2961\tnoting that they could help them quickly understand key concepts, especially when reviewing a topic or when time is limited. However, they raised concerns about the quality\n2962\tof the content in the summaries.\n2963\tThe main issues were related to the depth of content and the narration of the summary\n2964\tvideo. The summaries were found to be useful; however, they sometimes lacked suﬀicient detail to fully understand complex topics. In addition, several users pointed out\n2965\tthat the pronunciation of the words in the summary video was different, confirming that\n2966\t59\n2967\t\n2968\t\fthe quality of the summary video could be improved by using a more powerful speech\n2969\tsynthesizer.\n2970\tEvaluation of Specific Features\n2971\tThe ease of accessing video segments using thumbnails and summary text received an\n2972\taverage rating of 3.53 (σ =1.50) (Q11). Similarly, the ease of retrieving information in\n2973\tthe video using the search window received an average rating of 3.47 (σ =1.39) (Q12).\n2974\tThese features are not unique to FastPerson; however, they are compatible with the ability\n2975\tto switch between the summary and original videos. The majority of users rated both the\n2976\tthumbnail and summary text as well as the ability to switch between the original and\n2977\tsummarized videos (average rating 3.84) as useful, which indicates that presenting the\n2978\tuser with both the summary video and summary text, rather than just the summary text\n2979\tof the video, improves usability and convenience.\n2980\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6af2b770-4e64-4fd8-b01f-b5667819ace1","uuid":"94a3ba91-9fa8-453b-bb20-2bb315f2201a","timestamp":"2026-08-11T17:23:12.618Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/full.txt","content":"\fChapter 4\nFastPerson: Enhancing Video-Based Learning\nthrough Video Summarization that Preserves\nLinguistic and Visual Contexts\n4.1\n\nIntroduction\n\nThis chapter presents FastPerson, a novel video summarization system that enables efficient comprehension of video content in the Consume-Understand stages toward realizing AI-Guided Learning, the overall goal of this thesis. As discussed in Chapter 1,\nlecture videos contain multiple information channels including speaker explanations (audio), slides and board writing (visual), and gestures, but conventional summarization\nmethods cannot handle these integratively. FastPerson addresses this challenge through\nan innovative approach that integrates visual and audio information and generates summary videos in the original speaker’s voice.\nThe core innovation of FastPerson lies in its novel “video-to-video summarization” paradigm.\nUnlike conventional approaches that predominantly generate text-based transcripts or\nsummaries from audio [97], thereby sacrificing the rich visual context, or those that offer simple chaptering without true content distillation [206], FastPerson processes both\nvisual and audio streams to produce a condensed video summary. This approach maintains the multimodal integrity of the original lecture. Furthermore, by employing voice\ncloning technology to retain the original speaker’s vocal characteristics, FastPerson ensures a seamless and cognitively coherent learning experience, which is often disrupted\nwhen navigating between original content and disparate summary formats.\nThe proliferation of video-sharing platforms has significantly enhanced the use of videos\nacross various fields, with education emerging as a particularly impactful domain. The\nadoption of video-based learning methods, such as flipped learning and Massive Open\nOnline Courses (MOOCs), has revolutionized educational paradigms. Flipped learning\ninverts the conventional classroom model by encouraging students to engage with video\nlectures before class, allowing in-class sessions to focus on interactive discussions and\nhands-on learning experiences [23]. Similarly, MOOCs have democratized access to\neducation, offering an extensive array of video lectures and educational resources to a\nglobal audience [73, 66].\nNumerous studies have confirmed the effectiveness of video-based learning methods.\nZhang et al. demonstrated that interactive videos improve learners’ comprehension [214],\nwhile Kay underscored the positive impact of video-based learning on comprehension,\nadaptation, and achievement [93]. Despite these benefits, significant challenges persist\nin the realm of video-based learning.\nUsers often struggle with the sheer volume and length of educational videos, leading to\ntime-consuming searches and diﬀiculties in aligning learning pace with individual comprehension levels. As Chen et al. [33] found, users may spend up to 80% of their time\n46\n\n\fon video platforms browsing and viewing only parts of content, indicating a significant\nineﬀiciency in locating relevant information. Moreover, the linear nature of long-form\nvideo can impose substantial cognitive load and challenge sustained attention, particularly when learners must sift through extensive footage to find specific segments of interest or to grasp core concepts distributed across the timeline [180, 158].\nTo address these challenges, various video summarization methods have been proposed\nto help users grasp video content quickly. Existing approaches typically focus on either visual or audio information to determine important parts of the video and generate\nsummarized versions [189]. However, these methods are limited in their effectiveness\nfor lecture videos, where crucial information is often distributed across both visual and\naudio channels, and may not always overlap.\nFor example, while an instructor verbally explains complex concepts, slides may display\ncomplementary diagrams or equations. Audio-only summarization loses these visual elements, while visual-only summarization misses the instructor’s detailed explanations\nand nuances. Furthermore, many existing summarization systems present results in text\nformat, breaking continuity with the original video and fragmenting the learning experience.\nIn response to these limitations, this chapter introduces FastPerson, a novel video summarization technique that comprehensively considers both visual and audio information.\nUnlike conventional methods, FastPerson creates a summary text that integrates information from both modalities before generating a summary video with narration. This\napproach ensures a more holistic representation of the video content, reducing the risk\nof learners missing critical information from either the visual or audio components.\nThe key contributions of FastPerson are threefold:\n1. A comprehensive video summarization method that generates summary videos\nreflecting important information from both visual and audio elements of lecture\nvideos. Particularly, it establishes a new paradigm of “video-to-video summarization,” providing a learning experience beyond conventional text-based summarization.\n2. A user-centered learning experience design that enables seamless switching between original and summary videos. By preserving the original speaker’s voice\nquality through voice cloning technology, it minimizes cognitive dissonance and\nallows learners to freely choose between concise and detailed information acquisition for each video chapter.\n3. Empirical evidence that the proposed method significantly reduces viewing time,\nwith no statistically significant difference in quiz-measured comprehension from\nconventional playback.\nThese contributions position FastPerson as a crucial component within the Understand\nphase of our proposed AI-Guided Learning framework (see Chapter 1). While AIxSpeed\n(Chapter 3) focuses on optimizing the Consume phase for audio content through adaptive\nplayback, FastPerson extends this pursuit of eﬀiciency to the complex, multimodal nature\nof video lectures. By enabling learners to eﬀiciently grasp core concepts from visual\nand auditory information, FastPerson not only addresses the specific challenges of videobased learning but also prepares learners for the subsequent Imitate phase, supported by\nProfy (Chapter 5), which facilitates skill acquisition through targeted feedback. Together,\nthese systems aim to create a synergistic learning environment that enhances time eﬀiciency and learning outcomes across the entire spectrum of multimedia engagement.\nThe remainder of this chapter is organized as follows: Section 4.2 provides an overview\nof related work in video summarization and learning. Section 4.3 details the design and\n47\n\n\fFigure 4.1: FastPerson: Video summarization method that generates a summary sentence\nusing visual (objects and sentences on the frame) and audio information (speaker’s voice)\nbefore synthesizing the sentence into speech using the speech characteristics of the original speaker.\nimplementation of the FastPerson system. Section 4.4 presents the evaluation methodology and experimental setup. Section 4.5 discusses the results of the user study and their\nimplications. Finally, Section 4.6 concludes the chapter with a summary of findings and\ndirections for future research.\nBy addressing the challenges of eﬀicient video content comprehension, FastPerson complements the audio-focused approach of AIxSpeed presented in the previous chapter. Together, these systems contribute to a more comprehensive solution for time-eﬀicient learning in multimedia environments, paving the way for the skill acquisition support system,\nProfy, which will be introduced in the subsequent chapter.\n\n4.2\n\nSystem Design\n\nFastPerson can create a summarized video that considers both visual (text and objects\non the screen) and audio information (speaker’s voice). FastPerson automatically divides\nthe original video into multiple coherent video segments, providing the user with the\nsummarized video and original video for each segment.\nThe summary and original videos are aligned by combining the summary text with speech\nsynthesized to resemble the original speaker and a corresponding clip from the original\nvideo, thereby supporting a seamless transition between the two versions. In addition,\nwhen generating the summary video, the summary ratio is adjusted by reflecting the\namount of visual and audio information to avoid over- or under-information while preserving important content for the learner.\nThis method is expected to be applied to a wide range of lecture videos, and therefore,\nthe use of LLM-based summarization is designed to eliminate the need for training with\nsupervised data. We describe the summarization method by focusing on the FastPerson\narchitecture, as shown in Figure 4.1.\n4.2.1 Video Chapter Segmentation\nFastPerson first detects scene transitions and silence in a video and divides them into\nsegments to summarize the video into coherent chapters and generate a video summary.\nFor scene transition detection, we use a technique proposed by Nisreen et al. [150] to\n48\n\n\fanalyze the variation of the color distribution (histogram) between consecutive frames\nand determine the boundaries of the segments based on the magnitude of the variation.\nAccording to Truong et al. [189], distinct variations between consecutive frames indicate\na change in scene, and histogram variations indicate changes in the mood or background\nof the video. Specifically, we calculate histograms in RGB color space with 64 bins and\nmeasure similarity between consecutive frames using Bhattacharyya distance. Changes\nin the Bhattacharyya distance exceeding a threshold of 0.6 between consecutive frames\nare detected as scene transitions. This threshold was empirically determined based on a\npilot study involving diverse lecture videos, where it demonstrated a favorable balance\nbetween identifying meaningful segment breaks and avoiding over-segmentation due to\nminor visual shifts.\nWe use an algorithm that detects periods when the sound waveform has an amplitude below a certain threshold as silence because visual and audio breaks do not always coincide.\nThis method is based on the work of Scheirer et al., which enables detecting periods of\nlow amplitude or the absence of certain frequencies while identifying segments of silence\nor stillness [163]. Specifically, we calculate the RMS (Root Mean Square) of audio in\n500ms windows and detect periods where the amplitude is below 5% of the maximum\namplitude for more than 1 second as silence periods. This 5% threshold was selected\nbased on preliminary experiments to effectively identify pauses in speech without misclassifying quiet speech segments as silence.\nThese visual scene transitions and periods of silence in audio are considered to separate\nand segment the video at the cut point. This functionality enables automatic chaptering\n(segmentation) without the need for the video creator to tag each contiguous segment.\n4.2.2 Visual and Audio Information Extraction\nOur method converts visual and auditory information into textual information for generating a summary video that considers both. We use optical character recognition (OCR)\nand object detection techniques to obtain visual information.\nOCR is used for digitizing imaged document information, and it can detect characters\nin images and videos and convert them into documents [176]. This technology can effectively capture document information in video images, such as the content of slides or\nhandwritten notes on a whiteboard in a lecture video. This information can then be used\nto reflect the topic and content of the documents in the video in the summary.\nOur system uses the Tesseract OCR engine [176] for OCR. Tesseract 4.0 employs LSTMbased neural networks and supports over 100 languages. As preprocessing, we apply\ngrayscale conversion, noise removal (Gaussian blur), and binarization (Otsu’s method)\nto improve OCR accuracy. These preprocessing steps are standard techniques known to\nenhance Tesseract’s performance, particularly for text in video frames which can vary in\nquality.\nObject detection is a technique for detecting and identifying specific objects or entities\nin each frame of a video [156]. The objects in the video are often closely related to the\nstory focus or theme of the segment. If a car is shown on a slide in a scene from an online\nlecture on machine learning, the speaker is expected to talk about automated driving or\nimage recognition, and the object “car” is considered a key point in that video segment.\nTherefore, key scenes and important entities in the video can be identified using object\ndetection, and effective summaries can be generated based on this information. Our system uses the faster R-CNN [156] with ResNet-50-FPN as the object model backbone.\nThis model is pre-trained on the MS COCO dataset and can detect 80 classes of common objects. The detection threshold is set to 0.7 to extract only high-confidence objects.\nThis value was chosen after empirical testing to balance precision and recall for object\ndetection in lecture video contexts.\n49\n\n\fThe accurate extraction of speech information is necessary for academic content in videos,\nespecially lectures and seminars. Audio information is extracted from a video as follows:\n(1) Audio stream is extracted from the video file.\n(2) This audio stream is fed as an input to a speech recognition model to obtain a transcription of the document.\nThe speech signal needs to be converted into document data with high accuracy because\nthe accuracy at this stage can affect the quality of the summary. Recent advances in speech\nrecognition have been attributed to models based on RNN [64, 68] and Transformer [192,\n196].\nIn our method, we employ Whisper [147] as the speech recognition model. Chosen for its\nstate-of-the-art accuracy across diverse audio conditions, a result of its large-scale training (approx. 680,000 hours of multilingual speech), Whisper’s deep learning architecture enables robust transcription essential for downstream summarization. Specifically,\nwe use the Whisper Large model (1550M parameters) with a beam search width of 5\nand a temperature parameter of 0 for inference. These parameters were chosen based\non the recommendations in the original Whisper paper for achieving high accuracy in\ntranscription tasks.\nDespite the high performance of this method, its recognition accuracy is not perfect.\nTherefore, we use OCR to recognize text in the video, complementing technical terms\nand proper nouns missed by speech recognition.\n4.2.3 Summary Audio Generation\nVideo summarization uses data labeled with important points in a video, along with supervised learning methods to detect important locations in the video. However, our target\neducational videos cover a wide variety of domains, and it is diﬀicult to use training data\nto extract such important points.\nRecently, a number of LLMs have been proposed in the natural language processing domain based on Transformer architectures, including BERT [47], T5 [151], and GPT3 [148,\n149]. These models are trained on documents and dialogues from different domains and\nhave a wide range of domain knowledge, hence enabling them to perform various natural\nlanguage processing, including summary generation [19].\nOur system can summarize lecture videos without the need for teacher data using this\nLLM. Usually, when a summary is generated from speech, a transcript of the speech is\nused as input to generate the summary. However, this method generates a summary based\non both the transcript of the speech and visual metadata obtained through OCR and object\nrecognition, thereby reflecting the visual information of the video in the summary.\nThe following instructions are provided to an LLM [154] to generate a document summary for each video segment using both visual and audio information. In this study, we\nuse GPT-3.5-turbo with a temperature parameter of 0.7 and top-p of 0.9 for inference.\n\u0013\nInstructions\n\n\u0010\n\nUsing the provided transcription of spoken content, OCR-derived textual data, and\nobject detection information, synthesize a comprehensive summary. This summary\nshould highlight the key themes and actions depicted in the video. Focus on distilling the essence of the video by combining insights from the transcription (which\ncaptures the spoken words), the OCR data (which provides text found within the\nvideo), and object detection (which identifies significant objects and actions).\n\u0012\n\n\u0011\n\n50\n\n\fAlong with these instructions, the transcribed text of each video segment, OCR, and\nresulting visual information from object recognition are entered into the LLM as documents. This method enables creating a summary that emphasizes nuances not included\nin the speech content, speaker’s movements, visual objects, and documents within slides\nand visual presentations.\n4.2.4 Summary Length Calculation\nThe length of the document summary must appropriately reflect the amount of information in the original video when summarizing a video. In other words, if the video is long\nor rich in content, it is necessary to provide suﬀicient information in the summary, and\nif the original video is short, the summary should be shortened by the same amount to\neliminate redundancy.\nTherefore, we adjust the number of characters in the output summary based on the length\nof the transcribed document. In addition, the number of output characters after summarization is adjusted based on the amount of visual information because our method\ngenerates video summaries based on visual information as well as audio. For example,\nif the amount of text on a slide is large even if the speaker’s speech is short, it must be\nsummarized to accurately reflect the information content of the slide.\nFrom this point of view, the proposed system calculates the number of output characters\nN of the summary using Equation 4.1:\nN = max(Nmin , ws · Lt + wv · (Lo + Lc ))\n\n(4.1)\n\nwhere\n• N represents the final output word count of the summary.\n• Nmin is the minimum summary length (e.g., 50 words).\n• Lt denotes the length of the transcribed text.\n• Lo represents the number of objects identified on the screen.\n• Lc represents the word count of OCR-processed visual elements.\n• ws and wv represent the weight coeﬀicients to adjust the importance between audio\nand visual information, respectively.\nThe weight coeﬀicients ws and wv are selected based on experimental data and user preferences. These coeﬀicients can be varied to adjust the emphasis on visual or auditory\ninformation. Although the balance can be altered according to user interest and comprehension levels to provide personalized summary videos, in the present study, fixed values\nof 0.3 and 2.5 are set for ws and wv , respectively. We set Nmin = 50 words to ensure\nsummaries are not overly terse.\nThese values were determined through preliminary experiments, confirming that they\ngenerate appropriate summary lengths for both lecture videos rich in visual information\n(slide-centered) and those centered on audio information (speaker-centered).\n4.2.5 Summary Video Generation\nA conventional audio summarization method used for audiobooks evaluates the importance of each audio transcription on a sentence-by-sentence basis and directly extracts\nand combines important audio segments. However, this method poses a challenge to the\ncontinuity of audio and video when merging the extracted segments. This method cannot\nproduce a video summary that reflects the visual representation of the video.\n51\n\n\fFigure 4.2: Overview of the FastPerson user interface: A Video Window showcases\nthe current video playback and offers user-controlled speed adjustments B Video Title\nprovides segment-specific titles C Summary and Original buttons allow seamless toggling between video versions and facilitate segment switches D Search Container offers\na text-based search mechanism E Segments Container displays the thumbnails of video\nsegments, ensuring quick navigation.\nTherefore, this method uses a speech synthesis [166, 190] method, which uses a document\nsummary with visual and audio information as the input and speech synthesis technology\nto generate its reading voice. After the synthesized speech is generated, the optimal video\nsegment is selected from the original video based on the length of the speech.\nThe video segment is cut from the beginning, middle, or end of the original video, depending on the length of the summarized video. Based on the results of a simple user\nexperiment, the video segment in the middle of the original video is used as the default\njoining method. A continuous and concise video summary is achieved by combining this\nselected video segment with the synthesized audio.\nThis method avoids unnatural connections caused by combining different parts of the\nvideo. In addition, users are expected to frequently switch between the original and synthesized videos when using this method, and therefore, it is necessary to provide a seamless experience.\nThus, we adopt a method that minimizes the change in the audio between the original\nand summarized videos using the speaker adaptation technique when synthesizing the\naudio [9]. The features of the speaker’s voice in the original video are extracted, and\na synthesized voice is generated that reflects the speaker’s voice quality. This method\nreduces the mismatch of voice features when switching between the original and synthesized videos, thereby enabling seamless switching.\nSpecifically, this research uses the Coqui TTS (Text-to-Speech) system and adopts the\nVITS (Variational Inference with adversarial learning for end-to-end Text-to-Speech)\nmodel. For speaker adaptation, we extract clean speech samples of at least 30 seconds\nfrom the original video and learn speaker embeddings. This duration was found to be\nsuﬀicient for VITS to capture the primary characteristics of the speaker’s voice in our\npreliminary tests. Using these embeddings, we generate synthesized speech close to the\noriginal speaker’s voice quality from the summary text.\n\n52\n\n\f4.3\n\nApplication and Interaction\n\n4.3.1 Application Design\nFastPerson enables users to switch between the original longer video and summarized\nvideo for each segment within each video, depending on their level of interest and understanding. In addition, a static component placed on the side of the video displays a\nthumbnail image and summary text for each video segment, enabling the user to quickly\naccess the corresponding video chapter.\na Video Window allows users to view\nThe entire user interface is shown in Figure 4.2; ⃝\nvideos in the window in either a summarized or original format. The playback speed can\nbe adjusted based on preference, enabling flexible viewing of content.\nb Video Title is placed at the top of the video window and displays the title of the\n⃝\nc Summary and Original buttons allow the user to\nsegment that is currently playing. ⃝\nquickly switch between the summary and original full version of the video. In this case,\nmaking the voice quality of the summary video similar to that of the original video allows\nthe user to perceive continuity between the two versions.\nNext to the video is a window displaying a pair of text summaries and thumbnail images\nrepresenting the chapters of the video. These windows are effective in helping users\nquickly access the segment of interest in the video [37, 140].\nd Search Window provides a document entry box and a search button to search for\n⃝\nspecific keywords in a video segment. This feature allows the user to see where their\ntopics of interest are discussed in the video.\ne Segment Window displays thumbnails, titles, and summary text for each video seg⃝\nment. Users can quickly access the appropriate video segment by clicking on the appropriate thumbnail.\nThe application is built using Flask, a lightweight Python web framework. The FastPerson backend manages the files for each video segment and delivers them eﬀiciently. The\nbackend includes functions to load the associated thumbnail and text files based on the\nname of a specific segment in the video, enabling a quick delivery of the video segment\nrequested by the user.\nOn the front end, the user interface is built using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. The\ninterface includes a video player, video title display, a button to toggle between the summarized and original videos, a search function, and thumbnails and summaries for each\nsegment. JavaScript is used to control video playback, implement the search function,\nand respond to user interactions.\n4.3.2 User Interaction\nFastPerson provides a personalized learning experience by allowing users to adjust the\namount of time they spend watching a video and where they spend time. For example,\nusers can quickly grasp the gist of an entire video by watching only the summary video.\nAlternatively, users can view the summary video for the parts they understand and the\noriginal detailed video for the parts they do not.\nThe design of this learning experience is inspired by the flexibility of the information\nacquisition experience when reading a book. An example of how the system can be used\nto view a video is presented below.\n1. Skimming through the entire summary: By clicking the Summary button (C)\nand playing the video, users can watch the summarized version of the video. This\nis similar to the action of quickly skimming through the entire text content.\n\n53\n\n\fTable 4.1: Details about the videos used in the evaluation experiment.\n\nTitle\n\nVideo 1\n\nVideo 2\n\nOrigin of Life\n\nMarket Revolution\n\n12:58\nWhat molecule, also called the\nbiological blueprint for life, do all\nliving organisms possess?\nAll living things on Earth share\nimportant processes. What are\nthose four processes?\n\n21:55\nThe activity shown in the image\ncontributed most directly to which\nof the following?\nWhich of the following\ndevelopments most directly relates\nto the overall trend from 1800 to\n1840 depicted on the graph?\nAccording to the passage, which of\nthe following best explains the\nmost important effect that\ntechnological developments had on\nthe American society?\nWhich of the following had a\nsignificant long-term result of the\nmajor pattern depicted on the map?\nNone\n\nThumbnail\n\nLength\nQuestion 1\n\nQuestion 2\n\nQuestion 3\n\nQuestion 4\n\nQuestion 5\n\nIn 1828, a German chemist,\nFriedrich Wohler, proved that\norganic life is composed of the\nsame components as inorganic\nmatter. How did he do this?\nWhat ingredient(s) are considered\nnecessary for the creation of life?\nWhat quality of life allows for its\nslow diversification?\n\n2. Skimming over parts that are interesting: Using the Segments Container (E),\nusers can obtain a brief overview of each segment based on the associated thumbnail and text. Clicking on any segment will play its respective video after clicking\nthe Summary button (C), enabling users to sample content before deciding to\nwatch the detailed video.\n3. Watching only the parts of interest repeatedly: Users can replay any segment\nmultiple times for a thorough understanding, which mirrors the experience of rereading one’s favorite section of a book.\n4. Searching for specific content: The Search Container (D) allows users to search\nfor specific terms or keywords across segments, providing direct access to desired\ncontent, which is similar to using an index in a book.\n5. Switching between the summary and the detailed view: At any given point,\nusers can toggle between the summarized and original videos using the Summary\nand Original buttons (C), catering to their comprehension and interest levels.\n\n4.4\n\nEvaluation\n\nWe conducted a series of quantitative and qualitative evaluation experiments to assess\nthe learning effectiveness and user experience of the FastPerson system. The specific\nobjectives were as follows:\n54\n\n\fFigure 4.3: Correct answer rates for each question of the video.\n• To compare the learning effects when using the FastPerson system versus a standard\nvideo playback player.\n• To evaluate the effectiveness of summarization using the FastPerson system and its\nfeature of switching between the summarized and original videos in aiding understanding.\n• To assess the usability and user satisfaction of the FastPerson interface.\n\n4.4.1 Participants\nParticipants from diverse backgrounds were recruited through the crowdsourcing platform Prolific. A total of 40 people participated in the study and were randomly assigned\nto an intervention group that used FastPerson (19 participants) or a control group that\nused a generic video player (21 participants).\nThe participants were selected based on their fluency in English and completion of at least\nsecondary education, because the videos were in English and the participants needed to\nbe able to understand the content of the videos. The participants’ demographics (gender,\nage, educational background, etc.) were collected as additional information to assess the\neffect of the experiment.\nThe participants comprised 23 males and 17 females, with an average age of 26.05 years\n(σ = 7.05). Among the participants, 35, 35, 15, and 15% had a high school diploma\nor GED, university bachelor’s degree (BA/BSc/other), technical school or community\ncollege, and graduate degree (MA/MSc/MPhil/etc.), respectively.\nA post-experiment questionnaire measured the participants’ prior knowledge of the videos\nthey watched on a 5-point scale (1 being “no prior knowledge” and 5 being “extensive\nknowledge”), and the results showed that, for the first video, the intervention and control\ngroups had means of 2.63 (σ = 1.16) and 2.10 (σ = 0.94), respectively.\nFor the second video, the intervention and control groups had means of 1.32 (σ = 0.58)\nand 1.38 (σ = 0.59), respectively. A t-test between the two groups revealed t-statistic\nvalues of 1.607 with a p-value of 0.116 and -0.351 with a p-value of 0.727 for the first\nand second videos, with no statistically significant difference between the two groups at\nthe 0.05 level of significance.\nThus, there was no clear difference in prior knowledge between the two groups. Further,\nthe participants were motivated to achieve high scores because it was clearly stated that\nthose with the highest scores would receive a bonus reward. All participants understood\nand agreed that the process was voluntary and that they had the right to withdraw at any\ntime.\n55\n\n\fFigure 4.4: Average video viewing time by viewing method.\n4.4.2 Procedure\nIn this experiment, participants gathered through crowdsourcing were randomly assigned\nto an intervention group using FastPerson and a control group that used a general video\nplayer. Two videos were selected from Khan Academy, an online learning service, for\nviewing, and the accompanying questions were used to measure the learning effect of the\nvideos.\nTo prevent the participants’ understanding of the videos from changing drastically based\non their prerequisite knowledge, we selected lecture videos with a level of diﬀiculty that\ncould be understood from the video alone. The selected videos were from the fields of\nbiology and history, as shown in Table 4.1. The videos were accompanied by questions\nused to assess the participants’ understanding of their content.\nSeveral questions for the first video could not be answered correctly without looking at\nmaterials besides the video, and therefore, five questions excluding these ones were used\nto check the participants’ understanding of the video. For the second video, the four\nquestions included in the video were used to assess comprehension.\nThe first video is a lecture video that focuses on the speaker and visual elements, while the\nsecond focuses on the board. The participants viewed these videos under their assigned\ncondition and subsequently answered the accompanying questions.\nThe intervention group also completed an additional questionnaire about FastPerson. The\nexperiment was conducted through Prolific, with a median working time of 46 min and\n26 s, and each participant was paid £7.\n4.4.3 Evaluation of Learning Effectiveness\nWe analyzed the percentage of correct answers to questions about the videos and the\ntime taken to view the videos after watching the same two videos for the control and the\n56\n\n\fintervention groups to evaluate the learning effectiveness. The right side of Figure 4.3\ncompares the average correct response rates for questions related to Video 1 (“Q1”) and\nVideo 2 (“Q2”).\nFor Video 1, the correct answer rate for the control group (red bar) was 0.70 (σ = 0.33)\nand that of intervention group (blue bar) was 0.71 (σ = 0.42) while those for Video 2 are\n0.63 (σ = 0.39) and 0.67 (σ = 0.46), respectively. Therefore, there is no clear difference\nin the percentage of correct answers between both groups.\nIn fact, a t-test between the two groups yields t-statistic values of 0.170 with a p-value\nof 0.864 and 0.528 with a p-value of 0.598 for the first and second videos, respectively,\nneither of which is statistically significant when the significance level is set at 0.05.\nFurther, the percentage of correct answers for each question (left side of Figure 4.3) shows\nthat the score for viewing on a general video player was higher for question 1 of video 1\n(Q1-1), while the score for viewing on FastPerson was higher for question 3 of video 1\n(Q1-3) and question 1 of video 2 (Q2-1).\nQ1-1 is “What molecule, also called the biological blueprint for life, is found in all living\norganisms?” and the correct answer is “DNA,” which is picked up as a keyword in the\nFastPerson summary, indicating that it is a keyword that “all living organisms possess.”\nHowever, the percentage of correct answers is likely to have decreased because of a lack\nof explanation of the term “biological blueprint for life.”\nMeanwhile, although Q1-3 and Q2-1 are adequately covered in both the simple video\nplayback and FastPerson, the topics of the other incorrect choices in the question are\nrelatively fewer in FastPerson. Therefore, users who watched FastPerson are not expected\nto select these incorrect choices, resulting in a higher percentage of correct answers.\nThe time taken to watch the videos is summarized in Figure 4.4, where the average time\ntaken by users to watch Videos 1 and 2 was 1003 (σ = 312) and 1352 (σ = 270) s,\nrespectively, using the regular video player. FastPerson, which provides summary videos,\ntook users an average of 469 (σ = 424) and 634 (σ = 496) s to watch Videos 1 and 2,\nrespectively.\nUsing FastPerson reduced the viewing times for Videos 1 and 2 by 53.24 and 53.11%,\nrespectively. A t-test between the two groups showed that the t-statistic values for the first\nand second videos were 4.409, with a p-value of 1.063 · 10−3 , and 5.281, with a p-value\nof 9.955 · 10−6 , respectively. Both differences were statistically significant at p < 0.01.\nTherefore, FastPerson reduced viewing time without a statistically significant difference\nin quiz-measured comprehension compared with the original-video condition. This 53%\ntime reduction represents a significant improvement in time eﬀiciency, which has important implications especially for time-constrained learners.\n4.4.4 Evaluation of User Experience\nWe conducted a questionnaire survey on the proposed method after 19 participants in the\nFastPerson intervention group finished learning from the videos to evaluate the usability,\nlearning effectiveness, and user experience of the system.\nThe survey included the following aspects:\n1. Interface Usability\nQ1: Was the system interface intuitive to use? (1: Very Diﬀicult - 5: Very Intuitive)\nQ2: Was it easy to find the required information? (1: Very Diﬀicult - 5: Very\nEasy)\nQ3: How was your experience of using the Summary and Original buttons? (Openended Response)\n57\n\n\fFigure 4.5: Survey results on user experience.\nQ4: What improvements would you suggest for the user interface design? (Openended Response)\n2. Learning Experience Satisfaction\nQ5: Was learning with our system enjoyable? (1: Not Enjoyable At All - 5: Extremely Enjoyable)\nQ6: Compared with regular video players, how satisfied are you with learning\nusing our system? (1: Not Satisfied At All - 5: Extremely Satisfied)\nQ7: How would you rate your learning experience with our system compared to\nthat with other methods? (Open-ended Response)\n3. Usefulness of Summary and Original Videos\nQ8: Was the summarized video helpful in understanding the information? (1: Not\nHelpful At All - 5: Extremely Helpful)\nQ9: Was the feature to switch between the Original and Summary videos useful?\n(1: Not Useful At All - 5: Extremely Useful)\nQ10: How did you perceive the quality of the summarized videos? (Open-ended\nResponse)\n4. Evaluation of Specific Features\nQ11: Was it convenient to access specific video segments using thumbnails and\nsummary texts? (1: Not Convenient At All - 5: Extremely Convenient)\nQ12: Was it easy to find information in the videos using the search window? (1:\nVery Diﬀicult - 5: Very Easy)\nThe questions included both numerical and open-ended questions, and the results for the\nnumerical questions are shown in Figure 4.5. For each question, the percentage of users\nwho responded to each of the five levels is presented as a bar graph.\n\n58\n\n\fEvaluation of Interface Usability\nThe average rating was 3.89 (σ = 1.20) for the question about the system’s ease of use,\nespecially intuitiveness (Q1). This indicates that, on average, the users found the interface\nintuitive.\nFor the question about the ease of finding information in the system (Q2), the average\nrating was 3.11 (σ = 1.33). This rating reflects that users found it moderately easy to\nfind the required information. However, the relatively high standard deviation highlights\nvariation in the user experience.\nThe open-ended questions provided additional insights. For example, in terms of the\nusability of the Summary and Original buttons, many participants stated that they were\n“very easy to use and understand” and appreciated the usefulness and convenience of\nswitching between the different video modes (Q3). However, one user commented, “The\ninterface is all over the place, and you have to spend a lot of time to understand it.”\nThis suggests that there is room for improvement in the placement of buttons and components. Suggestions for interface design improvements received a wide range of responses\n(Q4). Although some participants felt that the humanization of the summary descriptions\nand voice reading needed further improvements, others called for improvements to the\noverall visual aspect of the site.\nEvaluation of Learning Experience Satisfaction\nA quantitative measure of the enjoyment of learning with FastPerson was obtained with\na mean rating of 3.00 (Q5). Although this rating indicates a moderate level of enjoyment\namong users, the standard deviation of 1.29 suggests that there is a wide range of variation\nin the participants’ experiences.\nNext, we measured satisfaction with learning using FastPerson compared with a regular\nvideo player and found a mean rating of 2.95 (σ = 1.43) (Q6). This could be attributed\nto some users preferring or being more familiar with traditional video players.\nIn the qualitative feedback received through the open-ended question Q7, users who rated\ntheir experience positively indicated that the features of FastPerson that allowed them to\nquickly obtain the information they needed and the interactive nature of the learning\nexperience contributed to their enjoyment and satisfaction.\nEvaluation of the Usefulness of Summary Videos\nThe mean rating for the usefulness of the summary videos in understanding the information was 3.42 (σ = 1.61) (Q8). This rating indicates a moderately positive acceptance\nof the effectiveness of the summary videos in conveying information. Similarly, the average rating for the ability to switch between summary and original video formats was\n3.84 (σ = 1.26) (Q9).\nUsers positively rated the ability to switch between in-depth and summary video formats.\nIn particular, 78% of FastPerson users rated the chapter-switching function as “useful”\nor “very useful.” This reports perceived usefulness; the study did not separately test the\nfeature’s causal effect on learning outcomes.\nIn the qualitative feedback (Q10), many users praised the concept of the summary videos,\nnoting that they could help them quickly understand key concepts, especially when reviewing a topic or when time is limited. However, they raised concerns about the quality\nof the content in the summaries.\nThe main issues were related to the depth of content and the narration of the summary\nvideo. The summaries were found to be useful; however, they sometimes lacked suﬀicient detail to fully understand complex topics. In addition, several users pointed out\nthat the pronunciation of the words in the summary video was different, confirming that\n59\n\n\fthe quality of the summary video could be improved by using a more powerful speech\nsynthesizer.\nEvaluation of Specific Features\nThe ease of accessing video segments using thumbnails and summary text received an\naverage rating of 3.53 (σ =1.50) (Q11). Similarly, the ease of retrieving information in\nthe video using the search window received an average rating of 3.47 (σ =1.39) (Q12).\nThese features are not unique to FastPerson; however, they are compatible with the ability\nto switch between the summary and original videos. The majority of users rated both the\nthumbnail and summary text as well as the ability to switch between the original and\nsummarized videos (average rating 3.84) as useful, which indicates that presenting the\nuser with both the summary video and summary text, rather than just the summary text\nof the video, improves usability and convenience.\n","numLines":600,"startLine":2381,"totalLines":4774}}}
