{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc2dc69b7ecaca98","type":"tool_result","content":"3000\tbe addressed by improving the implementation and replacing it with a higher quality\n3001\tspeech synthesis method. In particular, adopting the latest neural voice cloning technology could potentially generate more natural synthesized speech.\n3002\tOther users also pointed out the need for a more intuitive user interface because they\n3003\tfound it diﬀicult to understand the functionality of the various features. In addition, we\n3004\tobserved that some users prefer the original simple video playback player, and we believe\n3005\tthat simplifying the overall structure of FastPerson can help improve user accessibility.\n3006\t\n3007\t60\n3008\t\n3009\t\f4.5.3 Technological Enhancements and Future Directions\n3010\tFastPerson extracts visual elements to be synthesized with the summarized audio from the\n3011\toriginal video. Using a text-to-video model, other methods can be employed to generate\n3012\ta video from the summarized text; Runway’s Gen-2 and OpenAI’s Sora are possible\n3013\tcandidates for these methods.\n3014\tThese methods focus on generating object-centered videos, and therefore, it currently\n3015\trequires more work to create lecture videos that are text-heavy and slide-centered. However, it may be possible to create helpful summary videos for generating lecture videos\n3016\tfocused on some topics.\n3017\tFor example, if a lecture is centered around the intricacies of plant biology, a text-tovideo model could be used to generate video segments illustrating key concepts such as\n3018\tphotosynthesis, cellular structures of plants, or growth cycles. This approach would not\n3019\tonly make the content more accessible but also more engaging for viewers, especially for\n3020\tvisual learners.\n3021\tAdditionally, future developments should consider creating adaptive summarization systems that automatically adjust the level of detail in summaries based on users’ learning\n3022\thistory and comprehension levels. By analyzing user learning patterns using machine\n3023\tlearning and generating summaries optimized for individual needs, more effective personalized learning could be achieved.\n3024\t4.5.4 Broader Implications and Applications of FastPerson\n3025\tThe broader implications of FastPerson go beyond its immediate application in educational video summarization. FastPerson has the potential to revolutionize how we interact with a wide range of video content. For example, in professional settings, FastPerson\n3026\tcould be used to distill long training sessions or meetings into concise summaries, allowing for eﬀicient review and better time management.\n3027\tFurther, FastPerson has potential applications related to media consumption, where users\n3028\tcan quickly view news or entertainment highlights. In addition, its application could extend to content creators and marketers, giving them a video editing tool to create engaging,\n3029\tsummarized versions of their content for better audience engagement.\n3030\tFastPerson’s adaptability could enable its integration into various platforms, making information more accessible and digestible for a broader audience. In particular, its application is expected in diverse fields such as news distribution, corporate training, online\n3031\teducation, and even the entertainment industry. This new paradigm of “video-to-video\n3032\tsummarization” could become a new standard for eﬀicient information consumption in\n3033\tthe age of information overload.\n3034\t\n3035\t4.6\n3036\t\n3037\tConclusion\n3038\t\n3039\tThis chapter has presented FastPerson, an innovative video summarization system that\n3040\taddresses the challenge of enabling rapid comprehension of video content using visual\n3041\tinformation in the Consume-Understand stages, which is one of the three primary objectives of this thesis aimed at achieving time-eﬀicient learning through audio and visual\n3042\tcontent under the AI-Guided Learning framework. FastPerson demonstrates a novel approach to video summarization by comprehensively considering both visual and auditory\n3043\tinformation in lecture videos, thereby enhancing the eﬀiciency of the learning experience.\n3044\tThe key contributions of FastPerson are threefold. Firstly, it introduces a comprehensive\n3045\tvideo summarization method that generates summary videos reflecting important information from both visual and audio elements of lecture videos. Particularly, it establishes\n3046\t\n3047\t61\n3048\t\n3049\t\fa new paradigm of “video-to-video summarization,” preserving context and speaker characteristics that are often lost in conventional text-based summarization. Secondly, FastPerson presents a user-centered learning experience design that enables seamless switching between original and summary videos. Reproducing the original speaker’s voice\n3050\tthrough voice cloning supports continuity between the two versions. Thirdly, the system\n3051\tdemonstrates empirically that it significantly reduces viewing time, with no statistically\n3052\tsignificant difference in quiz-measured comprehension from conventional playback.\n3053\tThe experimental results presented in this chapter provide evidence for the eﬀicacy of\n3054\tFastPerson. The evaluation experiments indicated no significant difference in video comprehension when using FastPerson compared to conventional video playback methods.\n3055\tCrucially, FastPerson reduced viewing time by approximately 53%. This substantial time\n3056\tsaving, together with the absence of a statistically significant quiz-score difference, indicates the system’s potential to enhance the eﬀiciency of video-based learning. Furthermore, 78% of FastPerson users rated the ability to switch between summary and original\n3057\tvideos as useful or very useful.\n3058\tDespite these promising results, it is important to acknowledge the limitations of the\n3059\tcurrent implementation of FastPerson. The evaluation results indicate room for improvement in terms of the depth of the summary content and the quality of the audio in the\n3060\tgenerated summaries. These areas present opportunities for future research and development. Additionally, further investigation is needed to assess the long-term effects of\n3061\tusing FastPerson on knowledge retention and learning outcomes across various subject\n3062\tareas and learner demographics.\n3063\tLooking ahead, FastPerson holds considerable promise for a variety of applications in\n3064\teducational technology and beyond. In the context of online learning platforms and\n3065\tMOOCs, it could significantly enhance the learning experience by allowing students to\n3066\tquickly grasp the key points of lengthy lecture videos and eﬀiciently review course materials. In corporate training scenarios, FastPerson could help employees quickly assimilate\n3067\tinformation from training videos, potentially improving the eﬀiciency of professional development programs.\n3068\tFurthermore, the system’s approach to integrating visual and auditory information could\n3069\thave applications in fields such as multimedia content analysis and automated video captioning. In particular, the innovative approach of “video-to-video summarization” could\n3070\topen new applications that were not possible with conventional text-based summarization.\n3071\tWithin the broader scope of this thesis, FastPerson complements the other two systems\n3072\tpresented: AIxSpeed, which focuses on optimizing audio playback speed, and Profy,\n3073\twhich addresses eﬀicient feedback systems for skill acquisition. While AIxSpeed enhances the auditory aspect of content consumption and Profy facilitates interactive learning, FastPerson optimizes the visual component of educational videos. Together, these\n3074\tthree systems form a comprehensive approach to enhancing time eﬀiciency in learning\n3075\tthrough multimedia content, collectively contributing to a more effective and personalized learning experience.\n3076\tIn conclusion, FastPerson represents an advancement in video summarization technology\n3077\tand its application to educational contexts. By reducing viewing time without a statistically significant difference in quiz-measured comprehension in the present experiment, it\n3078\taddresses a critical challenge in the era of abundant online video content. As research in\n3079\tthis area continues to evolve, future work should focus on refining the summary generation algorithms, improving the quality of synthesized audio, and exploring the integration\n3080\tof FastPerson with other learning technologies.\n3081\tFurthermore, longitudinal studies to assess the impact of FastPerson on long-term learning outcomes and its effectiveness across different subject domains would provide valuable insights. The findings from this research have the potential to reshape how we ap62\n3082\t\n3083\t\fproach video-based learning and content consumption in educational and professional\n3084\tsettings, contributing to more eﬀicient and effective knowledge acquisition in our increasingly digital world.\n3085\t\n3086\t63\n3087\t\n3088\t\fChapter 5\n3089\tProfy: Pronunciation Evaluation and Visualization\n3090\tof Differences from Native Speaker Groups Using\n3091\tUnannotated Audio Data\n3092\t5.1\n3093\t\n3094\tIntroduction\n3095\t\n3096\tThis chapter presents Profy, a skill acquisition feedback system in the Imitate stage toward realizing AI-Guided Learning, the overall goal of this thesis. As discussed in Chapter 1, it is diﬀicult for learners to evaluate differences between their own performance\n3097\tand expert models in imitation-based learning. Profy addresses this challenge by learning a proficiency classifier from small amounts of unannotated audio data and visualizing\n3098\twaveform regions emphasized by that classifier.\n3099\tThe core innovation of Profy lies in its ability to learn a proficiency classifier end-toend without specific predefined rules or detailed annotations. While many conventional\n3100\tCAPT (Computer-Assisted Pronunciation Training) systems use detailed phoneme-level\n3101\tannotations or linguistic expertise, Profy learns a classifier and visualizes model-emphasized\n3102\tregions from small amounts of unannotated data. Pronunciation proficiency is a cornerstone of effective communication in a foreign language, yet it remains one of the most\n3103\tchallenging aspects for learners in online environments due to the lack of scalable, individualized feedback [159]. Profy specifically targets this bottleneck within the broader\n3104\tscope of AI-Guided Learning. By enabling data-driven assessment and detailed feedback on pronunciation, Profy aims to make the skill acquisition phase of language learning more eﬀicient and effective, thereby contributing to the overall goal of optimizing\n3105\tonline learning experiences. The “proficiency” learned by Profy primarily refers to the\n3106\tacoustic characteristics that distinguish native-like speech from beginner speech in the\n3107\tcontext of target utterance repetition, rather than evaluating the semantic content or the\n3108\tpedagogical clarity of, for instance, a lecturer’s intentional slow speech, which might be\n3109\ta consideration for systems like AIxSpeed.\n3110\tImportantly, Profy’s methodology may extend beyond pronunciation learning. The framework of learning proficiency from data and visualizing model-emphasized regions could\n3111\tbe investigated for skills acquired through imitating expert performance, such as sports\n3112\tform correction, musical instrument instruction, and professional skill acquisition. This\n3113\tchapter evaluates pronunciation only; transfer to other domains remains future work.\n3114\tLearning a non-native language presents numerous challenges, particularly in the domain\n3115\tof speaking proficiency. The acquisition of proper pronunciation is a critical aspect of\n3116\tlanguage learning, as it significantly impacts communication effectiveness and overall\n3117\tlanguage competence. Traditional methods of pronunciation instruction often rely heavily on direct feedback from native speakers or language experts. However, this approach\n3118\tis limited by the availability of such experts and the time-intensive nature of individual\n3119\tfeedback sessions.\n3120\t64\n3121\t\n3122\t\fTo address these limitations, there has been a growing interest in the development of\n3123\tComputer-Assisted Pronunciation Training (CAPT) systems. Existing CAPT systems\n3124\thave made significant strides in automating pronunciation feedback. These systems typically compare the user’s speech with that of a specific native speaker as a model, analyzing units of rhythm, phonemes, or words to calculate differences [200, 106, 146].\n3125\tWhile effective, these approaches face several limitations. Firstly, they often require extensive speech data with detailed annotations, which can be costly and time-consuming\n3126\tto produce. Secondly, many systems are limited to comparisons with a single specific\n3127\tnative speaker, potentially narrowing the range of acceptable pronunciations. Thirdly,\n3128\tthe development of such systems for new languages or dialects can be challenging due to\n3129\tthe need for specialized linguistic expertise and resources.\n3130\tTo address these challenges, this chapter introduces Profy, a language learning support\n3131\tsystem that calculates model-derived speech scores from a small amount of unannotated\n3132\tspeech data without comparison to a specific person. Profy displays a proficiency score,\n3133\tclassifier-emphasized waveform regions, and distances between learner speech and a\n3134\tgroup-level native-speech representation.\n3135\tThis approach lets learners compare how the model-derived score, highlighted regions,\n3136\tand distance change across repeated attempts. The attention mechanism highlights regions that contributed to the classifier output, providing more localized information than\n3137\tan overall score; it does not by itself establish that a region is a phonetic error or explain\n3138\thow to correct it.\n3139\tThe key contributions of Profy are threefold:\n3140\t1. A technical framework for assessing learners’ speech proficiency using unannotated speech data. It (a) learns to distinguish acoustic patterns in native and learner\n3141\tspeech for specific utterances in an end-to-end, data-driven manner, without explicit rules for each target phoneme or prosodic element, and (b) visualizes classifieremphasized regions through an attention mechanism, moving beyond a holistic\n3142\tscore while not claiming phonetic error diagnosis.\n3143\t2. A language learning support system for non-native English speakers that lets learners inspect how model-derived waveform highlights and latent-space distances\n3144\tchange across repeated attempts.\n3145\t3. Empirical evidence from a user study in which the Profy condition showed a larger\n3146\tobserved intelligibility improvement than elicited imitation (EI), with non-overlapping\n3147\tpre- and post-practice confidence intervals within the Profy condition.\n3148\tThese contributions address pronunciation-learning feedback within the broader objectives of this thesis. The present chapter does not measure practice-time eﬀiciency or\n3149\testablish generalization to imitation tasks beyond pronunciation.\n3150\tThe remainder of this chapter is organized as follows: Section 5.2 provides an overview\n3151\tof related work in Computer-Assisted Language Learning (CALL) and pronunciation assessment. Section 5.3 details the design and implementation of the Profy system. Section\n3152\t5.4 presents the evaluation methodology and experimental setup. Section 5.5 discusses\n3153\tthe results of the user study and their implications. Finally, Section 5.6 concludes the\n3154\tchapter with a summary of findings and directions for future research.\n3155\tBy addressing feedback in the Imitate stage, Profy complements AIxSpeed (focused on\n3156\tthe Consume stage for audio) and FastPerson (focused on the Consume and Understand\n3157\tstages for video) presented in the previous chapters. Together, these three systems instantiate the proposed AI-Guided Learning framework. Profy’s contribution is a scalable,\n3158\tmodel-derived mechanism for skill practice. This chapter details Profy’s methodology,\n3159\timplementation, pronunciation evaluation, and possible̶but not yet evaluated̶extensions to other imitation-based skills.\n3160\t65\n3161\t\n3162\t\fFigure 5.1: Profy architecture: Overview of the system’s components and data flow.\n3163\t\n3164\t5.2\n3165\t\n3166\tSystem Design\n3167\t\n3168\t5.2.1 Overview\n3169\tProfy has three main functions:\n3170\t• Pronunciation Score: a measure of how well the user speaks.\n3171\t• Difference: different positions of the user’s speech and a model’s speech on the\n3172\twaveform.\n3173\t• Distance: two points on the two-dimensional coordinates that indicate how far the\n3174\tuser’s speech is from the model’s speech.\n3175\tThe proposed system uses mainly deep neural network techniques to achieve these functions, as shown in Figure 5.1. If the speech dataset contains values such as proficiency,\n3176\tdifference, and distance as annotations, then a simple supervised classifier can be trained\n3177\tto build a model that finds these values in the user’s speech.\n3178\tHowever, such annotated data must usually be manually created, which is expensive. To\n3179\taddress this problem, our system was designed to find the three desired values only from\n3180\traw (unannotated) speech data from non-native and native speakers of the target language.\n3181\tAs shown in Figure 5.1, the pronunciation score is calculated using the classification\n3182\tof the unannotated data, the difference is calculated using attention, and the distance is\n3183\tcalculated using metric learning. A further problem is the diﬀiculty of collecting speech\n3184\tdata from non-native speakers.\n3185\tIn contrast, speech data from native speakers can be easily collected from various sources,\n3186\tsuch as the radio, TV, and video streaming services [8, 136, 213]. For this reason, the\n3187\tsystem was designed to effectively use large amounts of data from native speakers using\n3188\tself-supervised learning so that it does not need to collect large amounts of speech data\n3189\tfrom non-native speakers.\n3190\tThe technical challenges in designing this system are:\n3191\tTP1 We need to train a deep learning model with minimal speech data from non-native\n3192\tspeakers.\n3193\tTP2 We need to obtain the desired values from speech data without human evaluation\n3194\tannotations.\n3195\t\n3196\t66\n3197\t\n3198\t\fFigure 5.2: Profy pronunciation score determination: Process of classifying speech data\n3199\tand calculating proficiency value.\n3200\t\n3201\t5.2.2 Pronunciation Score\n3202\tAs shown in Figure 5.2, the model for calculating the pronunciation score of a user’s\n3203\tspeech is trained to classify speech data from non-native and native speakers of the target\n3204\tlanguage. Importantly, this model learns the abstract concept of proficiency from data\n3205\twithout predefined rules. In this context, proficiency refers to the learnable acoustic and\n3206\ttemporal patterns that statistically differentiate native speaker utterances from those of\n3207\tnon-native speakers for a given phrase, rather than relying on explicit phonetic correctness rules or subjective perceptual judgments of clarity alone. The model aims to capture\n3208\ta holistic set of features that characterize well-formed target language pronunciation as\n3209\texemplified by a group of native speakers.\n3210\tThe model consists of an LSTM and a linear classifier for learning temporal features of\n3211\tspeech.\n3212\tThe linear classifier uses the LSTM’s hidden states H = [h1 , h2 , . . . , hT ] as input:\n3213\ts = W (S) Hα + b(S)\n3214\tp̂(y | X ) = softmax (s) ,\n3215\tŷ = arg max p̂(y | X ),\n3216\ty\n3217\t\n3218\t(5.1)\n3219\t(5.2)\n3220\t(5.3)\n3221\t\n3222\twhere W (S) and b(S) are trained parameters, and α is the attention weight introduced in\n3223\tSection 5.2.3.\n3224\tSubsequently, the calibrated value [130] that brings the output value of the model closer\n3225\tto the probability of belonging to each class is used as the proficiency value.\n3226\tHere, as shown in TP1, the problem of insuﬀicient training data for the classes of nonnative speakers arises. Recently, self-supervised learning, a method in which the model\n3227\tprovides teacher labels even if the data do not have them, has been actively studied [164,\n3228\t13, 109].\n3229\tTraining such a model on a large amount of unlabeled data and fine-tuning it with a\n3230\tsmall amount of labeled data is known to effectively perform downstream tasks [209]. In\n3231\tour system, a self-supervised learning model is trained on a large amount of unlabeled\n3232\tspeech data from native speakers, and then a binary classifier is trained on a small amount\n3233\tof labeled speech data from native and non-native speakers.\n3234\tWe use HuBERT [80] as a self-supervised model for training with a large amount of\n3235\t67\n3236\t\n3237\t\fspeech data from native speakers, obtaining latent representations Z from the input speech\n3238\tX.\n3239\t′ , z′\n3240\t′\n3241\tThen, the time series data [z1:k\n3242\tk+1:2k , . . . , zT −k+1:T ] obtained by combining the representations Z at each step k are fed into the LSTM.\n3243\t5.2.3 Difference\n3244\tSuppose that the non-native speakers’ speech data have human-rated labels indicating\n3245\twhich parts of the speech differ from that of native speakers. In this case, we can train\n3246\ta supervised learning model that inputs the speech data and outputs the parts that differ\n3247\tfrom the native speakers’ speech.\n3248\tHowever, as stated in TP2, such labeled data were not available for this study. Therefore,\n3249\tour system uses an attention mechanism to localize regions that contribute to classification from unannotated speech data. These regions are model-derived and were not\n3250\tindependently validated as phonetic error locations.\n3251\tAttention has been successfully used in various tasks, such as question answering, machine translation, and speech recognition [14, 39, 72], to improve model performance and\n3252\tthe interpretability of deep learning models. Specifically, it visualizes where in the data\n3253\tthe model focuses its attention when performing a particular task. The core idea is that if\n3254\tthe classifier (trained as described in Section 5.2.2) deems an utterance as “non-native”\n3255\tor “less proficient,” the attention weights will highlight the specific time-frames in the\n3256\tinput speech that contributed most to this classification. These highlighted segments are\n3257\tinterpreted as the regions where the learner’s pronunciation deviates most significantly\n3258\tfrom the learned patterns of native speech.\n3259\tAs shown in Figure 5.3, we apply this mechanism to the classifier to determine the parts\n3260\tof the data on which the classifier focuses when it determines that the learner is not\n3261\tsuﬀiciently proficient. The attention weight α, which indicates the importance of the\n3262\ttime-series feature, is calculated as follows:\n3263\te = tanh(W A1 H)\n3264\t\n3265\t(5.4)\n3266\t\n3267\tα = softmax(W A2 e)\n3268\t\n3269\t(5.5)\n3270\t\n3271\twhere W A1 and W A2 are trained parameters. The weight α takes the value [0, 1] ∈ R.\n3272\tThis value is standardized, and the waveform in the corresponding area is displayed in\n3273\tred when it exceeds a certain threshold.\n3274\tThe color is darker in segments that require greater attention to encourage the user to\n3275\tfocus on them.\n3276\t5.2.4 Distance\n3277\tBefore calculating the speech distance between the user and the native speakers, it is necessary to design an optimal distance function to measure it. One of the main approaches\n3278\tto learning the distance between data is triplet loss [75].\n3279\tTriplet loss is learned using the following three types of speech data, as shown in Figure 5.4:\n3280\t• Anchor: speech data from a non-native speaker.\n3281\t• Positive: speech data from a non-native speaker different from anchor.\n3282\t• Negative: speech data from a native speaker.\n3283\t\n3284\t68\n3285\t\n3286\t\fIn obtaining the distance function, the distance from the anchor data to the positive data\n3287\t(dap ) is trained to be smaller, while the distance from the anchor data to the negative data\n3288\t(dan ) is trained to be larger:\n3289\tLtriplet = [dap − dan + m]+ ,\n3290\t\n3291\t(5.6)\n3292\t\n3293\twhere m is the margin—that is, the desired difference between the anchor-positive and\n3294\tanchor-negative distances.\n3295\tThe learned distance function is then used to calculate the two-dimensional vector difference between the user’s speech data and the native speaker’s speech data. In this case,\n3296\tthe latter are the average of the native speaker’s speech included in the data. Finally, the\n3297\tuser’s and native speaker’s speech data are represented as points on the two-dimensional\n3298\tcoordinates, with the native speaker’s point at the center (Figure 5.4). The axes of this\n3299\tlearned two-dimensional space are not assigned specific phonetic or acoustic meanings.\n3300\tThe visualization therefore indicates relative proximity to or divergence from the average\n3301\tnative-speaker representation, rather than explaining which articulatory change a learner\n3302\tshould make.\n3303\tFor metric learning, we use a linear layer with 512 hidden units and an LSTM with 128dimensional hidden states.\n3304\tIn this way, the distance can be automatically calculated from the raw speech data, even\n3305\tif the data do not include a detailed human evaluation of how the native and non-native\n3306\tspeech data differ, which is the challenge described in TP2.\n3307\t\n3308\t5.3\n3309\t\n3310\tEnglish Learning Application\n3311\t\n3312\t5.3.1 Implementation\n3313\tWe developed a prototype Profy application to support Japanese people in learning to\n3314\tspeak American English. The model speakers are native speakers of standard American\n3315\tEnglish as defined in [61]. Two datasets were used to create this prototype.\n3316\tOne was LibriSpeech (960 h) [136], which was used as a large unlabeled dataset of English speech to train HuBERT. LibriSpeech consists of audiobook readings.\n3317\tThe other was UME-ERJ [122], a database of English texts read by Japanese students\n3318\tand native speakers of standard American English, which was used as a small amount of\n3319\tlabeled speech data. UME-ERJ contains 806 sentences spoken by 202 Japanese and 20\n3320\tAmerican speakers. This dataset was used to train the classifier.\n3321\tAll audio files in the two datasets were standardized to a single-channel 16000 Hz format\n3322\tand aligned to 4 s in length.\n3323\tWe trained the model with HuBERT using the LibriSpeech data to obtain common speech\n3324\tfeatures in English.\n3325\tThe attention layer used to visualize the differences was a 32-dimensional hidden layer.\n3326\tWe used LSTM with 128-dimensional hidden layers and a linear layer with 512 hidden\n3327\tlayers for metric learning for distance visualization. We used Dropout [177] to train these\n3328\tmodels with a probability of 0.5 to suppress overfitting.\n3329\tSince UME-ERJ is unbalanced, containing more speech data from Japanese than from\n3330\tAmerican English speakers, we used focal loss [108] as an error function to reduce the\n3331\teffect of the imbalance.\n3332\tThe optimization algorithm used Adam [99], with a batch size of 32 and a learning rate\n3333\tof 10−4 .\n3334\tWe also augmented the data to make them more tolerant of noise and to compensate\n3335\tfor the scarcity of data. The data augmentation included adding Gaussian noise in the\n3336\t\n3337\t69\n3338\t\n3339\t\fFigure 5.3: Profy difference visualization: Method for highlighting parts of speech that\n3340\tdiffer from native speakers.\n3341\tbackground, randomly increasing or decreasing the audio volume, randomly shifting the\n3342\tpitch, and silencing randomly selected audio parts.\n3343\tThese deep models were implemented using PyTorch [139] as the backend and an NVIDIA\n3344\tGeForce 1080Ti GPU. We then made it into a web application based on Flask, a microweb\n3345\tframework written in Python.\n3346\t5.3.2 Interface\n3347\tFigure 5.5 shows the Profy interface. The system displays the target sentence that the\n3348\t2 , the user\n3349\t2 . By playing back ⃝\n3350\t1 and the model speech ⃝\n3351\tuser is about to learn to utter ⃝\n3352\tcan listen to the correct pronunciation of the target sentence.\n3353\t3 . The Pause button\n3354\tThe user can input his/her speech into the system using the button ⃝\n3355\tcan be used to interrupt the input. After the user has finished inputting his/her speech,\n3356\tthe Analyze button is used to analyze it.\n3357\t4 and\n3358\tWhen the Analyze button is pressed, the degree of nativeness of the user’s speech ⃝\n3359\t6\n3360\t5\n3361\t⃝\n3362\t⃝\n3363\tbetween the model speech and the user’s speech are\n3364\tand distance\n3365\tthe difference\n3366\tdisplayed. The difference is displayed in red on the waveform. The red area is the part\n3367\tthat differs from the model speech, and the larger the difference, the darker the color.\n3368\tThe distance is displayed as two points on two-dimensional coordinates. The red point\n3369\tis the model’s speech, and the blue point is the user’s speech. The closer these points\n3370\tare, the closer the user’s speech is to the model speech. This visual representation allows\n3371\t70\n3372\t\n3373\t\fFigure 5.4: Profy distance visualization: Representation of speech data in twodimensional space using triplet loss.\n3374\tlearners to intuitively understand the degree of their improvement.\n3375\t5.3.3 Learning Process\n3376\tHere, we describe how a user can interact with the application to practice speaking the\n3377\ttarget language. The user’s practice process is shown in Figure 5.6.\n3378\tThe user speaks given sentences into the system. Then, the system evaluates the user’s\n3379\tutterance, gives a numerical value for the user’s pronunciation score, and visually shows\n3380\tthe difference and distance from the model utterance. The user repeatedly modifies his\n3381\tor her speech until the pronunciation score is satisfactory.\n3382\tBy repeating the sentence, the user can compare changes in the displayed difference and\n3383\tdistance. The waveform highlights localize regions emphasized by the classifier, but do\n3384\tnot by themselves identify the phonetic cause or prescribe a correction.\n3385\tEliminating the difference\n3386\tFigure 5.6 (upper panel) shows how the difference between the user’s speech and the\n3387\tmodel’s speech is eliminated as the user repeatedly modifies his or her speech. In the\n3388\twaveform of the user’s speech shown in the figure, the parts that differ from the model\n3389\tspeech are indicated in red.\n3390\tWhen the user first speaks the sentence “This was easy for us,” the system highlights\n3391\ta ). After the user repeats the sentence,\n3392\tregions corresponding to “this,” “eas,” and “for” (⃝\n3393\tb ).\n3394\tthe highlights corresponding to “eas” and “for” disappear (⃝\n3395\t\n3396\t71\n3397\t\n3398\t\fFigure 5.5: Profy user interface: Layout and components of the application’s main screen.\n3399\t\n3400\tFigure 5.6: Examples of Profy interactions: Demonstration of eliminating differences\n3401\tand shortening distances in user speech.\n3402\tFinally, after another repetition, the remaining highlight corresponding to “this” also disc .\n3403\tappears, as shown in ⃝\n3404\tShortening the distance\n3405\tFigure 5.6 (lower panel) shows how the distance between the user’s speech and the model’s\n3406\tspeech decreases as the user repeatedly revises his or her speech. The model speech and\n3407\tthe learner’s speech are represented by dots in a two-dimensional coordinate space.\n3408\tThe red dots represent the model speech, and the blue dots represent the learner’s speech.\n3409\tThe greater the distance between the dots, the more the user’s speech differs from the\n3410\tmodel speech. The user changes the pronunciation so that the distance between these\n3411\tpoints becomes shorter.\n3412\tIf the user’s dot moves closer to the model-speech dot across repetitions, the displayed\n3413\te ). If it moves elsewhere, the disd →⃝\n3414\tb and ⃝\n3415\ta →⃝\n3416\tmodel-derived distance decreases (⃝\n3417\tc ); the axes do not by themselves\n3418\tb\n3419\t⃝\n3420\ttance representation changes in another direction ( →⃝\n3421\t72\n3422\t\n3423\t\fdetermine whether a phonetic change is correct.\n3424\tThe visualization lets the user observe whether successive attempts move closer to the\n3425\tmodel point, although the coordinate axes themselves do not identify which phonetic\n3426\tfeature changed.\n3427\t\n3428\t5.4\n3429\t\n3430\tEvaluation\n3431\t\n3432\tWe conducted a user study to evaluate the learning effectiveness of speaking practice\n3433\tusing Profy. We examined whether the prototype application helped Japanese learners\n3434\timprove their English pronunciation. Ten individuals aged 24–35 years participated in\n3435\tthe experiment.\n3436\t5.4.1 Procedure\n3437\tThis experiment compared Profy with elicited imitation (EI), a method of learning speaking by having the user listen to a model speech and imitate it several times. On the other\n3438\thand, practice using our system provides feedback on the user’s pronunciation score and\n3439\tdifference and distance from model speech to help the user modify his or her speech\n3440\taccordingly.\n3441\tEach participant was presented with 10 sentences from TIMIT [60], a major English\n3442\tspeech corpus, and learned to speak half of them using EI and the other half using Profy’s\n3443\tmethod. To ensure that the effect estimation would not be affected by the level of diﬀiculty,\n3444\tthe sentences were divided into five-sentence sets A and B; half of the participants learned\n3445\tset A using Profy, and the other half learned set B using Profy.\n3446\tDuring the experiment, all participants’ utterances were recorded, and the first and last\n3447\tutterances of each sentence were used to estimate the learning effect.\n3448\tOnce the participants had completed the speaking practice with both methods, a brief\n3449\tquestionnaire was administered regarding their user experiences.\n3450\t5.4.2 Results\n3451\tLearning Effect\n3452\tTo compare the effectiveness of Profy with that of the standard EI method, we presented\n3453\tfive American raters with two sets of pre- and post-practice sentence pairs and asked\n3454\tthem to judge which sentence was more intelligible. The raters were blinded to the preand post-practice sentences.\n3455\tThe experimental results are shown in Figure 5.7. The y-axis indicates the percentage of\n3456\tthe user’s speech judged to be more intelligible when compared before and after learning.\n3457\tThe sentences practiced using both Profy and EI were found more intelligible than the\n3458\tpre-practice sentences.\n3459\tHowever, the sentences practiced using Profy showed a larger observed improvement in\n3460\tintelligibility than those practiced using EI.\n3461\tFurthermore, unlike EI, Profy had non-overlapping pre- and post-practice confidence\n3462\tintervals. The source paper did not report a direct paired significance test for this comparison.\n3463\tUser Experience and Questionnaires\n3464\tAfter the experiment, we conducted questionnaires with the following Likert scale questions about the Profy:\n3465\tQ1\n3466\t\n3467\tI found it easy to use Profy.\n3468\t73\n3469\t\n3470\t\fFigure 5.7: Comparison of post-practice intelligibility gains for users of Profy and standard EI method.\n3471\t\n3472\tFigure 5.8: Questionnaire results: User feedback on Profy’s ease of use and helpfulness\n3473\tof features.\n3474\tQ2\n3475\t\n3476\tI found the display of pronunciation score helpful.\n3477\t\n3478\tQ3\n3479\t\n3480\tI found the display of difference helpful.\n3481\t\n3482\tQ4\n3483\t\n3484\tI found the display of distance helpful.\n3485\t\n3486\tFor each question, we asked respondents to rate on a five-point scale of strongly agree,\n3487\tagree, neutral, disagree and strongly disagree. The questionnaire results are shown in\n3488\tFigure 5.8.\n3489\tAll participants found the proposed system easy to use (“agree”/“strongly agree”).\n3490\tMoreover, all participants found the presentation of the pronunciation score helpful.\n3491\tOn the other hand, while the majority found the presentation of differences and distances\n3492\tuseful, some answered “neutral” or “disagree” (one and two, respectively).\n3493\tIn summary, useful features in Profy’s functional assessment are pronunciation scores,\n3494\tdifferences, and distance, in that order. This result shows that learners evaluate feedback\n3495\tin order from the most intuitively understandable.\n3496\t\n3497\t74\n3498\t\n3499\t\f5.5\n3500\t\n3501\tDiscussion\n3502\t\n3503\t5.5.1 Effectiveness of Data-Driven Proficiency Learning\n3504\tOne of Profy’s technical contributions is learning a proficiency classifier end-to-end. Unlike approaches that rely on phoneme-level annotations and explicit linguistic rules, Profy\n3505\ttrains from unannotated utterance-level data.\n3506\tThis design may reduce annotation requirements, but the present study did not test adaptation to new languages, dialects, or non-speech imitation tasks. Such transfer requires\n3507\tnew data, models, and domain-specific validation.\n3508\tThe visualization of classifier-emphasized regions through an attention mechanism also\n3509\tsets Profy apart from overall-score-only feedback. It shows which regions contributed to\n3510\tthe model’s judgment, while not identifying their phonetic cause.\n3511\t5.5.2 Limitations and Future Improvements\n3512\tThe current implementation has several limitations. First, distance visualization is difficult for some users to understand. Because the axes of the learned two-dimensional\n3513\tcoordinates do not have directly interpretable acoustic meanings, the visualization does\n3514\tnot by itself explain how to correct an error.\n3515\tSecond, the system is currently limited to feedback at the single sentence level. In actual\n3516\tconversation, prosodic features at larger units such as intonation, rhythm, and stress are\n3517\talso important. In the future, it will be necessary to extend to a feedback system that also\n3518\tconsiders these suprasegmental features.\n3519\tThird, the current system only works with recorded audio and cannot provide real-time\n3520\tfeedback. By utilizing edge computing on mobile devices, real-time feedback could be\n3521\tenabled, providing a more natural learning experience.\n3522\tFourthly, and perhaps most significantly from a pedagogical standpoint, Profy highlights\n3523\tclassifier-relevant regions and displays an abstract acoustic distance, but it does not validate those regions as phonetic errors or provide guidance on how to correct them. The\n3524\tlearner is still responsible for interpreting the display and deducing any articulatory adjustment. Future work should critically explore validating the highlighted regions and\n3525\tintegrating corrective actions, for example by retrieving relevant native-speaker examples, linking to targeted phonetic exercises, or generating minimal pairs.\n3526\t5.5.3 Relation to Existing Language Learning Tools and CAPT Systems\n3527\tWhen comparing Profy to established language learning applications like Duolingo or\n3528\tBabbel, or other research-based CAPT systems, several distinctions and potential synergies emerge. Many commercial platforms (e.g., Duolingo, Busuu) offer comprehensive, gamified curricula covering various language skills (vocabulary, grammar, listening,\n3529\tspeaking). Their pronunciation feedback, if available, often relies on ASR to provide a\n3530\tgeneral correctness score for an utterance or identifies very coarse-grained errors at the\n3531\tword level, rather than pinpointing specific phonetic deviations within a word. While\n3532\teffective for motivation and broad practice, this level of feedback may be insuﬀicient for\n3533\tlearners seeking to refine specific aspects of their pronunciation.\n3534\tSpecialized CAPT systems often provide more detailed phonetic analysis (e.g., based on\n3535\tGoodness of Pronunciation (GOP) scores derived from forced alignment, or analysis of\n3536\tspecific acoustic features like formants or pitch contours) [200, 106]. However, these\n3537\tsystems frequently require: (a) accurately transcribed speech, (b) detailed annotations\n3538\tof target pronunciations, often at the phoneme level, (c) pre-defined linguistic rules for\n3539\terror detection, or (d) a single, reference native speaker model, which may not capture\n3540\tthe natural variability in pronunciation.\n3541\t75\n3542\t\n3543\t\fProfy’s approach offers several differentiating factors in this landscape:\n3544\t• Data-Driven Proficiency Learning from Largely Unannotated Data: Profy\n3545\tlearns the characteristics of proficient speech directly from audio examples of native speakers and contrasts them with learner speech, minimizing the need for extensive manual annotation or explicit rule-setting for every possible mispronunciation. This is particularly advantageous for scalability and adaptation to different\n3546\tlanguages or dialects.\n3547\t• Localized Visualization of Classifier-Relevant Regions: The attention-based\n3548\twaveform highlighting shows which segments of an utterance contributed most\n3549\tto its classification as non-native-like. This is more localized than an overall score,\n3550\tbut is not a phonetic error diagnosis.\n3551\t• Learning from a Group of Native Speakers: By training on data from multiple\n3552\tnative speakers, Profy has the potential to learn a more robust and representative\n3553\tmodel of target pronunciation, implicitly accommodating natural variations (although the current “Distance” visualization uses an average native representation\n3554\tas a target).\n3555\tIt is important to acknowledge that Profy, in its current form, is a specialized tool focused\n3556\ton model-derived feedback for relatively short, isolated utterances. It does not offer a\n3557\tcomplete language learning curriculum, personalized learning paths, or the gamification\n3558\telements found in many commercial applications. A promising avenue for future work\n3559\tlies in integrating Profy’s feedback mechanisms into broader language learning platforms\n3560\tafter further validation.\n3561\t5.5.4 Broader Applications Beyond Language Learning\n3562\tThe Profy framework suggests possible future research on other skills acquired by imitating expert performance. For example, related models might compare musical performances or movement data from experts and learners.\n3563\tIn sports or professional training, such systems would require domain-specific representations, labels or evaluation criteria, and user studies before claims about corrective feedback or learning effectiveness could be made.\n3564\tThe current pronunciation study supplies no evidence for effectiveness in music, sports,\n3565\tsurgery, or other professional skills; these are examples of research directions only.\n3566\tA transferable design principle is learning a performance classifier from examples rather\n3567\tthan encoding every rule manually, while still requiring expert validation of what the\n3568\tlearned representation means in each domain.\n3569\t\n3570\t5.6\n3571\t\n3572\tConclusion\n3573\t\n3574\tThis chapter has presented Profy, a language learning support system that addresses the\n3575\tchallenge of providing scalable feedback for learner skill assessment in the Imitate stage\n3576\tof the AI-Guided Learning framework. Profy uses deep learning-based speech processing to provide model-derived visual feedback on pronunciation; whether this improves\n3577\tthe eﬀiciency of skill acquisition requires further evaluation.\n3578\tThe key contributions of Profy are threefold. Firstly, it introduces a technical framework\n3579\tfor assessing learners’ speech proficiency and visualizing classifier-emphasized regions\n3580\tusing unannotated speech data without comparison to a specific native speaker. Particularly important is that it learns a proficiency classifier end-to-end and localizes regions\n3581\tthat influence the classifier output.\n3582\t76\n3583\t\n3584\t\fSecondly, Profy presents a language learning support system for non-native English speakers that provides visual feedback on pronunciation. By highlighting classifier-emphasized\n3585\twaveform regions and visualizing distances in latent space, it provides more localized\n3586\tmodel-derived information than systems that present only an overall score.\n3587\tThirdly, the user study with Japanese learners of English showed a larger observed intelligibility improvement for Profy than for conventional Elicited Imitation (EI). Unlike EI,\n3588\tthe pre- and post-practice confidence intervals did not overlap for Profy.\n3589\tDespite these promising results, it is important to acknowledge the limitations of the current implementation of Profy. The system’s performance may vary depending on factors\n3590\tsuch as the linguistic distance between the learner’s native language and the target language, the diversity of accents represented in the model speech data, and the complexity\n3591\tof the practiced sentences.\n3592\tLooking ahead, Profy holds considerable promise for applications beyond language learning. The core concept of learning proficiency from data and visualizing model-emphasized\n3593\tregions could be adapted to support skill acquisition in fields such as sports, music, and\n3594\tprofessional training, subject to domain-specific validation.\n3595\tFor instance, future systems could analyze an athlete’s movements or compare musical\n3596\tperformances with expert examples. These examples illustrate possible extensions; the\n3597\tpresent study does not provide evidence that Profy’s framework generalizes beyond pronunciation.\n3598\tWithin the broader scope of this thesis, Profy complements the other two systems presented: AIxSpeed, which focuses on optimizing audio playback speed, and FastPerson,\n3599\twhich addresses rapid comprehension of video content. While AIxSpeed and FastPerson\n3600\tenhance the eﬀiciency of content consumption, Profy optimizes the process of skill acquisition and feedback. Together, these three systems form a comprehensive approach to\n3601\tenhancing time eﬀiciency in learning through multimedia content, collectively contributing to a more effective and personalized learning experience across various stages of the\n3602\tlearning process.\n3603\tIn conclusion, Profy represents a significant advancement in the field of computer-assisted\n3604\tlanguage learning and, more broadly, in the development of eﬀicient skill acquisition\n3605\tsupport systems. By enabling learners to receive immediate, visual feedback on their\n3606\tpronunciation without the need for constant expert supervision, it addresses a critical\n3607\tchallenge in language education and opens new possibilities for self-directed learning.\n3608\tAs research in this area continues to evolve, future work should focus on expanding the\n3609\tsystem’s capabilities to cover more aspects of language learning, such as grammar and\n3610\tvocabulary acquisition, and exploring its application in other skill domains. Crucially,\n3611\tenhancing the feedback to include guidance on how to correct identified errors, alongside exploring pathways for practical deployment in educational settings and addressing\n3612\tassociated pedagogical and logistical considerations, will be vital steps towards realizing\n3613\tProfy’s full potential. Furthermore, integrating Profy with other learning technologies,\n3614\tsuch as virtual reality or augmented reality, could potentially create even more immersive\n3615\tand effective learning experiences.\n3616\tThe insights gained from this research have the potential to reshape how we approach\n3617\tskill acquisition in educational and professional settings, contributing to more eﬀicient\n3618\tand effective learning methods in our increasingly digital and globally connected world.\n3619\tIn particular, the approach of learning proficiency in a data-driven manner holds the\n3620\tpotential to become a new design principle for imitation learning support systems in\n3621\tvarious fields.\n3622\t\n3623\t77\n3624\t\n3625\t\fChapter 6\n3626\tDiscussion\n3627\t6.1\n3628\t\n3629\tChapter Overview\n3630\t\n3631\tThis chapter synthesizes how the three systems̶AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy̶collectively instantiate the Consume‒Understand‒Imitate (CUI) framework for audio‑visual\n3632\tlearning. We first provide a brief recap of CUI and the role of each system, then position the work within established learning theories. We next discuss how the systems\n3633\tcomplement one another, examine limitations and open questions, and conclude with a\n3634\tvision‑driven trajectory that extends beyond the three prototypes.\n3635\t\n3636\t6.2\n3637\t\n3638\tBrief Recap: CUI and the Three Systems\n3639\t\n3640\tIn this dissertation, CUI denotes a process model tailored to audio‑visual learning: (i)\n3641\tConsume̶eﬀicient access to content; (ii) Understand̶deep comprehension of multimodal material; and (iii) Imitate̶practice with feedback for skill acquisition. AIxSpeed\n3642\tsupports eﬀicient consumption (and early understanding) by adaptively adjusting playback speed based on acoustic features. FastPerson supports understanding by integrating\n3643\tlinguistic and visual cues and providing summaries that preserve the speaker’s voice and\n3644\tcontext. Profy supports imitation by analyzing performance against model patterns and\n3645\tproviding model-derived, localized feedback for practice.\n3646\t\n3647\t6.3\n3648\t\n3649\tPositioning in the Learning Sciences\n3650\t\n3651\tWe position the work within the learning sciences by relating CUI to established models\n3652\tand clarifying its scope for audio‑visual learning.\n3653\tRelation to established models. CUI partially aligns with (a) Kolb’s experiential cycle [100] (Consume ↔ concrete experience; Understand ↔ reflection and conceptualization; Imitate ↔ active experimentation), while recognizing Kolb’s cyclic nature (learners\n3654\tmay move back and forth) and individual differences; (b) Bloom’s revised taxonomy [6],\n3655\twhich is two-dimensional (knowledge × cognitive process) rather than a strict linear hierarchy, consistent with CUI’s cyclic use; and (c) ICAP [36], which classifies engagement\n3656\tby visible learner behaviors (e.g., notetaking, summarizing, self-explanation, interaction).\n3657\tOur systems may help shifts from Passive to Active, and in some uses toward Constructive, when such generative behaviors are present. Support for fully Interactive learning\n3658\tis limited and remains an avenue for future work.\n3659\tThis work reinterprets general learning theories for the characteristics of audio‑visual\n3660\tmedia and offers practical system design choices addressing challenges such as temporal\n3661\tineﬀiciency, multimodal integration, and feedback scarcity. Whereas prior theory emphasizes describing cognitive processes, this research materializes learning support as\n3662\timplementable systems.\n3663\t78\n3664\t\n3665\t\f6.4\n3666\t\n3667\tSynthesis: How the Systems Work Together\n3668\t\n3669\tThe three systems are designed to be complementary along the Consume→Understand→Imitate\n3670\tflow. A typical workflow is to summarize a long lecture with FastPerson (with seamless\n3671\taccess back to details), use AIxSpeed to increase listening eﬀiciency, and then connect to\n3672\tpractice with Profy using model-derived feedback. Together they support an end‑to‑end\n3673\tlearner workflow; understanding potential synergies and trade‑offs (e.g., cognitive load,\n3674\tcontrollability) requires further empirical study.\n3675\tFurthermore, this research can be situated with respect to Chi and Wylie’s ICAP framework [36] (Passive, Active, Constructive, Interactive). Because ICAP is defined by visible\n3676\tlearner behaviors, AIxSpeed and FastPerson may support Passive→Active shifts when\n3677\tlearners engage in generative actions (e.g., notetaking, summarizing, self‑explanation),\n3678\tand Profy may prompt Constructive activity via feedback on learner productions. Support\n3679\tfor the Interactive level (collaborative knowledge construction) is limited and remains future work.\n3680\t\n3681\t6.5\n3682\t\n3683\tRedefining Learning in the Digital Age: Learner Autonomy and the\n3684\tAI-Guided Consume‒Understand‒Imitate Framework\n3685\t\n3686\tThe AI‑Guided Learning framework proposed in this research goes beyond system design considerations to discuss fundamental questions about the nature of learning. The\n3687\tlong‑standing idea of widening learner autonomy and access (e.g., “learning webs” [82])\n3688\tgains new practical avenues through AI. This section describes how the Consume‒Understand‒Imitate (CUI) cycle and the three systems̶AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy̶\n3689\taddress challenges such as the ineﬀiciency of long‑form content, the diﬀiculty of understanding multimodal information, and the lack of feedback in imitation‑based learning,\n3690\tthereby supporting learner autonomy and broadening access to expert‑like guidance.\n3691\tThe traditional school system has been described as potentially commodifying knowledge\n3692\tand transforming learning into an act of consumption, thereby constraining spontaneous\n3693\tlearning capacities. In contrast, concepts like “learning webs” proposed networks where\n3694\tlearners could access knowledge resources based on their interests, meet peers, and learn\n3695\tfrom experts. The AI-Guided Learning and CUI framework in this research can be positioned as a reinterpretation of this vision for the digital age, implemented as a concrete\n3696\tset of technological systems aiming to solve challenges specific to audio-visual learning.\n3697\tAIxSpeed supports eﬀicient information intake (Consume), FastPerson facilitates deep\n3698\tmultimodal understanding (Understand), and Profy aids in the imitation and refinement\n3699\tof practical skills (Imitate), thereby strengthening the entire learning cycle.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6af2b770-4e64-4fd8-b01f-b5667819ace1","uuid":"8839131b-cab5-4a9d-afd2-42e53bd5fb9d","timestamp":"2026-08-11T17:26:44.732Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/full.txt","content":"be addressed by improving the implementation and replacing it with a higher quality\nspeech synthesis method. In particular, adopting the latest neural voice cloning technology could potentially generate more natural synthesized speech.\nOther users also pointed out the need for a more intuitive user interface because they\nfound it diﬀicult to understand the functionality of the various features. In addition, we\nobserved that some users prefer the original simple video playback player, and we believe\nthat simplifying the overall structure of FastPerson can help improve user accessibility.\n\n60\n\n\f4.5.3 Technological Enhancements and Future Directions\nFastPerson extracts visual elements to be synthesized with the summarized audio from the\noriginal video. Using a text-to-video model, other methods can be employed to generate\na video from the summarized text; Runway’s Gen-2 and OpenAI’s Sora are possible\ncandidates for these methods.\nThese methods focus on generating object-centered videos, and therefore, it currently\nrequires more work to create lecture videos that are text-heavy and slide-centered. However, it may be possible to create helpful summary videos for generating lecture videos\nfocused on some topics.\nFor example, if a lecture is centered around the intricacies of plant biology, a text-tovideo model could be used to generate video segments illustrating key concepts such as\nphotosynthesis, cellular structures of plants, or growth cycles. This approach would not\nonly make the content more accessible but also more engaging for viewers, especially for\nvisual learners.\nAdditionally, future developments should consider creating adaptive summarization systems that automatically adjust the level of detail in summaries based on users’ learning\nhistory and comprehension levels. By analyzing user learning patterns using machine\nlearning and generating summaries optimized for individual needs, more effective personalized learning could be achieved.\n4.5.4 Broader Implications and Applications of FastPerson\nThe broader implications of FastPerson go beyond its immediate application in educational video summarization. FastPerson has the potential to revolutionize how we interact with a wide range of video content. For example, in professional settings, FastPerson\ncould be used to distill long training sessions or meetings into concise summaries, allowing for eﬀicient review and better time management.\nFurther, FastPerson has potential applications related to media consumption, where users\ncan quickly view news or entertainment highlights. In addition, its application could extend to content creators and marketers, giving them a video editing tool to create engaging,\nsummarized versions of their content for better audience engagement.\nFastPerson’s adaptability could enable its integration into various platforms, making information more accessible and digestible for a broader audience. In particular, its application is expected in diverse fields such as news distribution, corporate training, online\neducation, and even the entertainment industry. This new paradigm of “video-to-video\nsummarization” could become a new standard for eﬀicient information consumption in\nthe age of information overload.\n\n4.6\n\nConclusion\n\nThis chapter has presented FastPerson, an innovative video summarization system that\naddresses the challenge of enabling rapid comprehension of video content using visual\ninformation in the Consume-Understand stages, which is one of the three primary objectives of this thesis aimed at achieving time-eﬀicient learning through audio and visual\ncontent under the AI-Guided Learning framework. FastPerson demonstrates a novel approach to video summarization by comprehensively considering both visual and auditory\ninformation in lecture videos, thereby enhancing the eﬀiciency of the learning experience.\nThe key contributions of FastPerson are threefold. Firstly, it introduces a comprehensive\nvideo summarization method that generates summary videos reflecting important information from both visual and audio elements of lecture videos. Particularly, it establishes\n\n61\n\n\fa new paradigm of “video-to-video summarization,” preserving context and speaker characteristics that are often lost in conventional text-based summarization. Secondly, FastPerson presents a user-centered learning experience design that enables seamless switching between original and summary videos. Reproducing the original speaker’s voice\nthrough voice cloning supports continuity between the two versions. Thirdly, the system\ndemonstrates empirically that it significantly reduces viewing time, with no statistically\nsignificant difference in quiz-measured comprehension from conventional playback.\nThe experimental results presented in this chapter provide evidence for the eﬀicacy of\nFastPerson. The evaluation experiments indicated no significant difference in video comprehension when using FastPerson compared to conventional video playback methods.\nCrucially, FastPerson reduced viewing time by approximately 53%. This substantial time\nsaving, together with the absence of a statistically significant quiz-score difference, indicates the system’s potential to enhance the eﬀiciency of video-based learning. Furthermore, 78% of FastPerson users rated the ability to switch between summary and original\nvideos as useful or very useful.\nDespite these promising results, it is important to acknowledge the limitations of the\ncurrent implementation of FastPerson. The evaluation results indicate room for improvement in terms of the depth of the summary content and the quality of the audio in the\ngenerated summaries. These areas present opportunities for future research and development. Additionally, further investigation is needed to assess the long-term effects of\nusing FastPerson on knowledge retention and learning outcomes across various subject\nareas and learner demographics.\nLooking ahead, FastPerson holds considerable promise for a variety of applications in\neducational technology and beyond. In the context of online learning platforms and\nMOOCs, it could significantly enhance the learning experience by allowing students to\nquickly grasp the key points of lengthy lecture videos and eﬀiciently review course materials. In corporate training scenarios, FastPerson could help employees quickly assimilate\ninformation from training videos, potentially improving the eﬀiciency of professional development programs.\nFurthermore, the system’s approach to integrating visual and auditory information could\nhave applications in fields such as multimedia content analysis and automated video captioning. In particular, the innovative approach of “video-to-video summarization” could\nopen new applications that were not possible with conventional text-based summarization.\nWithin the broader scope of this thesis, FastPerson complements the other two systems\npresented: AIxSpeed, which focuses on optimizing audio playback speed, and Profy,\nwhich addresses eﬀicient feedback systems for skill acquisition. While AIxSpeed enhances the auditory aspect of content consumption and Profy facilitates interactive learning, FastPerson optimizes the visual component of educational videos. Together, these\nthree systems form a comprehensive approach to enhancing time eﬀiciency in learning\nthrough multimedia content, collectively contributing to a more effective and personalized learning experience.\nIn conclusion, FastPerson represents an advancement in video summarization technology\nand its application to educational contexts. By reducing viewing time without a statistically significant difference in quiz-measured comprehension in the present experiment, it\naddresses a critical challenge in the era of abundant online video content. As research in\nthis area continues to evolve, future work should focus on refining the summary generation algorithms, improving the quality of synthesized audio, and exploring the integration\nof FastPerson with other learning technologies.\nFurthermore, longitudinal studies to assess the impact of FastPerson on long-term learning outcomes and its effectiveness across different subject domains would provide valuable insights. The findings from this research have the potential to reshape how we ap62\n\n\fproach video-based learning and content consumption in educational and professional\nsettings, contributing to more eﬀicient and effective knowledge acquisition in our increasingly digital world.\n\n63\n\n\fChapter 5\nProfy: Pronunciation Evaluation and Visualization\nof Differences from Native Speaker Groups Using\nUnannotated Audio Data\n5.1\n\nIntroduction\n\nThis chapter presents Profy, a skill acquisition feedback system in the Imitate stage toward realizing AI-Guided Learning, the overall goal of this thesis. As discussed in Chapter 1, it is diﬀicult for learners to evaluate differences between their own performance\nand expert models in imitation-based learning. Profy addresses this challenge by learning a proficiency classifier from small amounts of unannotated audio data and visualizing\nwaveform regions emphasized by that classifier.\nThe core innovation of Profy lies in its ability to learn a proficiency classifier end-toend without specific predefined rules or detailed annotations. While many conventional\nCAPT (Computer-Assisted Pronunciation Training) systems use detailed phoneme-level\nannotations or linguistic expertise, Profy learns a classifier and visualizes model-emphasized\nregions from small amounts of unannotated data. Pronunciation proficiency is a cornerstone of effective communication in a foreign language, yet it remains one of the most\nchallenging aspects for learners in online environments due to the lack of scalable, individualized feedback [159]. Profy specifically targets this bottleneck within the broader\nscope of AI-Guided Learning. By enabling data-driven assessment and detailed feedback on pronunciation, Profy aims to make the skill acquisition phase of language learning more eﬀicient and effective, thereby contributing to the overall goal of optimizing\nonline learning experiences. The “proficiency” learned by Profy primarily refers to the\nacoustic characteristics that distinguish native-like speech from beginner speech in the\ncontext of target utterance repetition, rather than evaluating the semantic content or the\npedagogical clarity of, for instance, a lecturer’s intentional slow speech, which might be\na consideration for systems like AIxSpeed.\nImportantly, Profy’s methodology may extend beyond pronunciation learning. The framework of learning proficiency from data and visualizing model-emphasized regions could\nbe investigated for skills acquired through imitating expert performance, such as sports\nform correction, musical instrument instruction, and professional skill acquisition. This\nchapter evaluates pronunciation only; transfer to other domains remains future work.\nLearning a non-native language presents numerous challenges, particularly in the domain\nof speaking proficiency. The acquisition of proper pronunciation is a critical aspect of\nlanguage learning, as it significantly impacts communication effectiveness and overall\nlanguage competence. Traditional methods of pronunciation instruction often rely heavily on direct feedback from native speakers or language experts. However, this approach\nis limited by the availability of such experts and the time-intensive nature of individual\nfeedback sessions.\n64\n\n\fTo address these limitations, there has been a growing interest in the development of\nComputer-Assisted Pronunciation Training (CAPT) systems. Existing CAPT systems\nhave made significant strides in automating pronunciation feedback. These systems typically compare the user’s speech with that of a specific native speaker as a model, analyzing units of rhythm, phonemes, or words to calculate differences [200, 106, 146].\nWhile effective, these approaches face several limitations. Firstly, they often require extensive speech data with detailed annotations, which can be costly and time-consuming\nto produce. Secondly, many systems are limited to comparisons with a single specific\nnative speaker, potentially narrowing the range of acceptable pronunciations. Thirdly,\nthe development of such systems for new languages or dialects can be challenging due to\nthe need for specialized linguistic expertise and resources.\nTo address these challenges, this chapter introduces Profy, a language learning support\nsystem that calculates model-derived speech scores from a small amount of unannotated\nspeech data without comparison to a specific person. Profy displays a proficiency score,\nclassifier-emphasized waveform regions, and distances between learner speech and a\ngroup-level native-speech representation.\nThis approach lets learners compare how the model-derived score, highlighted regions,\nand distance change across repeated attempts. The attention mechanism highlights regions that contributed to the classifier output, providing more localized information than\nan overall score; it does not by itself establish that a region is a phonetic error or explain\nhow to correct it.\nThe key contributions of Profy are threefold:\n1. A technical framework for assessing learners’ speech proficiency using unannotated speech data. It (a) learns to distinguish acoustic patterns in native and learner\nspeech for specific utterances in an end-to-end, data-driven manner, without explicit rules for each target phoneme or prosodic element, and (b) visualizes classifieremphasized regions through an attention mechanism, moving beyond a holistic\nscore while not claiming phonetic error diagnosis.\n2. A language learning support system for non-native English speakers that lets learners inspect how model-derived waveform highlights and latent-space distances\nchange across repeated attempts.\n3. Empirical evidence from a user study in which the Profy condition showed a larger\nobserved intelligibility improvement than elicited imitation (EI), with non-overlapping\npre- and post-practice confidence intervals within the Profy condition.\nThese contributions address pronunciation-learning feedback within the broader objectives of this thesis. The present chapter does not measure practice-time eﬀiciency or\nestablish generalization to imitation tasks beyond pronunciation.\nThe remainder of this chapter is organized as follows: Section 5.2 provides an overview\nof related work in Computer-Assisted Language Learning (CALL) and pronunciation assessment. Section 5.3 details the design and implementation of the Profy system. Section\n5.4 presents the evaluation methodology and experimental setup. Section 5.5 discusses\nthe results of the user study and their implications. Finally, Section 5.6 concludes the\nchapter with a summary of findings and directions for future research.\nBy addressing feedback in the Imitate stage, Profy complements AIxSpeed (focused on\nthe Consume stage for audio) and FastPerson (focused on the Consume and Understand\nstages for video) presented in the previous chapters. Together, these three systems instantiate the proposed AI-Guided Learning framework. Profy’s contribution is a scalable,\nmodel-derived mechanism for skill practice. This chapter details Profy’s methodology,\nimplementation, pronunciation evaluation, and possible̶but not yet evaluated̶extensions to other imitation-based skills.\n65\n\n\fFigure 5.1: Profy architecture: Overview of the system’s components and data flow.\n\n5.2\n\nSystem Design\n\n5.2.1 Overview\nProfy has three main functions:\n• Pronunciation Score: a measure of how well the user speaks.\n• Difference: different positions of the user’s speech and a model’s speech on the\nwaveform.\n• Distance: two points on the two-dimensional coordinates that indicate how far the\nuser’s speech is from the model’s speech.\nThe proposed system uses mainly deep neural network techniques to achieve these functions, as shown in Figure 5.1. If the speech dataset contains values such as proficiency,\ndifference, and distance as annotations, then a simple supervised classifier can be trained\nto build a model that finds these values in the user’s speech.\nHowever, such annotated data must usually be manually created, which is expensive. To\naddress this problem, our system was designed to find the three desired values only from\nraw (unannotated) speech data from non-native and native speakers of the target language.\nAs shown in Figure 5.1, the pronunciation score is calculated using the classification\nof the unannotated data, the difference is calculated using attention, and the distance is\ncalculated using metric learning. A further problem is the diﬀiculty of collecting speech\ndata from non-native speakers.\nIn contrast, speech data from native speakers can be easily collected from various sources,\nsuch as the radio, TV, and video streaming services [8, 136, 213]. For this reason, the\nsystem was designed to effectively use large amounts of data from native speakers using\nself-supervised learning so that it does not need to collect large amounts of speech data\nfrom non-native speakers.\nThe technical challenges in designing this system are:\nTP1 We need to train a deep learning model with minimal speech data from non-native\nspeakers.\nTP2 We need to obtain the desired values from speech data without human evaluation\nannotations.\n\n66\n\n\fFigure 5.2: Profy pronunciation score determination: Process of classifying speech data\nand calculating proficiency value.\n\n5.2.2 Pronunciation Score\nAs shown in Figure 5.2, the model for calculating the pronunciation score of a user’s\nspeech is trained to classify speech data from non-native and native speakers of the target\nlanguage. Importantly, this model learns the abstract concept of proficiency from data\nwithout predefined rules. In this context, proficiency refers to the learnable acoustic and\ntemporal patterns that statistically differentiate native speaker utterances from those of\nnon-native speakers for a given phrase, rather than relying on explicit phonetic correctness rules or subjective perceptual judgments of clarity alone. The model aims to capture\na holistic set of features that characterize well-formed target language pronunciation as\nexemplified by a group of native speakers.\nThe model consists of an LSTM and a linear classifier for learning temporal features of\nspeech.\nThe linear classifier uses the LSTM’s hidden states H = [h1 , h2 , . . . , hT ] as input:\ns = W (S) Hα + b(S)\np̂(y | X ) = softmax (s) ,\nŷ = arg max p̂(y | X ),\ny\n\n(5.1)\n(5.2)\n(5.3)\n\nwhere W (S) and b(S) are trained parameters, and α is the attention weight introduced in\nSection 5.2.3.\nSubsequently, the calibrated value [130] that brings the output value of the model closer\nto the probability of belonging to each class is used as the proficiency value.\nHere, as shown in TP1, the problem of insuﬀicient training data for the classes of nonnative speakers arises. Recently, self-supervised learning, a method in which the model\nprovides teacher labels even if the data do not have them, has been actively studied [164,\n13, 109].\nTraining such a model on a large amount of unlabeled data and fine-tuning it with a\nsmall amount of labeled data is known to effectively perform downstream tasks [209]. In\nour system, a self-supervised learning model is trained on a large amount of unlabeled\nspeech data from native speakers, and then a binary classifier is trained on a small amount\nof labeled speech data from native and non-native speakers.\nWe use HuBERT [80] as a self-supervised model for training with a large amount of\n67\n\n\fspeech data from native speakers, obtaining latent representations Z from the input speech\nX.\n′ , z′\n′\nThen, the time series data [z1:k\nk+1:2k , . . . , zT −k+1:T ] obtained by combining the representations Z at each step k are fed into the LSTM.\n5.2.3 Difference\nSuppose that the non-native speakers’ speech data have human-rated labels indicating\nwhich parts of the speech differ from that of native speakers. In this case, we can train\na supervised learning model that inputs the speech data and outputs the parts that differ\nfrom the native speakers’ speech.\nHowever, as stated in TP2, such labeled data were not available for this study. Therefore,\nour system uses an attention mechanism to localize regions that contribute to classification from unannotated speech data. These regions are model-derived and were not\nindependently validated as phonetic error locations.\nAttention has been successfully used in various tasks, such as question answering, machine translation, and speech recognition [14, 39, 72], to improve model performance and\nthe interpretability of deep learning models. Specifically, it visualizes where in the data\nthe model focuses its attention when performing a particular task. The core idea is that if\nthe classifier (trained as described in Section 5.2.2) deems an utterance as “non-native”\nor “less proficient,” the attention weights will highlight the specific time-frames in the\ninput speech that contributed most to this classification. These highlighted segments are\ninterpreted as the regions where the learner’s pronunciation deviates most significantly\nfrom the learned patterns of native speech.\nAs shown in Figure 5.3, we apply this mechanism to the classifier to determine the parts\nof the data on which the classifier focuses when it determines that the learner is not\nsuﬀiciently proficient. The attention weight α, which indicates the importance of the\ntime-series feature, is calculated as follows:\ne = tanh(W A1 H)\n\n(5.4)\n\nα = softmax(W A2 e)\n\n(5.5)\n\nwhere W A1 and W A2 are trained parameters. The weight α takes the value [0, 1] ∈ R.\nThis value is standardized, and the waveform in the corresponding area is displayed in\nred when it exceeds a certain threshold.\nThe color is darker in segments that require greater attention to encourage the user to\nfocus on them.\n5.2.4 Distance\nBefore calculating the speech distance between the user and the native speakers, it is necessary to design an optimal distance function to measure it. One of the main approaches\nto learning the distance between data is triplet loss [75].\nTriplet loss is learned using the following three types of speech data, as shown in Figure 5.4:\n• Anchor: speech data from a non-native speaker.\n• Positive: speech data from a non-native speaker different from anchor.\n• Negative: speech data from a native speaker.\n\n68\n\n\fIn obtaining the distance function, the distance from the anchor data to the positive data\n(dap ) is trained to be smaller, while the distance from the anchor data to the negative data\n(dan ) is trained to be larger:\nLtriplet = [dap − dan + m]+ ,\n\n(5.6)\n\nwhere m is the margin—that is, the desired difference between the anchor-positive and\nanchor-negative distances.\nThe learned distance function is then used to calculate the two-dimensional vector difference between the user’s speech data and the native speaker’s speech data. In this case,\nthe latter are the average of the native speaker’s speech included in the data. Finally, the\nuser’s and native speaker’s speech data are represented as points on the two-dimensional\ncoordinates, with the native speaker’s point at the center (Figure 5.4). The axes of this\nlearned two-dimensional space are not assigned specific phonetic or acoustic meanings.\nThe visualization therefore indicates relative proximity to or divergence from the average\nnative-speaker representation, rather than explaining which articulatory change a learner\nshould make.\nFor metric learning, we use a linear layer with 512 hidden units and an LSTM with 128dimensional hidden states.\nIn this way, the distance can be automatically calculated from the raw speech data, even\nif the data do not include a detailed human evaluation of how the native and non-native\nspeech data differ, which is the challenge described in TP2.\n\n5.3\n\nEnglish Learning Application\n\n5.3.1 Implementation\nWe developed a prototype Profy application to support Japanese people in learning to\nspeak American English. The model speakers are native speakers of standard American\nEnglish as defined in [61]. Two datasets were used to create this prototype.\nOne was LibriSpeech (960 h) [136], which was used as a large unlabeled dataset of English speech to train HuBERT. LibriSpeech consists of audiobook readings.\nThe other was UME-ERJ [122], a database of English texts read by Japanese students\nand native speakers of standard American English, which was used as a small amount of\nlabeled speech data. UME-ERJ contains 806 sentences spoken by 202 Japanese and 20\nAmerican speakers. This dataset was used to train the classifier.\nAll audio files in the two datasets were standardized to a single-channel 16000 Hz format\nand aligned to 4 s in length.\nWe trained the model with HuBERT using the LibriSpeech data to obtain common speech\nfeatures in English.\nThe attention layer used to visualize the differences was a 32-dimensional hidden layer.\nWe used LSTM with 128-dimensional hidden layers and a linear layer with 512 hidden\nlayers for metric learning for distance visualization. We used Dropout [177] to train these\nmodels with a probability of 0.5 to suppress overfitting.\nSince UME-ERJ is unbalanced, containing more speech data from Japanese than from\nAmerican English speakers, we used focal loss [108] as an error function to reduce the\neffect of the imbalance.\nThe optimization algorithm used Adam [99], with a batch size of 32 and a learning rate\nof 10−4 .\nWe also augmented the data to make them more tolerant of noise and to compensate\nfor the scarcity of data. The data augmentation included adding Gaussian noise in the\n\n69\n\n\fFigure 5.3: Profy difference visualization: Method for highlighting parts of speech that\ndiffer from native speakers.\nbackground, randomly increasing or decreasing the audio volume, randomly shifting the\npitch, and silencing randomly selected audio parts.\nThese deep models were implemented using PyTorch [139] as the backend and an NVIDIA\nGeForce 1080Ti GPU. We then made it into a web application based on Flask, a microweb\nframework written in Python.\n5.3.2 Interface\nFigure 5.5 shows the Profy interface. The system displays the target sentence that the\n2 , the user\n2 . By playing back ⃝\n1 and the model speech ⃝\nuser is about to learn to utter ⃝\ncan listen to the correct pronunciation of the target sentence.\n3 . The Pause button\nThe user can input his/her speech into the system using the button ⃝\ncan be used to interrupt the input. After the user has finished inputting his/her speech,\nthe Analyze button is used to analyze it.\n4 and\nWhen the Analyze button is pressed, the degree of nativeness of the user’s speech ⃝\n6\n5\n⃝\n⃝\nbetween the model speech and the user’s speech are\nand distance\nthe difference\ndisplayed. The difference is displayed in red on the waveform. The red area is the part\nthat differs from the model speech, and the larger the difference, the darker the color.\nThe distance is displayed as two points on two-dimensional coordinates. The red point\nis the model’s speech, and the blue point is the user’s speech. The closer these points\nare, the closer the user’s speech is to the model speech. This visual representation allows\n70\n\n\fFigure 5.4: Profy distance visualization: Representation of speech data in twodimensional space using triplet loss.\nlearners to intuitively understand the degree of their improvement.\n5.3.3 Learning Process\nHere, we describe how a user can interact with the application to practice speaking the\ntarget language. The user’s practice process is shown in Figure 5.6.\nThe user speaks given sentences into the system. Then, the system evaluates the user’s\nutterance, gives a numerical value for the user’s pronunciation score, and visually shows\nthe difference and distance from the model utterance. The user repeatedly modifies his\nor her speech until the pronunciation score is satisfactory.\nBy repeating the sentence, the user can compare changes in the displayed difference and\ndistance. The waveform highlights localize regions emphasized by the classifier, but do\nnot by themselves identify the phonetic cause or prescribe a correction.\nEliminating the difference\nFigure 5.6 (upper panel) shows how the difference between the user’s speech and the\nmodel’s speech is eliminated as the user repeatedly modifies his or her speech. In the\nwaveform of the user’s speech shown in the figure, the parts that differ from the model\nspeech are indicated in red.\nWhen the user first speaks the sentence “This was easy for us,” the system highlights\na ). After the user repeats the sentence,\nregions corresponding to “this,” “eas,” and “for” (⃝\nb ).\nthe highlights corresponding to “eas” and “for” disappear (⃝\n\n71\n\n\fFigure 5.5: Profy user interface: Layout and components of the application’s main screen.\n\nFigure 5.6: Examples of Profy interactions: Demonstration of eliminating differences\nand shortening distances in user speech.\nFinally, after another repetition, the remaining highlight corresponding to “this” also disc .\nappears, as shown in ⃝\nShortening the distance\nFigure 5.6 (lower panel) shows how the distance between the user’s speech and the model’s\nspeech decreases as the user repeatedly revises his or her speech. The model speech and\nthe learner’s speech are represented by dots in a two-dimensional coordinate space.\nThe red dots represent the model speech, and the blue dots represent the learner’s speech.\nThe greater the distance between the dots, the more the user’s speech differs from the\nmodel speech. The user changes the pronunciation so that the distance between these\npoints becomes shorter.\nIf the user’s dot moves closer to the model-speech dot across repetitions, the displayed\ne ). If it moves elsewhere, the disd →⃝\nb and ⃝\na →⃝\nmodel-derived distance decreases (⃝\nc ); the axes do not by themselves\nb\n⃝\ntance representation changes in another direction ( →⃝\n72\n\n\fdetermine whether a phonetic change is correct.\nThe visualization lets the user observe whether successive attempts move closer to the\nmodel point, although the coordinate axes themselves do not identify which phonetic\nfeature changed.\n\n5.4\n\nEvaluation\n\nWe conducted a user study to evaluate the learning effectiveness of speaking practice\nusing Profy. We examined whether the prototype application helped Japanese learners\nimprove their English pronunciation. Ten individuals aged 24–35 years participated in\nthe experiment.\n5.4.1 Procedure\nThis experiment compared Profy with elicited imitation (EI), a method of learning speaking by having the user listen to a model speech and imitate it several times. On the other\nhand, practice using our system provides feedback on the user’s pronunciation score and\ndifference and distance from model speech to help the user modify his or her speech\naccordingly.\nEach participant was presented with 10 sentences from TIMIT [60], a major English\nspeech corpus, and learned to speak half of them using EI and the other half using Profy’s\nmethod. To ensure that the effect estimation would not be affected by the level of diﬀiculty,\nthe sentences were divided into five-sentence sets A and B; half of the participants learned\nset A using Profy, and the other half learned set B using Profy.\nDuring the experiment, all participants’ utterances were recorded, and the first and last\nutterances of each sentence were used to estimate the learning effect.\nOnce the participants had completed the speaking practice with both methods, a brief\nquestionnaire was administered regarding their user experiences.\n5.4.2 Results\nLearning Effect\nTo compare the effectiveness of Profy with that of the standard EI method, we presented\nfive American raters with two sets of pre- and post-practice sentence pairs and asked\nthem to judge which sentence was more intelligible. The raters were blinded to the preand post-practice sentences.\nThe experimental results are shown in Figure 5.7. The y-axis indicates the percentage of\nthe user’s speech judged to be more intelligible when compared before and after learning.\nThe sentences practiced using both Profy and EI were found more intelligible than the\npre-practice sentences.\nHowever, the sentences practiced using Profy showed a larger observed improvement in\nintelligibility than those practiced using EI.\nFurthermore, unlike EI, Profy had non-overlapping pre- and post-practice confidence\nintervals. The source paper did not report a direct paired significance test for this comparison.\nUser Experience and Questionnaires\nAfter the experiment, we conducted questionnaires with the following Likert scale questions about the Profy:\nQ1\n\nI found it easy to use Profy.\n73\n\n\fFigure 5.7: Comparison of post-practice intelligibility gains for users of Profy and standard EI method.\n\nFigure 5.8: Questionnaire results: User feedback on Profy’s ease of use and helpfulness\nof features.\nQ2\n\nI found the display of pronunciation score helpful.\n\nQ3\n\nI found the display of difference helpful.\n\nQ4\n\nI found the display of distance helpful.\n\nFor each question, we asked respondents to rate on a five-point scale of strongly agree,\nagree, neutral, disagree and strongly disagree. The questionnaire results are shown in\nFigure 5.8.\nAll participants found the proposed system easy to use (“agree”/“strongly agree”).\nMoreover, all participants found the presentation of the pronunciation score helpful.\nOn the other hand, while the majority found the presentation of differences and distances\nuseful, some answered “neutral” or “disagree” (one and two, respectively).\nIn summary, useful features in Profy’s functional assessment are pronunciation scores,\ndifferences, and distance, in that order. This result shows that learners evaluate feedback\nin order from the most intuitively understandable.\n\n74\n\n\f5.5\n\nDiscussion\n\n5.5.1 Effectiveness of Data-Driven Proficiency Learning\nOne of Profy’s technical contributions is learning a proficiency classifier end-to-end. Unlike approaches that rely on phoneme-level annotations and explicit linguistic rules, Profy\ntrains from unannotated utterance-level data.\nThis design may reduce annotation requirements, but the present study did not test adaptation to new languages, dialects, or non-speech imitation tasks. Such transfer requires\nnew data, models, and domain-specific validation.\nThe visualization of classifier-emphasized regions through an attention mechanism also\nsets Profy apart from overall-score-only feedback. It shows which regions contributed to\nthe model’s judgment, while not identifying their phonetic cause.\n5.5.2 Limitations and Future Improvements\nThe current implementation has several limitations. First, distance visualization is difficult for some users to understand. Because the axes of the learned two-dimensional\ncoordinates do not have directly interpretable acoustic meanings, the visualization does\nnot by itself explain how to correct an error.\nSecond, the system is currently limited to feedback at the single sentence level. In actual\nconversation, prosodic features at larger units such as intonation, rhythm, and stress are\nalso important. In the future, it will be necessary to extend to a feedback system that also\nconsiders these suprasegmental features.\nThird, the current system only works with recorded audio and cannot provide real-time\nfeedback. By utilizing edge computing on mobile devices, real-time feedback could be\nenabled, providing a more natural learning experience.\nFourthly, and perhaps most significantly from a pedagogical standpoint, Profy highlights\nclassifier-relevant regions and displays an abstract acoustic distance, but it does not validate those regions as phonetic errors or provide guidance on how to correct them. The\nlearner is still responsible for interpreting the display and deducing any articulatory adjustment. Future work should critically explore validating the highlighted regions and\nintegrating corrective actions, for example by retrieving relevant native-speaker examples, linking to targeted phonetic exercises, or generating minimal pairs.\n5.5.3 Relation to Existing Language Learning Tools and CAPT Systems\nWhen comparing Profy to established language learning applications like Duolingo or\nBabbel, or other research-based CAPT systems, several distinctions and potential synergies emerge. Many commercial platforms (e.g., Duolingo, Busuu) offer comprehensive, gamified curricula covering various language skills (vocabulary, grammar, listening,\nspeaking). Their pronunciation feedback, if available, often relies on ASR to provide a\ngeneral correctness score for an utterance or identifies very coarse-grained errors at the\nword level, rather than pinpointing specific phonetic deviations within a word. While\neffective for motivation and broad practice, this level of feedback may be insuﬀicient for\nlearners seeking to refine specific aspects of their pronunciation.\nSpecialized CAPT systems often provide more detailed phonetic analysis (e.g., based on\nGoodness of Pronunciation (GOP) scores derived from forced alignment, or analysis of\nspecific acoustic features like formants or pitch contours) [200, 106]. However, these\nsystems frequently require: (a) accurately transcribed speech, (b) detailed annotations\nof target pronunciations, often at the phoneme level, (c) pre-defined linguistic rules for\nerror detection, or (d) a single, reference native speaker model, which may not capture\nthe natural variability in pronunciation.\n75\n\n\fProfy’s approach offers several differentiating factors in this landscape:\n• Data-Driven Proficiency Learning from Largely Unannotated Data: Profy\nlearns the characteristics of proficient speech directly from audio examples of native speakers and contrasts them with learner speech, minimizing the need for extensive manual annotation or explicit rule-setting for every possible mispronunciation. This is particularly advantageous for scalability and adaptation to different\nlanguages or dialects.\n• Localized Visualization of Classifier-Relevant Regions: The attention-based\nwaveform highlighting shows which segments of an utterance contributed most\nto its classification as non-native-like. This is more localized than an overall score,\nbut is not a phonetic error diagnosis.\n• Learning from a Group of Native Speakers: By training on data from multiple\nnative speakers, Profy has the potential to learn a more robust and representative\nmodel of target pronunciation, implicitly accommodating natural variations (although the current “Distance” visualization uses an average native representation\nas a target).\nIt is important to acknowledge that Profy, in its current form, is a specialized tool focused\non model-derived feedback for relatively short, isolated utterances. It does not offer a\ncomplete language learning curriculum, personalized learning paths, or the gamification\nelements found in many commercial applications. A promising avenue for future work\nlies in integrating Profy’s feedback mechanisms into broader language learning platforms\nafter further validation.\n5.5.4 Broader Applications Beyond Language Learning\nThe Profy framework suggests possible future research on other skills acquired by imitating expert performance. For example, related models might compare musical performances or movement data from experts and learners.\nIn sports or professional training, such systems would require domain-specific representations, labels or evaluation criteria, and user studies before claims about corrective feedback or learning effectiveness could be made.\nThe current pronunciation study supplies no evidence for effectiveness in music, sports,\nsurgery, or other professional skills; these are examples of research directions only.\nA transferable design principle is learning a performance classifier from examples rather\nthan encoding every rule manually, while still requiring expert validation of what the\nlearned representation means in each domain.\n\n5.6\n\nConclusion\n\nThis chapter has presented Profy, a language learning support system that addresses the\nchallenge of providing scalable feedback for learner skill assessment in the Imitate stage\nof the AI-Guided Learning framework. Profy uses deep learning-based speech processing to provide model-derived visual feedback on pronunciation; whether this improves\nthe eﬀiciency of skill acquisition requires further evaluation.\nThe key contributions of Profy are threefold. Firstly, it introduces a technical framework\nfor assessing learners’ speech proficiency and visualizing classifier-emphasized regions\nusing unannotated speech data without comparison to a specific native speaker. Particularly important is that it learns a proficiency classifier end-to-end and localizes regions\nthat influence the classifier output.\n76\n\n\fSecondly, Profy presents a language learning support system for non-native English speakers that provides visual feedback on pronunciation. By highlighting classifier-emphasized\nwaveform regions and visualizing distances in latent space, it provides more localized\nmodel-derived information than systems that present only an overall score.\nThirdly, the user study with Japanese learners of English showed a larger observed intelligibility improvement for Profy than for conventional Elicited Imitation (EI). Unlike EI,\nthe pre- and post-practice confidence intervals did not overlap for Profy.\nDespite these promising results, it is important to acknowledge the limitations of the current implementation of Profy. The system’s performance may vary depending on factors\nsuch as the linguistic distance between the learner’s native language and the target language, the diversity of accents represented in the model speech data, and the complexity\nof the practiced sentences.\nLooking ahead, Profy holds considerable promise for applications beyond language learning. The core concept of learning proficiency from data and visualizing model-emphasized\nregions could be adapted to support skill acquisition in fields such as sports, music, and\nprofessional training, subject to domain-specific validation.\nFor instance, future systems could analyze an athlete’s movements or compare musical\nperformances with expert examples. These examples illustrate possible extensions; the\npresent study does not provide evidence that Profy’s framework generalizes beyond pronunciation.\nWithin the broader scope of this thesis, Profy complements the other two systems presented: AIxSpeed, which focuses on optimizing audio playback speed, and FastPerson,\nwhich addresses rapid comprehension of video content. While AIxSpeed and FastPerson\nenhance the eﬀiciency of content consumption, Profy optimizes the process of skill acquisition and feedback. Together, these three systems form a comprehensive approach to\nenhancing time eﬀiciency in learning through multimedia content, collectively contributing to a more effective and personalized learning experience across various stages of the\nlearning process.\nIn conclusion, Profy represents a significant advancement in the field of computer-assisted\nlanguage learning and, more broadly, in the development of eﬀicient skill acquisition\nsupport systems. By enabling learners to receive immediate, visual feedback on their\npronunciation without the need for constant expert supervision, it addresses a critical\nchallenge in language education and opens new possibilities for self-directed learning.\nAs research in this area continues to evolve, future work should focus on expanding the\nsystem’s capabilities to cover more aspects of language learning, such as grammar and\nvocabulary acquisition, and exploring its application in other skill domains. Crucially,\nenhancing the feedback to include guidance on how to correct identified errors, alongside exploring pathways for practical deployment in educational settings and addressing\nassociated pedagogical and logistical considerations, will be vital steps towards realizing\nProfy’s full potential. Furthermore, integrating Profy with other learning technologies,\nsuch as virtual reality or augmented reality, could potentially create even more immersive\nand effective learning experiences.\nThe insights gained from this research have the potential to reshape how we approach\nskill acquisition in educational and professional settings, contributing to more eﬀicient\nand effective learning methods in our increasingly digital and globally connected world.\nIn particular, the approach of learning proficiency in a data-driven manner holds the\npotential to become a new design principle for imitation learning support systems in\nvarious fields.\n\n77\n\n\fChapter 6\nDiscussion\n6.1\n\nChapter Overview\n\nThis chapter synthesizes how the three systems̶AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy̶collectively instantiate the Consume‒Understand‒Imitate (CUI) framework for audio‑visual\nlearning. We first provide a brief recap of CUI and the role of each system, then position the work within established learning theories. We next discuss how the systems\ncomplement one another, examine limitations and open questions, and conclude with a\nvision‑driven trajectory that extends beyond the three prototypes.\n\n6.2\n\nBrief Recap: CUI and the Three Systems\n\nIn this dissertation, CUI denotes a process model tailored to audio‑visual learning: (i)\nConsume̶eﬀicient access to content; (ii) Understand̶deep comprehension of multimodal material; and (iii) Imitate̶practice with feedback for skill acquisition. AIxSpeed\nsupports eﬀicient consumption (and early understanding) by adaptively adjusting playback speed based on acoustic features. FastPerson supports understanding by integrating\nlinguistic and visual cues and providing summaries that preserve the speaker’s voice and\ncontext. Profy supports imitation by analyzing performance against model patterns and\nproviding model-derived, localized feedback for practice.\n\n6.3\n\nPositioning in the Learning Sciences\n\nWe position the work within the learning sciences by relating CUI to established models\nand clarifying its scope for audio‑visual learning.\nRelation to established models. CUI partially aligns with (a) Kolb’s experiential cycle [100] (Consume ↔ concrete experience; Understand ↔ reflection and conceptualization; Imitate ↔ active experimentation), while recognizing Kolb’s cyclic nature (learners\nmay move back and forth) and individual differences; (b) Bloom’s revised taxonomy [6],\nwhich is two-dimensional (knowledge × cognitive process) rather than a strict linear hierarchy, consistent with CUI’s cyclic use; and (c) ICAP [36], which classifies engagement\nby visible learner behaviors (e.g., notetaking, summarizing, self-explanation, interaction).\nOur systems may help shifts from Passive to Active, and in some uses toward Constructive, when such generative behaviors are present. Support for fully Interactive learning\nis limited and remains an avenue for future work.\nThis work reinterprets general learning theories for the characteristics of audio‑visual\nmedia and offers practical system design choices addressing challenges such as temporal\nineﬀiciency, multimodal integration, and feedback scarcity. Whereas prior theory emphasizes describing cognitive processes, this research materializes learning support as\nimplementable systems.\n78\n\n\f6.4\n\nSynthesis: How the Systems Work Together\n\nThe three systems are designed to be complementary along the Consume→Understand→Imitate\nflow. A typical workflow is to summarize a long lecture with FastPerson (with seamless\naccess back to details), use AIxSpeed to increase listening eﬀiciency, and then connect to\npractice with Profy using model-derived feedback. Together they support an end‑to‑end\nlearner workflow; understanding potential synergies and trade‑offs (e.g., cognitive load,\ncontrollability) requires further empirical study.\nFurthermore, this research can be situated with respect to Chi and Wylie’s ICAP framework [36] (Passive, Active, Constructive, Interactive). Because ICAP is defined by visible\nlearner behaviors, AIxSpeed and FastPerson may support Passive→Active shifts when\nlearners engage in generative actions (e.g., notetaking, summarizing, self‑explanation),\nand Profy may prompt Constructive activity via feedback on learner productions. Support\nfor the Interactive level (collaborative knowledge construction) is limited and remains future work.\n\n6.5\n\nRedefining Learning in the Digital Age: Learner Autonomy and the\nAI-Guided Consume‒Understand‒Imitate Framework\n\nThe AI‑Guided Learning framework proposed in this research goes beyond system design considerations to discuss fundamental questions about the nature of learning. The\nlong‑standing idea of widening learner autonomy and access (e.g., “learning webs” [82])\ngains new practical avenues through AI. This section describes how the Consume‒Understand‒Imitate (CUI) cycle and the three systems̶AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy̶\naddress challenges such as the ineﬀiciency of long‑form content, the diﬀiculty of understanding multimodal information, and the lack of feedback in imitation‑based learning,\nthereby supporting learner autonomy and broadening access to expert‑like guidance.\nThe traditional school system has been described as potentially commodifying knowledge\nand transforming learning into an act of consumption, thereby constraining spontaneous\nlearning capacities. In contrast, concepts like “learning webs” proposed networks where\nlearners could access knowledge resources based on their interests, meet peers, and learn\nfrom experts. The AI-Guided Learning and CUI framework in this research can be positioned as a reinterpretation of this vision for the digital age, implemented as a concrete\nset of technological systems aiming to solve challenges specific to audio-visual learning.\nAIxSpeed supports eﬀicient information intake (Consume), FastPerson facilitates deep\nmultimodal understanding (Understand), and Profy aids in the imitation and refinement\nof practical skills (Imitate), thereby strengthening the entire learning cycle.","numLines":700,"startLine":3000,"totalLines":4774}}}
