{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-861269dc87f417c0","type":"tool_result","content":"3700\tThis research aims not only to technically realize this vision but also to open new theoretical horizons. First, AI-Guided Learning introduces “autonomous learning orchestration.”\n3701\tThis refers to AI’s ability to understand learner needs, progress, and context, and dynamically adjust optimal learning paths or information presentation. In this work, orchestration is framed as supportive of self‑regulated learning (SRL): goal setting, monitoring,\n3702\tand evaluation remain learner‑led, with AI assisting rather than replacing metacognitive\n3703\tcontrol. For instance, AIxSpeed adjusts playback speed based on estimated listening\n3704\tdiﬀiculty from acoustic features, attempting to reduce the cognitive load learners would\n3705\totherwise expend on manual speed adjustments. This capability, viewed through the\n3706\tlens of distributed cognition theory and the extended mind thesis, suggests that learning\n3707\tprocesses unfold across the entire human-AI-environment interaction, extending beyond\n3708\tindividual cognition [81, 40].\n3709\tSecond, this research suggests the potential for “predictive learning support.” AI-Guided\n3710\tLearning, beyond responding to explicit learner requests, can predict implicit needs from\n3711\tbehavioral patterns and content characteristics, offering proactive support. The automatic\n3712\t79\n3713\t\n3714\t\fextraction of important segments in FastPerson, or the presentation of differences from\n3715\texpert models in Profy, can serve as support before learners even identify a problem themselves. This extends self-regulated learning theory, enabling AI to assist with parts of the\n3716\tmetacognitive process, thereby allowing learners to focus on higher-order thinking [220].\n3717\tThird, the three systems̶AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy̶each aim to reduce common constraints in audio‑visual learning. AIxSpeed addresses time constraints by adjusting redundant or diﬀicult‑to‑hear segments. FastPerson addresses information overload\n3718\tand comprehension constraints through eﬀicient presentation of multimodal information.\n3719\tProfy addresses constraints of expert access and self‑assessment by providing automated,\n3720\tmodel-derived feedback. These are intended to function not merely as eﬀiciency tools\n3721\tbut as instruments that reduce common constraints on audio‑visual learning, enabling\n3722\tlearners to proactively design their own learning processes.\n3723\tThe potential for these systems to reconstruct power relationships in learning is also noteworthy. In traditional education, teachers and institutions often functioned as gatekeepers of knowledge. AI-Guided Learning aims to alter this structure, supporting learners in\n3724\tbecoming architects of their own learning. This resonates with the principles of critical\n3725\tpedagogy, which repositions learners not as passive recipients of knowledge but as active\n3726\tknowledge constructors [58].\n3727\t\n3728\t6.6\n3729\t\n3730\tThe Synthesis of Human and Machine Intelligence in the CUI Cycle\n3731\t\n3732\tAI‑Guided Learning reframes the human‒AI relationship from a unidirectional teacher‒\n3733\tstudent framework to one of collaborative intelligence. In this paradigm, AI does not unilaterally transmit knowledge but functions as a cognitive partner that augments learners’\n3734\tabilities and helps optimize processes at each stage of the CUI cycle.\n3735\tThis concept can be understood in the context of post-humanist pedagogy, particularly\n3736\tin relation to the notion of “entanglement” [18]. In AI-Guided Learning, humans and\n3737\tAI are not viewed as separate entities interacting, but as mutually influencing beings that\n3738\tjointly constitute the phenomenon of learning. Learning does not occur solely within the\n3739\thuman or the AI but emerges from the dynamic relationship between them.\n3740\tThe three systems developed realize this collaborative intelligence within the CUI cycle\n3741\tin distinct ways. In the Consume and initial Understand stages, AIxSpeed utilizes ASR\n3742\tmodel confidence as an indicator of listening diﬀiculty, technologically supplementing\n3743\tthe limits of human auditory processing. The system does not replace human understanding but optimizes auditory information presentation so learners can process and\n3744\tcomprehend information more eﬀiciently. This can be seen as an application of the Zone\n3745\tof Proximal Development concept for the AI age, where AI functions as digital scaffolding, enabling learners to tackle tasks they might not manage alone [193]. However, its\n3746\tinability to discern semantic importance (e.g., intentionally slow speech by an instructor)\n3747\tbased solely on acoustic features remains a challenge for future work.\n3748\tIn the Understand stage, FastPerson integrates and eﬀiciently presents multimodal information. This is based on the “economy of attention” concept, where attention is a scarce\n3749\tresource in an information-rich world; it uses an LLM to summarize transcribed speech\n3750\tand detected visual information [174]. Furthermore, generating summary videos in the\n3751\toriginal speaker’s voice and enabling seamless switching between summary and full versions aligns with the Cognitive Theory of Multimedia Learning by retaining visual and\n3752\tauditory channels [117]. This video‑to‑video summarization differs from text‑based summarization by preserving the speakerʼs voice and visual context. The current prototype\n3753\tdoes not implement citation-backed generation or a separate factual-verification stage;\n3754\tthese are priorities for mitigating hallucination and fidelity loss in future deployment.\n3755\tIn the Imitate stage, Profy ventures into the realm of “tacit knowledge” using self-supervised\n3756\t\n3757\t80\n3758\t\n3759\t\flearning. Skills like pronunciation are typical examples of tacit knowledge, a state of\n3760\t“knowing more than we can tell” [144]. Profy learns norms of proficiency from large\n3761\tamounts of expert data in a data-driven manner and visualizes discrepancies with learner\n3762\tperformance as waveform regions or distances in a learned two-dimensional space. This\n3763\tis an attempt to partially formalize tacit knowledge and make it learnable. The meaning\n3764\tof this learned distance and its axes requires further validation; the current representation does not map axis directions to specific phonetic features. It nevertheless provides\n3765\tmodel-derived feedback without one-on-one coaching.\n3766\t\n3767\t6.7\n3768\t\n3769\tLimitations and Open Questions\n3770\t\n3771\tWhile each system was evaluated independently, combined workflows have not yet been\n3772\tstudied; potential synergies and trade‑offs in cognitive load and usability remain to be\n3773\texamined. A factorial evaluation (e.g., summary × speed optimization × feedback) could\n3774\tisolate interaction effects on comprehension and transfer. The CUI model covers key\n3775\tphases of audio‑visual learning but does not directly address metacognitive regulation,\n3776\taffective factors, or fully interactive collaboration. Our evaluations primarily capture\n3777\timmediate or short‑term outcomes; long‑term retention, transfer, and behavioral change\n3778\twarrant longitudinal study. Eﬀiciency gains must be balanced with pedagogical pacing\n3779\t(e.g., intentional pauses and repetitions). For Profy, explaining why specific errors occur\n3780\tand how to correct them remains challenging; establishing mappings from the learned\n3781\trepresentations to actionable articulatory guidance is a priority for future work.\n3782\tFinally, beyond the limitations of individual systems, we must recognize that AI-Guided\n3783\tLearning as a whole provides an incomplete answer to the fundamental question of “what\n3784\tis learning”. While eﬀicient information intake, deep understanding, and skill imitation\n3785\tare important aspects of learning, addressing other crucial elements that constitute the\n3786\tcomplete picture of human learning̶such as creativity, critical thinking, and collaborative problem-solving̶remains a future challenge.\n3787\t\n3788\t6.8\n3789\t\n3790\tBroadened Access and Educational Justice\n3791\t\n3792\tOne notable social implication of AI‑Guided Learning is broadened access to expert‑like\n3793\tfeedback and resources. By this, we mean widening equitable access to top‑tier instruction, expert models, and high‑quality feedback regardless of geography, aﬀiliation, or socioeconomic status. High‑quality education and expert guidance have traditionally been\n3794\tlimited to a privileged few. The systems developed in this research, particularly Profy,\n3795\tdemonstrate the potential to technologically alleviate this inequality. At the same time,\n3796\tfairness risks in speech technologies (e.g., accent‑ or race‑related performance gaps in\n3797\tASR and pronunciation scoring) must be addressed. We plan to audit datasets and models (e.g., score validity across groups and environment effects) and to calibrate thresholds\n3798\tto reduce systematic disparities.\n3799\tProfy addresses the challenge of expensive and inaccessible expert coaching by extracting\n3800\tpatterns of proficiency from speech data via self-supervised learning, offering automated\n3801\tfeedback in a widely accessible form. This can be interpreted as an attempt to embody the\n3802\t“difference principle” within educational technology, contributing to educational justice\n3803\tby maximizing benefits for the least advantaged [153].\n3804\tThis democratization also prompts a reconsideration of concepts like standards and correct answers. As AI learns from diverse expert data, it may become possible to recognize\n3805\tand present not a single correct method but various patterns of excellence. This could\n3806\tchallenge the reproduction mechanisms of cultural capital in educational systems that\n3807\ttend to favor those with specific cultural backgrounds [27]. AI-Guided Learning might\n3808\t\n3809\t81\n3810\t\n3811\t\fcontribute to creating environments where learners from diverse backgrounds can pursue\n3812\texcellence in their respective contexts.\n3813\tFurthermore, AI-Guided Learning adds a new dimension to the “politics of recognition” [183]. When AI systems consistently acknowledge learner effort and progress\n3814\twhile making the basis and limitations of their feedback transparent, they may enhance\n3815\tlearner self-eﬀicacy and motivation. The question remains whether AI-based recognition\n3816\tis equivalent to human recognition or can embody true intersubjectivity [77], but it may\n3817\tplay a complementary role alongside human instructors.\n3818\t\n3819\t6.9\n3820\t\n3821\tFuture Trajectories: The Co-evolution of Humans, AI, and Learning\n3822\tEcosystems\n3823\t\n3824\tThe future of AI-Guided Learning should be considered not only in terms of technological advancements but also from the perspective of human-AI co-evolution and the\n3825\ttransformation of surrounding learning ecosystems. The development of generative AI\n3826\tand large language models, in particular, presents new possibilities and challenges.\n3827\tTechnologically, further evolution of multimodal AI is expected to lead to systems that\n3828\tcan more sophisticatedly integrate text, audio, images, and video, dynamically selecting\n3829\toptimal media and linguistic expressions using evidence‑based signals (e.g., prior knowledge, task diﬀiculty, learning history) rather than debated constructs such as learning\n3830\tstyles. AI with extensive context windows will enable more continuous support based on\n3831\tlong‑term learning histories and deep contextual understanding.\n3832\tSystem improvements could include, for Profy, not only error indication but also suggestions for specific correction methods or step-by-step approaches to target pronunciations, plus stronger local explanations (e.g., projecting onto IPA/phonetic features or using SHAP‑style attributions). AIxSpeed might aim for more context-aware speed adjustments by incorporating semantic information or speaker intent, and provide spaced‑repetition\n3833\tprompts or retrieval cues to support long‑term retention. FastPerson could be enhanced\n3834\twith greater interactivity and fidelity safeguards for LLM outputs (e.g., extractive bias,\n3835\tcitation‑backed responses, verification QA).\n3836\tSocially and institutionally, new learning communities and certification systems supporting AI-Guided Learning will be crucial. AI-mediated skill exchange and peer matching could be fostered, and communities of practice might be reconstructed in digital\n3837\tspaces [104]. Furthermore, to accommodate diverse individual learning paths, the development of new learning record and competency authentication systems (e.g., digital\n3838\tcredentials) using technologies like blockchain is anticipated.\n3839\tCulturally, a transformation in the societal perception of “learning” itself is required. In\n3840\ta society where lifelong learning becomes the norm, AI-Guided Learning provides the\n3841\ttechnological foundation, but fostering a culture that accepts and utilizes it is essential.\n3842\tThis co-evolution of humans and AI also carries risks, such as the degeneration of human cognitive abilities, loss of critical thinking towards AI, and attenuation of human\n3843\trelationships. Therefore, in parallel with technological development, the formulation of\n3844\tethical guidelines, revision of educational policies, and formation of social consensus are\n3845\ta priority for future work.\n3846\t\n3847\t6.10\n3848\t\n3849\tComplementarity with LLM-based Systems\n3850\t\n3851\tThe rapid development of Large Language Models (LLMs) and generative AI is fundamentally expanding the possibilities of educational technology. Advanced LLMs like\n3852\tGPT-4 enable answering complex questions, generating personalized explanations, and\n3853\teven creating educational content. Amid such technological advances, it is important to\n3854\t\n3855\t82\n3856\t\n3857\t\fclarify what unique value the three systems proposed in this research̶AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy̶possess.\n3858\tFirst, it is important that these systems focus on enhancement rather than generation.\n3859\tWhile LLMs excel at generating new content, existing high-quality educational content\n3860\t(expert lectures, authoritative materials, practitioner demonstrations) contains value that\n3861\tis diﬀicult to reproduce with generative AI. This includes deep domain expertise, structuring of explanations based on years of teaching experience, and subtle nuances in demonstrations.\n3862\t6.10.1\n3863\t\n3864\tAIxSpeed: Beyond Text-Based Interaction\n3865\t\n3866\tThe value of AIxSpeed lies in its direct action on the auditory characteristics of audio\n3867\tcontent, whereas LLMs primarily rely on text-based interaction. While state‑of‑the‑art\n3868\tmultimodal LLMs can control players and assist navigation, phoneme‑level, signal‑aware\n3869\tspeed optimization is a distinct function; AIxSpeed targets this perceptual stream specifically.\n3870\tMore importantly, AIxSpeed transforms passive consumption into active consumption.\n3871\tAdaptive speed control based on acoustic features maximizes information intake eﬀiciency while minimizing learners’ cognitive load. This is an optimization of the learning\n3872\texperience from continuous audio streams that is not directly addressed by LLM‑based\n3873\tdialogue systems. Prior work on time-compressed multimedia and instructional design\n3874\thas shown that playback rate and cognitive load can materially affect learning eﬀiciency\n3875\tand comprehension [138, 182], and AIxSpeed’s contribution in this domain is diﬀicult\n3876\tto replace with LLMs.\n3877\tAdditionally, many learners engage in concurrent learning (while commuting, exercising,\n3878\tdoing housework), and in this context, optimized audio content is more practical than\n3879\ttext-based LLM interfaces. AIxSpeed has unique value in technically supporting such\n3880\tlearning styles.\n3881\t6.10.2\n3882\t\n3883\tFastPerson: Preserving Multimodal Richness in the Age of Text Generation\n3884\t\n3885\tThe uniqueness of FastPerson lies in the integrated processing and preservation of multimodal information. While current LLMs excel mainly at text generation and image/video\n3886\tgeneration models are developing, it remains diﬀicult to integratively summarize and reconstruct complex multimodal content like lecture videos (speaker explanations, slides,\n3887\tboard writing, gestures, demonstrations).\n3888\tParticularly important is that FastPerson preserves the original speaker’s voice and visual\n3889\tcontext. Mayer et al. [119] found that learners performed better on a transfer test when\n3890\tmultimedia narration used a human rather than a machine-synthesized voice. This finding supports the design choice to preserve the original voice instead of replacing it with\n3891\tgeneric synthesized narration.\n3892\tFurthermore, FastPerson’s interaction design̶seamless switching between summary and\n3893\tdetail̶provides a learning experience that is diﬀicult to achieve with LLM-based systems. Learners can dynamically adjust the granularity of information according to their\n3894\tunderstanding level, which can be considered a dynamic implementation of scaffolding [155]. Even if video generation AI becomes capable of generating educational videos\n3895\tincluding lecture slides and board writing in the future, the value of this interaction design that provides multiple levels of abstraction while maintaining relationships with the\n3896\toriginal content will be maintained.\n3897\tAdditionally, context preservation of educational content is an important issue. In specialized field lectures, educators’ considerations are reflected in terminology usage, concept\n3898\tintroduction order, and example selection. FastPerson seeks to retain more of this context\n3899\t83\n3900\t\n3901\t\fthan a text-only summary by linking each generated summary back to the original video\n3902\tsegments, although preservation of pedagogical intent was not directly evaluated.\n3903\t6.10.3\n3904\t\n3905\tProfy: Tacit Knowledge and Embodied Learning Beyond Language Models\n3906\t\n3907\tThe domain of skill acquisition that Profy targets remains challenging for current LLMs.\n3908\tPhysical skills like pronunciation belong to the realm of tacit knowledge that is diﬀicult to\n3909\tverbalize, and text-based explanations or instructions yield only limited learning effects.\n3910\tWhile LLM‑based dialogue can explain pronunciation rules, it does not directly evaluate\n3911\tlearners’ actual performance or pinpoint specific improvements; robust automatic evaluation remains challenging.\n3912\tProfy’s self-supervised learning approach extracts implicit patterns of proficiency from\n3913\tlarge amounts of expert data. This is learning support at the perceptual-motor level that\n3914\tgoes beyond the linguistic descriptions that LLMs rely on. Recent findings in embodied cognitive science emphasize the importance of perception-action coupling in skill\n3915\tacquisition [133], and Profy realizes AI-based support in this domain.\n3916\tParticularly important is the visual feedback that Profy provides. Highlighting waveform regions emphasized by the classifier and visualizing distances in latent space gives\n3917\tlearners more localized information than an overall score. The current system does not\n3918\tidentify the phonetic cause or prescribe how to correct it, and it was not compared with\n3919\tLLM-generated text feedback. The immediacy and concreteness of visual feedback can\n3920\tplay an important role in motor learning [173].\n3921\tProfy is trained on speech from multiple native speakers rather than a single reference\n3922\tspeaker. However, the present study did not quantify how well the training data cover\n3923\tacceptable pronunciation variation or evaluate fairness across accents and learner groups;\n3924\tthese require explicit bias and coverage audits.\n3925\t6.10.4\n3926\t\n3927\tIntegration and Complementarity with LLM-Based Systems\n3928\t\n3929\tImportantly, these systems do not compete with LLMs but have a complementary relationship. Recent multimodal LLMs (e.g., with real‑time audio I/O) can support conversational navigation of content; our systems remain focused on perceptual‑signal‑level\n3930\toptimization (AIxSpeed), source‑grounded multimodal summarization (FastPerson), and\n3931\tembodied skill feedback (Profy). For example, one could interactively explore content\n3932\teﬀiciently listened to with AIxSpeed through dialogue with LLMs, have LLMs provide\n3933\tadditional context or related knowledge about content summarized by FastPerson, or have\n3934\tLLMs linguistically interpret Profy’s analysis results to generate personalized practice\n3935\tplans.\n3936\tSuch integration points to the potential of AI‑Guided Learning. By combining the domains where each technology excels̶LLMs’ linguistic reasoning and generation, and\n3937\tour systems’ perceptual processing and enhancement of existing content̶more comprehensive and effective learning support can be realized, with uncertainty display and robust\n3938\terror‑handling following human‑AI interaction guidelines [3]. This suggests a future vision of learning support through coordination of specialized AI systems rather than a\n3939\tsingle omnipotent AI.\n3940\t\n3941\t6.11\n3942\t\n3943\tVision and Concluding Synthesis: An AI‑Orchestrated Open Learning Ecosystem Beyond Media Boundaries\n3944\t\n3945\tThis section integrates the dissertationʼs discussion into a single conclusion about the\n3946\tvision of AI‑Guided Learning. Crucially, the scope of AI‑Guided Learning extends far\n3947\tbeyond audio and video. AI is conceived as transforming any medium or environment\n3948\t84\n3949\t\n3950\t\f̶including tools, spaces, events, and everyday practices that are not ordinarily treated\n3951\tas instructional materials̶into learnable substrates. It does so by orchestrating contexts\n3952\tand pathways that account for each learnerʼs interests, goals, current state, and constraints,\n3953\tthereby enabling self‑directed learning. The emphasis is not merely “humans learning\n3954\tfrom AI,”but AI shaping the learning ecosystem so that humans can learn with agency\n3955\tthrough an optimized, adaptive environment.\n3956\tVision. Our long‑term vision is an open learning ecosystem in which AI provides autonomous learning orchestration and predictive support that generalize and continuously\n3957\toptimize the Consume‒Understand‒Imitate (CUI) cycle across diverse media and environments. This ecosystem democratizes access to expert‑level models, guidance, and\n3958\tobjective feedback while preserving learner agency and advancing equity.\n3959\tGeneralizing CUI. We reinterpret Consume as access and sensing, Understand as multimodal integration and contextualization, and Imitate as acting, practicing, and receiving feedback. This generalization applies across knowledge, skills, and dispositions,\n3960\tand reaches beyond audio‑video to physical spaces, devices, social interaction, and data\n3961\tstreams̶enhancing existing resources while connecting them to the learnerʼs personal\n3962\tcontext.\n3963\tDesign commitments. (1) Agency first: learners retain control over goals, monitoring,\n3964\tand evaluation; AI assists as coach and orchestrator. (2) Predictive and adaptive support: anticipate needs from behavioral, content, and environmental signals with transparency and user control. (3) Fidelity and provenance: ground summaries and guidance\n3965\tin sources with uncertainty display to prevent mislearning. (4) Equity and access: audit\n3966\tcross‑group validity, calibrate thresholds, and follow universal design to make democratization substantive. (5) Interoperability and openness: APIs and standards, auditable\n3967\tlearning records, and privacy protection open the ecosystem.\n3968\tFrom prototypes to an ecosystem. AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy address time eﬀiciency, multimodal content access, and model-derived feedback, respectively. The next\n3969\tstep is integration: standardizing the conversion of resources and environments into learnable form while coordinating with general‑purpose reasoning (e.g., LLMs). Rather than\n3970\ta single omnipotent AI, a federation of specialized AIs could compose learner‑centered\n3971\tpathways.\n3972\tToward responsible deployment. We treat ethics, governance, and evaluation̶provenance, fairness audits, learning validity, privacy̶as first‑class design requirements. Through\n3973\tcollaboration among learners, educators, researchers, developers, and policymakers, we\n3974\tcan transition toward a human‑centered ecosystem that democratizes excellence and substantially reduces time‑to‑proficiency without trading off agency.\n3975\tClosing. The three implementations lay foundations for AI‑Guided Learning that turns\n3976\teverything̶across media and environments̶into learnable opportunities. Going forward, by centering orchestration and predictive support, extending CUI to the lived world,\n3977\tand coupling enhancement of existing resources with interoperability and equitable access, we move toward a world in which learners design their own learning with AI‑supported,\n3978\tcontext‑aware pathways.\n3979\t\n3980\t85\n3981\t\n3982\t\fChapter 7\n3983\tConclusion\n3984\t7.1\n3985\t\n3986\tSummary of Research Contributions\n3987\t\n3988\tThis doctoral thesis presents a learning support framework, AI-Guided Learning, together with three implementations (AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy) tailored for audiovisual learning. These offer an integrated approach to fundamental challenges̶eﬀicient\n3989\taccess to long-form content (Consume), understanding of multimodal information (Understand), and acquisition and refinement of practical skills based on observation (Imitate)̶by supporting the Consume-Understand-Imitate (CUI) learning cycle.\n3990\tThe academic contributions of this research are organized along four dimensions: theoretical, technical, empirical, and societal.\n3991\t7.1.1 Theoretical Contributions\n3992\tFirst, it presented the three-stage learning model of Consume-Understand-Imitate (CUI),\n3993\torganizing audio‑visual learning processes in a cross‑cutting way and offering a perspective that positions the potential roles of AI support at each stage.\n3994\tSecond, through the concept of AI-Guided Learning, it situated a view of human‒AI collaboration in education. Rather than a tool or teacher replacement, AI can be viewed as\n3995\ta cognitive partner that supports learners and helps optimize processes. This provides a\n3996\tbasis for connecting distributed cognition and the extended mind with modern AI technology in educational contexts [81, 40].\n3997\tThird, it demonstrated the potential to reinterpret and extend ideals of liberation from\n3998\tinstitutionalized education and autonomous learning, as raised by thinkers like Ivan Illich, for the contemporary era by leveraging AI technology [82]. Concepts such as “autonomous learning orchestration” and “predictive learning support” are attempts to concretize this direction.\n3999\t7.1.2 Technical Contributions\n4000\tTechnically, we designed and implemented three systems corresponding to each stage\n4001\tof the CUI cycle: (1) AIxSpeed uses ASR confidence as a proxy for listening diﬀiculty\n4002\tand implements phoneme‑level speed adaptation; (2) FastPerson integrates audio and visual information to summarize lecture videos, preserves the original speakerʼs voice in\n4003\tshort clips, and allows chapter‑level back‑and‑forth navigation (with consent and clear\n4004\tlabelling when synthetic voice is used); (3) Profy leverages unannotated expert audio\n4005\tand small learner datasets to provide model-derived feedback that highlights classifieremphasized regions and visualizes distance from model speech. Together, these implementations strengthen existing content and represent practical contributions in speech\n4006\tprocessing, multimedia analysis, and educational AI.\n4007\t\n4008\t86\n4009\t\n4010\t\f7.1.3 Empirical Contributions\n4011\tEmpirically, we evaluated each system via user studies. AIxSpeed achieved average playback factors of 1.30x for LibriSpeech and 1.29x for UME-ERJ, with higher mean opinion scores than constant-speed playback at the same average factors. FastPerson reduced\n4012\tviewing time by an average of 53%, with no statistically significant difference in quizmeasured comprehension from conventional playback. Profy showed a larger observed\n4013\timprovement in pronunciation intelligibility than elicited imitation, and its pre- and postpractice confidence intervals did not overlap.\n4014\t7.1.4 Societal Contributions\n4015\tSocietal contributions include: (i) highlighting transparency principles for summarization fidelity and explicit labelling/consent when synthetic voice is used; (ii) emphasizing\n4016\tthe need for bias auditing in pronunciation assessment; and (iii) articulating design considerations that lower temporal and cognitive barriers to high‑quality existing content for\n4017\tdiverse learners. These provide concrete guidance to advance equitable access to learning opportunities.\n4018\t\n4019\t7.2\n4020\t\n4021\tAddressing the Research Questions\n4022\t\n4023\tThis research empirically addressed the Central Research Question (CRQ) and three Specific Research Questions (RQ1-3) presented in the introduction.\n4024\tCRQ: To what extent can adaptive support at each stage of consuming, understanding, and imitating audio-visual content through deep learning technologies achieve\n4025\treductions in learning time and improvements in learning outcomes? This research\n4026\tdemonstrated, through systems tailored to each CUI stage, a 53% average viewing-time\n4027\treduction with FastPerson, adaptive playback at average factors of approximately 1.3x\n4028\twith AIxSpeed, and an observed improvement in pronunciation intelligibility with Profy.\n4029\tRQ1 (AIxSpeed: Balancing Listening Eﬀiciency and Listenability in Audio Content): Phoneme-level adaptive speed control achieved average playback factors of approximately 1.3x and higher mean opinion scores than constant-speed playback at the\n4030\tsame average factors. This supports eﬀicient information consumption (Consume).\n4031\tRQ2 (FastPerson: Eﬀicient Summarization Integrating Visual and Audio Information in Lecture Videos): Video summarization preserving multimodal information reduced viewing time by an average of 53%, with no statistically significant difference\n4032\tin quiz-measured comprehension from conventional playback; 78% of FastPerson users\n4033\trated the ability to switch between summary and detailed content as useful or very useful.\n4034\tThis supports content understanding (Understand).\n4035\tRQ3 (Profy: Model-Derived Localized Feedback in Imitation Learning): The feedback system based on self-supervised learning showed a larger observed improvement\n4036\tin pronunciation intelligibility than elicited imitation, and the Profy condition had nonoverlapping pre- and post-practice confidence intervals. This supports practical skill acquisition (Imitate) through scalable feedback.\n4037\t\n4038\t7.3\n4039\t\n4040\tImplications for Theory and Practice\n4041\t\n4042\tThe theoretical implications of this research extend to learning sciences, cognitive science, and HCI. They include the extension of multimedia learning theory for the AI era,\n4043\tthe presentation of a collaborative cognitive model for humans and AI, and new implementation examples of learner-centered design for adaptive interfaces.\n4044\t\n4045\t87\n4046\t\n4047\t\fPractical implications include concrete examples and design considerations for AI‑leveraged\n4048\tpersonalized learning support, effective AI‑assisted content design (e.g., fidelity in summarization, explicit labelling/consent for synthetic voice), and operational guidance (e.g.,\n4049\tbias audits across diverse learner groups). It also illustrates applications of user‑centered\n4050\tdesign principles [131]; and for policy (e.g., teacher training extending TPACK [124]).\n4051\t\n4052\t7.4\n4053\t\n4054\tLimitations and Future Research Directions\n4055\t\n4056\tLimitations of this research include: (1) limited diversity and scale of experimental participants, and unverified long-term effects; (2) lack of support for collaborative learning\n4057\tor more complex social learning contexts; (3) limited evaluation of long-term knowledge\n4058\tretention and transfer effects (cf. levels of processing theory [44]), where longitudinal\n4059\tdesigns incorporating spaced repetition and retrieval practice would be valuable; (4) insuﬀicient consideration of new possibilities (e.g., more interactive feedback) and challenges (e.g., misinformation risks [21]) arising from the rapid development of generative\n4060\tAI.\n4061\tFuture research should primarily address these limitations. Specifically: (1) Conducting\n4062\tlarge-scale, long-term empirical studies with more diverse learner populations. (2) Extending AI-Guided Learning to collaborative learning environments (e.g., AI-facilitated\n4063\tpeer learning). (3) Developing features for Profy to provide specific corrective guidance, and enhancing semantic understanding and contextual awareness in AIxSpeed and\n4064\tFastPerson. (4) Advancing personalized and interactive learning support through deeper\n4065\tintegration with generative AI, alongside developing risk management methods. (5) Establishing design and operational guidelines based on ethical AI principles, and researching societal acceptance. (6) Expanding Profy’s framework to support the acquisition of\n4066\tdiverse skills beyond pronunciation (e.g., musical instruments, sports, presentations).\n4067\t\n4068\t7.5\n4069\t\n4070\tFuture Vision: The Evolution of AI-Guided Learning Ecosystems\n4071\t\n4072\tWe articulate a future in which AI transforms any medium or environment into a learnable\n4073\tsubstrate and orchestrates individualized pathways that respect each learnerʼs interests,\n4074\tgoals, and state. Extending beyond audio‑visual resources, AI reframes tools, spaces,\n4075\tevents, and everyday practices as contexts for learning while continuously optimizing the\n4076\tConsume‒Understand‒Imitate cycle̶reinterpreted as access and sensing, multimodal\n4077\tintegration and contextualization, and acting, practicing, and receiving feedback. Rather\n4078\tthan a single omnipotent system, this ecosystem is composed by coordinating specialized\n4079\tAIs̶exemplified by AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy̶with general‑purpose reasoning\n4080\tto assemble learner‑centered routes.\n4081\tThe evolution of this ecosystem rests on treating provenance and fidelity, auditable fairness, interoperability, and privacy as first‑class design requirements. Learners traverse\n4082\tfrom summaries to sources with minimal friction, understand the uncertainty of feedback,\n4083\tand modulate the degree of AI intervention to fit their aims and constraints. Educators\n4084\tand developers align on demonstrable outcomes such as reductions in time‑to‑proficiency\n4085\tand improvements in retention and transfer on real‑world tasks, while communities connect through portable learning records enriched with context. In this future, AI is not\n4086\tprimarily a lecturer but an orchestrator of environments, uniting enhancement of existing resources with broadened and equitable access.\n4087\t\n4088\t88\n4089\t\n4090\t\f7.6\n4091\t\n4092\tFinal Reflections\n4093\t\n4094\tThis dissertation has shown, through AIxSpeed, FastPerson, and Profy, that we can advance CUI in implementable ways that enhance existing resources and reconcile time\n4095\teﬀiciency with comprehension and skill acquisition without eroding learner agency. It\n4096\talso reframed AI as a cognitive partner and orchestrator, offering a view in which learning\n4097\temerges across human‒AI‒environment couplings. The vision of AI‑Guided Learning\n4098\tarticulated here moves beyond audio and video to extend CUI to the lived world, building\n4099\tan open ecosystem that renders diverse media and environments learnable.\n4100\tRealizing this vision requires not only technical progress but also embedding principles\n4101\tof ethics, governance, and evaluation into design and advancing deployment through\n4102\tcollaboration among learners, educators, researchers, developers, and policymakers. The\n4103\taim is to democratize access to excellence and materially shorten the time to proficiency\n4104\twithout trading off agency or equity. We hope that the contributions and limitations\n4105\tidentified here, together with the consolidated vision, serve as a starting point for the next\n4106\twave of research and practice toward making learning a more autonomous and flourishing\n4107\tendeavor for everyone.\n4108\t\n4109\t89\n4110\t\n4111\t\fReferences\n4112\t[1] Asma Almusharraf, Hassan Saleh Mahdi, Haifa Al-Nofaie, and Amal Aljasser.\n4113\tThe effect of settings, educational level and tools on computer-assisted pronunciation training: A meta-analysis. Journal of Computer Assisted Learning,\n4114\t40(4):1605–1615, 2024.\n4115\t[2] Daniel Alpert and Donald Lester Bitzer. Advances in computer-based education:\n4116\tThe Plato program will provide a major test of the educational and economic feasibility of this medium. Science, 167(3925):1582–1590, 1970.\n4117\t[3] Saleema Amershi, Dan Weld, Mihaela Vorvoreanu, Adam Fourney, Besmira\n4118\tNushi, Penny Collisson, Jina Suh, Shamsi Iqbal, Paul N. Bennett, Kori Inkpen,\n4119\tJaime Teevan, Ruth Kikin-Gil, and Eric Horvitz. Guidelines for human-AI interaction. In Proceedings of the 2019 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing\n4120\tSystems, pages 1–13, 2019.\n4121\t[4] Moustafa Amrate and Pi-hua Tsai. Computer-assisted pronunciation training: A\n4122\tsystematic review. ReCALL, 37(1):22–42, 2025. Published online 19 September\n4123\t2024.\n4124\t[5] John R. Anderson and Brian J. Reiser. The LISP tutor. BYTE, 10(4):159–175,\n4125\t1985.\n4126\t[6] Lorin W. Anderson and David R. Krathwohl, editors. A Taxonomy for Learning,\n4127\tTeaching, and Assessing: A Revision of Bloom’s Taxonomy of Educational Objectives. Longman, New York, 2001.\n4128\t[7] Evlampios Apostolidis, Eleni Adamantidou, Alexandros I. Metsai, Vasileios\n4129\tMezaris, and Ioannis Patras. Video summarization using deep neural networks:\n4130\tA survey. Proceedings of the IEEE, 109(11):1838–1863, 2021.\n4131\t[8] Rosana Ardila, Megan Branson, Kelly Davis, Michael Kohler, Josh Meyer,\n4132\tMichael Henretty, Reuben Morais, Lindsay Saunders, Francis Tyers, and Gregor\n4133\tWeber. Common voice: A massively-multilingual speech corpus. In Proceedings\n4134\tof the Twelfth Language Resources and Evaluation Conference, pages 4218–4222.\n4135\tEuropean Language Resources Association, 2020.\n4136\t[9] Sercan Ö. Arik, Mike Chrzanowski, Adam Coates, Gregory Diamos, Andrew\n4137\tGibiansky, Yongguo Kang, Xian Li, John Miller, Andrew Ng, Jonathan Raiman,\n4138\tShubho Sengupta, and Mohammad Shoeybi. Deep voice: Real-time neural textto-speech. In Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning,\n4139\tvolume 70, pages 195–204. PMLR, 2017.\n4140\t[10] Anurag Arnab, Mostafa Dehghani, Georg Heigold, Chen Sun, Mario Lučić,\n4141\tand Cordelia Schmid. ViViT: A video vision transformer. In Proceedings of\n4142\tthe IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision, pages 6816–6826,\n4143\t2021.\n4144\t90\n4145\t\n4146\t\f[11] Barry Arons. SpeechSkimmer: A system for interactively skimming recorded\n4147\tspeech. ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction, 4(1):3–38, 1997.\n4148\t[12] Roger Azevedo, Jennifer G. Cromley, and Diane Seibert. Does adaptive scaffolding facilitate students’ ability to regulate their learning with hypermedia? Contemporary Educational Psychology, 29(3):344–370, 2004.\n4149\t[13] Alexei Baevski, Yuhao Zhou, Abdelrahman Mohamed, and Michael Auli.\n4150\twav2vec 2.0: A framework for self-supervised learning of speech representations.\n4151\tIn Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, volume 33, pages 12449–\n4152\t12460. Curran Associates, Inc., 2020.\n4153\t[14] Dzmitry Bahdanau, Kyunghyun Cho, and Yoshua Bengio. Neural machine translation by jointly learning to align and translate. In Proceedings of the International\n4154\tConference on Learning Representations, 2015.\n4155\t[15] Zhongxin Bai and Xiao-Lei Zhang. Speaker recognition based on deep learning:\n4156\tAn overview. Neural Networks, 140:65–99, 2021.\n4157\t[16] Albert Bandura. Social learning theory. Prentice Hall, 1977.\n4158\t[17] Albert Bandura. Self-eﬀicacy: The exercise of control. W. H. Freeman, 1997.\n4159\t[18] Karen Barad. Meeting the universe halfway: Quantum physics and the entanglement of matter and meaning. Duke University Press, 2007.\n4160\t[19] Lochan Basyal and Mihir Sanghvi. Text summarization using large language models: A comparative study of MPT-7b-instruct, Falcon-7b-instruct, and OpenAI\n4161\tChat-GPT models. arXiv, 2023.\n4162\t[20] Noémie Beauchemin, Patrick Charland, Alexander Karran, Jared Boasen, Bella\n4163\tTadson, Sylvain Sénécal, and Pierre-Majorique Léger. Enhancing learning experiences: EEG-based passive BCI system adapts learning speed to cognitive\n4164\tload in real-time, with motivation as catalyst. Frontiers in Human Neuroscience,\n4165\t18:1416683, 2024.\n4166\t[21] Emily M. Bender, Timnit Gebru, Angelina McMillan-Major, and Margaret\n4167\tMitchell. On the dangers of